on, or cleanup execution is authorized until this issue is explicitly approved after plan review.\\n\\nParent ecosystem anchors: `workspace-hub#1962`, `workspace-hub#2397`.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/assetutilities/issues/78\", \"display_title\": \"chore(repo-structure): normalize assetutilities folder/file structure\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2655\", \"title\": \"chore(provider-session): route Codex nested-repo path drift to owning tier-1 repos\", \"issue_number\": 2655, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nPlan a bounded remediation for Codex provider-session `nested_repo_context_drift`: Codex logs are still reading tier-1 sibling/nested repository paths as if they were missing `workspace-hub` files. The next stream should make this routing explicit in provider-session prompts/docs/tests so Codex re-resolves the owning repo root before treating those reads as workspace-hub stale-path debt.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\nLatest provider-session audit (`analysis/provider-session-ecosystem-audit.json`, generated `2026-05-07T09:37:54Z`) reports for Codex:\\n\\n- `missing_repo_reads`: `730`\\n- remediation hint `nested_repo_context_drift`: `61`\\n- top matched examples:\\n  - `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/calibration_schema.py` \\u2014 9\\n  - `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/public_dataset.py` \\u2014 8\\n  - `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/__init__.py` \\u2014 8\\n  - `docs/plans/2026-04-21-issue-334-annual-operator-disclosures-dataset.md` \\u2014 8\\n  - `src/assethold/signals/watchlist.py` \\u2014 7\\n  - `sitemap.xml` \\u2014 7\\n  - `src/worldenergydata/cost/calibration/cost_predictor.py` \\u2014 7\\n  - `tests/unit/cost/test_proxy_comparison.py` \\u2014 7\\n\\nCanonical guidance already exists in `docs/ops/legacy-claude-reference-map.md` under \\u201cSession-local worktree and nested repo path drift\\u201d: examples map `src/worldenergydata/*` \\u2192 `worldenergydata/src/worldenergydata/*`, `tests/unit/cost/*` \\u2192 `worldenergydata/tests/unit/cost/*`, `src/assethold/*` \\u2192 `assethold/src/assethold/*`, and `sitemap.xml` \\u2192 `aceengineer-website/sitemap.xml`.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\nPlanning only until approved:\\n\\n- Add an approval-gated plan artifact under `docs/plans/`.\\n- Define Codex-facing routing/remediation requirements for nested tier-1 repo paths.\\n- Require tests or fixtures proving nested repo path examples are classified/routed without being recreated in `workspace-hub`.\\n- Link this issue to the provider-session audit and prior classification work (#2333) without duplicating already-closed non-repo-artifact classification work.\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n\\n- Recreating any missing `src/worldenergydata/*`, `src/assethold/*`, or website files under `workspace-hub`.\\n- Changing sibling repositories before this plan is reviewed and approved.\\n- LLM-wiki spinout cleanup; that is already covered by #2650 unless a narrower approved sub-plan is later required.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] Canonical issue plan exists in `docs/plans/` and cites audit evidence, related issues, and redirect guidance.\\n- [ ] Plan includes TDD fixtures for Codex nested repo path routing.\\n- [ ] Plan distinguishes actionable workspace-hub stale-path debt from sibling repo routing drift.\\n- [ ] Plan receives review and is moved to `status:plan-review` for user approval.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2655\", \"display_title\": \"chore(provider-session): route Codex nested-repo path drift to owning tier-1 repos\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/326\", \"title\": \"Bug: self.prepare_production_data called but never defined on ProductionAnalyzer (bsee/analysis/production_api10.py)\", \"issue_number\": 326, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nDiscovered during PR #319 (Block B scope-bugs agent) while restoring the missing `df_temp` / `completion_name` loop in `src/worldenergydata/bsee/analysis/production_api10.py`.\\n\\n## The bug\\n\\nIn the `router()` method (or adjacent), `self.prepare_production_data(...)` is called, but the class does not define a `prepare_production_data` method. flake8 does not catch this because F821 does not flag attribute access on `self`/object references \\u2014 only bare identifiers.\\n\\n## Why it wasn't fixed in #319\\n\\n#319's charter was strictly \\\\\\\"fix F821 lint errors\\\\\\\" \\u2014 not broader refactoring. The scope-bugs agent flagged this latent bug in the PR body for future work rather than silently fixing it in the same PR.\\n\\n## Investigation path\\n\\n1. Read the full `ProductionAnalyzer` class in `src/worldenergydata/bsee/analysis/production_api10.py` to understand the calling context.\\n2. Check git history for `prepare_production_data`:\\n   \\\\`\\\\`\\\\`bash\\n   git log -p --all -S \\\\\\\"def prepare_production_data\\\\\\\" src/worldenergydata/bsee/analysis/\\n   \\\\`\\\\`\\\\`\\n3. Check sibling modules \\u2014 the legacy reference `src/worldenergydata/common/legacy/ong_fd_components.py` has a similar pattern and might contain the intended implementation.\\n4. Check whether any test (including the skipped `test_analysis_modules_basic` in `tests/unit/test_achievable_coverage.py:147`) exercises the `router()` method at runtime \\u2014 if no code path reaches this call site, it's dead code and can be deleted. Otherwise, the method must be implemented or the call must be rewritten.\\n\\n## Priority\\n\\nLow-medium. The code path may not be exercised (no tests cover the `router()` method's call to `prepare_production_data` \\u2014 the skip at test_achievable_coverage.py:147 suggests so). But if ever exercised, it will raise `AttributeError`. Worth a proper fix or a delete.\\n\\n## Refs\\n\\nFlagged in #319 PR body. Related: the 10 xfailed bugs in [<umbrella issue URL will be added>] are a separate concern.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/326\", \"display_title\": \"Bug: self.prepare_production_data called but never defined on ProductionAnalyzer (bsee/analysis/production_api10.py)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/555\", \"title\": \"fix(marine_ops): _generate_chain_database diameters[:20] excludes 76mm \\u2014 clears 4 of 77 marine_ops failures\", \"issue_number\": 555, \"body\": \"Sub-issue of [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) cluster R4. 1-line test/fixture bug, ~4 test failures.\\n\\n## Symptom\\n\\n4 failing tests in `tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/legacy/test_component_database.py`:\\n\\n- `test_get_chain_by_specification`\\n- `test_find_chain_with_safety_factor`\\n- `test_database_performance`\\n- `test_weight_scales_with_diameter_squared`\\n\\nTop error:\\n```\\ntests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/legacy/test_component_database.py:589: in test_get_chain_by_specification\\n    chain = database.get_chain(...)\\ntests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/legacy/test_component_database.py:328: in get_chain\\n    raise ValueError(\\nE   ValueError: Chain not found: 76mm R4 Stud Link\\n```\\n\\n## Root cause\\n\\n`tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/legacy/test_component_database.py:236` (verified at HEAD `08e9c33`):\\n\\n```python\\nfor diameter in diameters[:20]:  # Generate 60 components\\n```\\n\\nThe `diameters` array is declared on lines 228\\u2013231:\\n```\\n[16, 19, 22, 24, 28, 32, 34, 36, 38, 40, 44, 46, 48, 50, 52,\\n 56, 58, 60, 62, 64, 66, 68, 70, 76, 78, 81, 84, 87, 90, 92, ...]\\n```\\n\\n76mm sits at index 23, so `diameters[:20]` truncates at 64mm and the four tests querying `76mm R4 Stud Link` cannot find their fixture.\\n\\nA second copy of the same bug exists at `tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/test_component_database.py:236` (non-legacy path) \\u2014 the fix should sweep both unless the divergence is intentional.\\n\\n## Fix shape\\n\\n~1\\u20132 LoC + test-data array re-verification. Change `diameters[:20]` to either:\\n\\n- **(a) The full list**: `for diameter in diameters:` \\u2014 generates all 49 diameters \\u00d7 3 grades = 147 components. The existing assertion on line 219 (`Loaded 60 chain components` print, plus any `len(...) == 60` test assertion) will need its expected count updated.\\n- **(b) A slice that includes 76mm**: e.g., `diameters[:24]` \\u2014 generates 24 \\u00d7 3 = 72 components, again breaking the \\\"60 components\\\" expectation.\\n\\n### Sub-decision (please confirm before implementation)\\n\\nWas the test author's intent:\\n\\n1. **\\\"First 20 standard sizes\\\"** \\u2014 then the array order is wrong (76mm should be among the first 20) and the array needs reordering, slice stays `[:20]`.\\n2. **\\\"All standard sizes up to and including 76mm\\\"** \\u2014 slice extends to `[:24]` and the `60` row-count expectation is replaced with `72`.\\n3. **\\\"All standard sizes\\\"** \\u2014 drop the slice entirely and replace `60` with `147`.\\n\\nReading the comment `# Generate 60 components` and the `Loaded 60 chain components` print on line 219, intent (1) is the most plausible original design but is incompatible with current test queries for 76mm. Intent (2) or (3) appears to be what the test queries actually require.\\n\\n## Acceptance\\n\\n- 4 named tests above pass\\n- No regression in other `test_component_database.py` tests (legacy + non-legacy paths)\\n- The \\\"Loaded N chain components\\\" print and any related `len(chain_db)` assertions are updated consistently\\n\\n## Estimated diff\\n\\n~1\\u20132 LoC in the slice + 1\\u20132 LoC in the count assertion(s). Test-data array re-verification for ordering if intent (1) is chosen.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Triage plan: `docs/plans/2026-05-03-2609-marine-ops-triage.md` (R4, lines 166\\u2013187)\\n- Parent: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609)\\n- Predecessor: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580) (closed)\\n- Bug locations at HEAD `08e9c33`:\\n  - `tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/legacy/test_component_database.py:236`\\n  - `tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/test_component_database.py:236`\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/555\", \"display_title\": \"fix(marine_ops): _generate_chain_database diameters[:20] excludes 76mm \\u2014 clears 4 of 77 marine_ops failures\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/327\", \"title\": \"Test infra: conftest.py blocks pytest collection of tests/unit/marine_safety/\", \"issue_number\": 327, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nDiscovered during PR #324 verification (part of #322 mop-up).\\n\\n## The problem\\n\\nRunning pytest against any file under `tests/unit/marine_safety/` fails at **collection time** with:\\n\\n\\\\`\\\\`\\\\`\\nImportError while loading conftest '/.../tests/unit/marine_safety/conftest.py'.\\n.../tests/unit/marine_safety/conftest.py:16: in <module>\\n    from tests.modules.marine_safety.fixtures.sample_data import (\\nE   ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tests.modules.marine_safety.fixtures'\\n\\\\`\\\\`\\\\`\\n\\nThe conftest imports fixtures from a path that doesn't exist. Effect: **the entire `tests/unit/marine_safety/` directory is uncollectable**, not just one test \\u2014 which means any future PR touching marine_safety tests can't verify via pytest.\\n\\n## How to fix\\n\\nTwo plausible paths, pick whichever matches project intent:\\n\\n1. **Restore the missing fixtures module** at `tests/modules/marine_safety/fixtures/sample_data.py`. Check git history:\\n   \\\\`\\\\`\\\\`bash\\n   git log --all --full-history --oneline -- tests/modules/marine_safety/fixtures/sample_data.py\\n   \\\\`\\\\`\\\\`\\n   If it existed and was removed, restore it.\\n\\n2. **Rewrite the conftest** to use an alternate fixture source (inline fixtures, a simpler path, or factories). Update `conftest.py:16` accordingly.\\n\\nEither fix should be accompanied by a smoke test: `uv run --extra dev pytest tests/unit/marine_safety/ --collect-only` should succeed with non-zero collected items.\\n\\n## Refs\\n\\nSurfaced during #324 but pre-existing (not introduced by that PR). Blocks downstream runtime validation of #324's marine_safety work. Low-urgency since #324 verified via flake8 static analysis, but should be fixed before the next marine_safety-adjacent PR.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/327\", \"display_title\": \"Test infra: conftest.py blocks pytest collection of tests/unit/marine_safety/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/348\", \"title\": \"docs(planning): add canonical docs/plans README index for issue-plan tracking\", \"issue_number\": 348, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nAdd a canonical `docs/plans/README.md` index for worldenergydata issue-plan tracking so planning state can be updated consistently during plan drafting, review, approval, and execution.\\n\\n## Why\\nDuring the latest planning wave for issues #342, #343, and #344, the repo had local plan files under `docs/plans/` but no `docs/plans/README.md` index file. That blocks the standard issue-planning workflow from recording plan status in one discoverable place and makes queue audits harder.\\n\\nThe missing index is now a recurring governance gap because the planning workflow expects:\\n- canonical plan artifact paths under `docs/plans/`\\n- a discoverable README/index of issue plans and statuses\\n- consistent reconciliation between issue labels, local plans, and approval markers\\n\\n## Scope\\n- add `docs/plans/README.md`\\n- define the plan index table format used by this repo\\n- backfill current known issue-plan entries at least for the active annual-disclosure cost wave (#334, #335, #336, #337, #338, #342, #343, #344)\\n- document how statuses should be maintained in the index\\n- keep the work docs-only\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- `docs/plans/README.md` exists\\n- it includes a canonical issue-plan index table\\n- active current plan artifacts are indexed\\n- the maintenance rule for keeping the index in sync is documented\\n\\n## Related\\n- #342\\n- #343\\n- #344\\n- #334\\n- #336\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/348\", \"display_title\": \"docs(planning): add canonical docs/plans README index for issue-plan tracking\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/270\", \"title\": \"Add `lng_terminals_refresh` to scheduler config\", \"issue_number\": 270, \"body\": \"## Problem\\n\\n`src/worldenergydata/scheduler/jobs/lng_terminals_refresh.py` exists as a job file but is not listed in `scheduler_config.yml`. The job is never registered or run.\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\nAdd an `lng_terminals_refresh` entry to `config/scheduler/scheduler_config.yml` with appropriate interval and settings.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nDiscovered during scheduler health audit (#264). Existing curated data in `data/modules/lng_terminals/` (12 files, last updated 2026-02-16).\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/270\", \"display_title\": \"Add `lng_terminals_refresh` to scheduler config\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/15\", \"title\": \"META: resolve standards-routing for maritime-law / lng-projects / acma-projects (post-#2615 deferred Open Questions)\", \"issue_number\": 15, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nThree llm-wikis remain OUT OF the `wiki/standards/<code-id>.md` routing principle scope after umbrella sanction vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2615 landed. They need an explicit user decision before further standards-content work proceeds.\\n\\n## Why\\n\\nUmbrella sanction vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2615 (Tier A+B+C, closed 2026-05-03 at commit `3467058b3`) formally extended the routing principle from {marine-engineering, engineering, naval-architecture} to also include {engineering-standards, asset-management}. Three wikis were carried as **conditional Open Questions** but did NOT receive separate user assent during W5-D approval:\\n\\n- **maritime-law** \\u2014 IMO/ILO conventions are versioned by `consolidated_edition` rather than `revision`; W2-C (#2592, closed) defaulted all 4 IMO/ILO pages to `wiki/concepts/` to avoid presupposing the routing decision\\n- **lng-projects** \\u2014 W5-C (#2612, closed) routed everything to `wiki/concepts/` and `wiki/entities/`; no standards routing\\n- **acma-projects** \\u2014 currently has 1 concept + 1 entity page, untouched by Tier C; W5-D deferred its sanction\\n\\nWithout a decision, future contributions either (a) invent ad-hoc paths or (b) get blocked at the W3-C governance test (`tests/governance/test_2471_citation_scope.py::test_out_of_principle_wiki_routing_requires_sanction_citation`) which is now the fail-closed enforcement.\\n\\n## Scope (decision-only \\u2014 no implementation in this issue)\\n\\nFor each of the 3 wikis, decide ONE of:\\n1. **Sanction** \\u2014 extend the routing principle to this wiki via a new sanction citation (`Sanctioned-by: #<this-issue>` pattern). Adds it to `IN_PRINCIPLE_WIKIS` in `tests/governance/test_2471_citation_scope.py`.\\n2. **Defer indefinitely** \\u2014 keep concepts-only routing, document the rationale in `~/.claude/projects/-mnt-local-analysis-workspace-hub/memory/project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md`\\n3. **Archive/de-scope** \\u2014 formally retire the wiki (acma-projects is the candidate)\\n\\n## Per-wiki context\\n\\n### maritime-law\\n- 17 concepts, 0 standards, 10 entities (post-#2592)\\n- Standards-content already authored (MARPOL, SOLAS, MLC, ISM Code) \\u2014 currently in `concepts/` with `consolidated_edition` frontmatter\\n- Decision Q: should IMO/ILO conventions get their own routing tier?\\n\\n### lng-projects\\n- 8 concepts, 0 standards, 0 entities (post-#2612)\\n- No LNG-specific standards authored yet \\u2014 but CSA-Z276, NFPA 59A, IGC Code likely candidates\\n- Decision Q: route LNG codes to `wiki/standards/`, or keep flat in `concepts/`?\\n\\n### acma-projects\\n- 1 concept, 0 standards, 1 entity \\u2014 barely populated\\n- Per memory `project_aceengineer_copy_canonical_sources.md`: aceengineer-strategy (private repo) is the canonical source for firm copy\\n- Decision Q: is this wiki worth investment, or should it be archived in favor of aceengineer-strategy?\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n\\n- Implementation of the routing changes (separate downstream issue per decision)\\n- Standards-page authoring (waits on routing decision)\\n- Cross-wiki schema changes (#2363, vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#30 territory)\\n\\n## Acceptance\\n\\n- One of (1)/(2)/(3) above explicitly chosen for each of the 3 wikis\\n- Decisions recorded in `project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md`\\n- Per-decision follow-up issue filed for any \\\"sanction\\\" outcome (mirroring the vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2615 4-deliverable pattern)\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2615 (closed) \\u2014 umbrella sanction precedent\\n- vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13 \\u2014 llm-wiki strengthening epic\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2471 \\u2014 original CSA-Z276 routing precedent\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2482 \\u2014 wiki/sources governance (deny-list)\\n- `docs/standards/calc-output-citation.md` \\u2014 routing/citation contract\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/15\", \"display_title\": \"META: resolve standards-routing for maritime-law / lng-projects / acma-projects (post-#2615 deferred Open Questions)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2346\", \"title\": \"feat(gtm): prospect-data customized-demo pipeline \\u2014 48hr turnaround + pre-staged vessel templates\", \"issue_number\": 2346, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nThe 30-day GTM plan Week 4 names \\\"run parametric demo with THEIR data within 48 hours\\\" as the **highest-conversion sales tool**. No issue tracks this pipeline; no automation, input schema, or pre-staged vessel library exists.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n- `docs/gtm/gtm-plan-30day.md` lines 133-138\\n- Risk mitigation: \\\"Always keep a clean demo environment ready; pre-stage common vessel types\\\"\\n- No issue, no `docs/gtm/intake/`, no template\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Define prospect intake schema (vessel + target structure) \\u2014 YAML at `docs/gtm/intake/`\\n- Pre-stage 3 canonical vessel profiles (pipelay barge, heavy-lift, PLSV)\\n- Document SOP: receive \\u2192 validate \\u2192 run demo \\u2192 render branded report \\u2192 deliver in 48 hr\\n- Shared input adapter across the 5 demos\\n\\n## Acceptance\\n- [ ] Intake YAML schema validated with 1 real example\\n- [ ] 3 canonical vessel profiles committed\\n- [ ] SOP runbook at `docs/gtm/prospect-demo-sop.md`\\n- [ ] Dry-run end-to-end <48 hr from dummy intake\\n\\n## Related\\n- #2016, #1800, #1669, 30-day plan Week 4\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2346\", \"display_title\": \"feat(gtm): prospect-data customized-demo pipeline \\u2014 48hr turnaround + pre-staged vessel templates\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2458\", \"title\": \"feat(canonical-spec): promote named OrcaWave multi-body benchmark fixture for roundtrip and handoff readiness\", \"issue_number\": 2458, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nPromote a named OrcaWave multi-body benchmark/spec fixture into a first-class canonical proof path, and prepare it for future OrcaWave -> OrcaFlex handoff validation.\\n\\n## Why\\nMulti-body cases are strategically important because they exercise body naming, parent/child relationships, fixed DOFs, and connection semantics that are easy to mishandle. The current repo has evidence for multi-body pathways, but not yet a clearly promoted benchmark-grade canonical proof artifact.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Select a representative multi-body fixture/spec\\n- Validate canonical `spec.yml` -> native OrcaWave YAML generation\\n- Validate reverse parsing of the native input back into canonical structures\\n- Verify preservation of body count, body identity, fixed DOFs, and connection parent relationships\\n- Leave the fixture ready for downstream OrcaWave -> OrcaFlex handoff testing\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n- Named multi-body benchmark fixture and regression tests\\n- Explicit multi-body preservation checks\\n- Documentation note connecting this fixture to future handoff validation work\\n\\n## Success criteria\\n- Multi-body semantics are preserved in a benchmark-backed canonical pathway\\n- The fixture becomes a stable bridge candidate for future handoff validation waves\\n\\n## Parent roadmap\\n- Reopened roadmap anchor: #1572\\n\\n## Related active issues\\n- Delivered foundations: #1605, #1592, #1768, #1638\\n- Structure readiness source: `docs/roadmaps/orcawave-orcaflex-canonical-spec-contract-roadmap.md`\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2458\", \"display_title\": \"feat(canonical-spec): promote named OrcaWave multi-body benchmark fixture for roundtrip and handoff readiness\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/assetutilities/issues/32\", \"title\": \"tech debt | environment management | poetry library\", \"issue_number\": 32, \"body\": \"Package management and Environments can be manged by using pip, conda, poetry, uv etc. The most latest and most complete package, most used package is pointing towards poetry. uv seems be more command based, simple and robust. \\n\\nWe are not inclined to any method. We will have any scripts with any method in our dev_tools. Utilize the most complete package and get the job done. if uv is even easier, we will pivot now or in future. \\n\\nUtilize poetry for environment management:\\n\\n- [x] gitbash to create environment\\n- [x] gitbash to update environment\\n- [x] Good example for poetry\\n  - [x] https://github.com/virattt/ai-hedge-fund/blob/main/pyproject.toml\\n\\n- [ ] Add basic scripts for uv?\\n\\nReferences:\\n- https://github.com/getsivakumar/20241030-AceEngineer-Consultation\\n- https://realpython.com/dependency-management-python-poetry/\\n- https://codemaker2016.medium.com/introducing-uv-next-gen-python-package-manager-b78ad39c95d7#8902\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/assetutilities/issues/32\", \"display_title\": \"tech debt | environment management | poetry library\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/552\", \"title\": \"fix(aqwa-backend): align CRLF/LF line endings between generate_single and generate_modular\", \"issue_number\": 552, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nSurfaced during workspace-hub [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614) Cat C fix ([digitalmodel#551](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/551), merged \\\\`d6a42770\\\\`).\\n\\n## Defect\\n\\n\\\\`AQWABackend.generate_single()\\\\` writes CRLF line endings via \\\\`newline=''\\\\` semantics; \\\\`AQWABackend.generate_modular()\\\\` uses \\\\`Path.write_text(...)\\\\` which normalizes to LF. The two paths produce byte-different output.\\n\\n## Why this is latent (not currently breaking)\\n\\nThe current test \\\\`test_modular_concatenation_matches_single\\\\` strips empty lines and compares stripped text, so the line-ending difference doesn't break the assertion. But any future byte-equality assertion (or downstream tooling that's CRLF-sensitive) will trip on it.\\n\\n## Proposed fix\\n\\nPick one and align both paths:\\n- (a) Both write CRLF (matches \\\\`generate_single\\\\` current behavior \\u2014 likely matches AQWA Workbench export convention)\\n- (b) Both write LF (matches \\\\`generate_modular\\\\` current behavior \\u2014 POSIX-friendly)\\n\\nConfirm canonical choice by checking what AQWA itself emits as native output.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] Both \\\\`generate_single\\\\` and \\\\`generate_modular\\\\` produce byte-identical output for the same inputs (excluding any explicit per-deck filename/header differences)\\n- [ ] A new test asserts byte-equality (not just stripped-text equality)\\n- [ ] Existing \\\\`test_modular_concatenation_matches_single\\\\` continues to pass\\n\\n## Files likely affected\\n- \\\\`src/digitalmodel/hydrodynamics/diffraction/aqwa_backend.py\\\\`\\n- \\\\`tests/hydrodynamics/diffraction/test_aqwa_backend.py\\\\`\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/552\", \"display_title\": \"fix(aqwa-backend): align CRLF/LF line endings between generate_single and generate_modular\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/18\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): execute marine-engineering P2 platform-entity and overview refresh backfill\", \"issue_number\": 18, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nCreate a bounded planning issue for the P2 marine-engineering llm-wiki backfill items identified by the W4-C gap audit: stability-in-waves concept coverage, canonical offshore platform/vessel entity pages, and stale overview regeneration.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n- `docs/audits/2026-05-03-marine-engineering-wiki-gap-audit.md` Table G identifies these P2 backfill items:\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/stability-in-waves.md`\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/fpso.md`\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/flng.md`\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/semisubmersible.md`\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/spar.md`\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/jack-up.md`\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/tlp.md`\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/jacket.md`\\n  - regenerate stale `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/overview.md`\\n- Live file check on 2026-05-04 showed the P2 concept/entity targets above are missing.\\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/overview.md` currently reports stale post-seed-migration counts (`Source documents: 5`, `Total wiki pages: 20`) while the audit/live inventory records roughly `wiki=19,199`, `sources=19,166`, `concepts=14`, `entities=15`.\\n\\n## Duplicate check\\nFocused live issue searches found related but non-duplicate work:\\n- `#2601` is the closed W4-C audit issue that produced Table G, not the execution issue for these P2 pages.\\n- `#1999` bootstrapped the original marine-engineering wiki, but current overview and entity gaps remain.\\n- `#2612` covered LNG-projects topical expansion, not marine-engineering platform entities.\\n- `#2372`, `#2378`, and `#2392` cover aliasing, index pagination, and gap detection respectively; they do not own these curated P2 pages.\\n\\n## Scope boundary\\nIn scope after planning approval:\\n- Plan and then implement the P2 concept/entity pages listed above.\\n- Refresh `overview.md` so it no longer claims the obsolete 20-page seed state.\\n- Update required wiki index/log/cross-link surfaces for the new curated pages.\\n\\nOut of scope:\\n- P1 standards-directory and station-keeping/motions concept work; that should be tracked separately.\\n- Broad raw data ingestion or copying from `/mnt/ace`.\\n- Full reclassification of all 19k source pages.\\n- No plan approval marker creation; this must stop at normal plan-review until user approval.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria candidate\\n- A plan will name exact files, source anchors, validation checks, and update surfaces before implementation.\\n- P2 curated pages will exist only after plan approval and will include frontmatter, summary prose, source references, and cross-links.\\n- `overview.md` will reflect current corpus scale and point readers to curated concept/entity coverage rather than obsolete seed counts.\\n\\n## Related\\n- Roadmap umbrella: `#2390`\\n- Source audit: `#2601`\\n- Gap detector: `#2392`\\n- Source-title aliasing: `#2372`\\n- Marine index chunking: `#2378`\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/18\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): execute marine-engineering P2 platform-entity and overview refresh backfill\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/16\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): backfill engineering classification-society entity pages (DNV/ABS/LR/BV)\", \"issue_number\": 16, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nCreate a bounded planning issue to backfill first-class `engineering` wiki entity pages for major classification societies surfaced by the engineering wiki gap audit: DNV, ABS, Lloyd's Register (LR), and Bureau Veritas (BV).\\n\\n## Evidence\\n- `docs/audits/2026-05-02-engineering-wiki-gap-audit.md` Table C identifies classification-society entity pages (`DNV`, `ABS`, `LR`, `BV`) as the new gap beyond the Tier B engineering standards issues.\\n- The same audit states that Table C items 1, 2, 3, 6, and 7 already overlap with Tier B issues `#2590`, `#2586`, `#2591`, and `#2595`; this issue intentionally targets only the remaining entity-page gap.\\n- Live file check on 2026-05-04 showed these pages are missing:\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/dnv.md`\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/abs.md`\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/lr.md`\\n  - `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/bv.md`\\n\\n## Duplicate check\\nFocused live issue searches found related but non-duplicate work:\\n- `#2588` is the closed audit issue that surfaced the gap, not the execution issue.\\n- `#2589` covered naval-architecture topical expansion and mentioned IACS/class societies, but it did not create the engineering entity pages above.\\n- `#2264` / `#2263` were ACMA metadata-only standards sweeps, not engineering entity-page backfill.\\n- `#186` tracks IACS Blue Book auto-fetch skill updates, not llm-wiki entity pages.\\n\\n## Scope boundary\\nIn scope after planning approval:\\n- Create concise, source-traceable engineering entity pages for DNV, ABS, LR, and BV.\\n- Cross-link the pages to existing engineering / engineering-standards wiki pages where appropriate.\\n- Update local wiki index/log/cross-link surfaces required by repository conventions.\\n\\nOut of scope:\\n- No standards text copying into git.\\n- No broad raw-data promotion from `/mnt/ace`.\\n- No new standards summaries beyond minimal references needed for the entity pages.\\n- No approval marker creation; this must follow the normal plan-review and user-approval gate.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria candidate\\n- A plan will define exact files, tests/checks, and source-traceability requirements before implementation.\\n- The four entity pages will exist only after plan approval and will have frontmatter, summaries, source references, and cross-links.\\n- Existing Tier B standards issues will remain the owner for standards-summary pages; this issue owns only society/entity pages.\\n\\n## Related\\n- Roadmap umbrella: `#2390`\\n- Source audit: `#2588`\\n- Related topical expansion: `#2589`\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/16\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): backfill engineering classification-society entity pages (DNV/ABS/LR/BV)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/17\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): execute marine-engineering P1 standards/concept backfill from W4-C audit\", \"issue_number\": 17, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nCreate a bounded planning issue for the P1 marine-engineering llm-wiki backfill items identified by the W4-C gap audit: standards-directory bootstrap, motions/RAO concept coverage, and station-keeping concept coverage.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n- `docs/audits/2026-05-03-marine-engineering-wiki-gap-audit.md` Table G identifies these P1 backfill items:\\n  1. Create `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/standards/` and seed `TEMPLATE.md`.\\n  2. Backfill `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/motions-rao.md` anchored to ITTC `7.5-02-07-02`.\\n  3. Backfill station-keeping concept set:\\n     - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/station-keeping.md`\\n     - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/dynamic-positioning.md`\\n     - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/spread-mooring.md`\\n- Live corpus inventory showed `marine-engineering` has `wiki=19,199`, `sources=19,166`, and `stubish=19,148`, so raw/source-page volume substantially exceeds curated concept coverage.\\n- Live file check on 2026-05-04 showed all P1 target files/directories above are missing.\\n\\n## Duplicate check\\nFocused live issue searches found related but non-duplicate work:\\n- `#2601` is the closed W4-C audit issue that produced Table G, not the execution issue for these P1 pages.\\n- `#2613` / `#2615` sanctioned standards routing, but did not create the marine-engineering standards directory or the P1 concept pages.\\n- `#2372` addresses source-title aliasing; `#2378` addresses index chunking/pagination; `#2392` addresses gap detection. None own the curated P1 backfill pages.\\n- Existing standards-summary issues such as `#2586`, `#2590`, `#2595`, `#2599`, and `#2611` target engineering-standards pages, not marine-engineering concept coverage.\\n\\n## Scope boundary\\nIn scope after planning approval:\\n- Plan and then implement the standards-directory bootstrap and P1 concept pages listed above.\\n- Use source summaries / metadata only; preserve raw-data boundary and do not copy raw standards or papers into git.\\n- Update required wiki index/log/cross-link surfaces for the new curated pages.\\n\\nOut of scope:\\n- P2 entity-platform pages and stale overview regeneration; those should be tracked separately to keep this issue approval-bounded.\\n- Broad reclassification of the 19k source-page layer; that remains related to `#1926`, `#2372`, `#2378`, and `#2392`.\\n- No plan approval marker creation; this must stop at normal plan-review until user approval.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria candidate\\n- A plan will name exact files, source anchors, validation checks, and update surfaces before implementation.\\n- P1 curated pages will exist only after plan approval and will include frontmatter, summary prose, source references, and cross-links.\\n- `marine-engineering/CLAUDE.md` standards-directory convention will match the actual directory structure after implementation.\\n\\n## Related\\n- Roadmap umbrella: `#2390`\\n- Source audit: `#2601`\\n- Gap detector: `#2392`\\n- Source-title aliasing: `#2372`\\n- Marine index chunking: `#2378`\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/17\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): execute marine-engineering P1 standards/concept backfill from W4-C audit\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/37\", \"title\": \"feat(knowledge): add llm-wiki strengthening scorecard and prioritized action queue\", \"issue_number\": 37, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nAdd an LLM-wiki strengthening scorecard that ranks wiki domains and source families by expected leverage, so future wiki work is driven by measurable gaps rather than ad hoc intuition.\\n\\n## Why\\nThe repo review surfaced a measurable imbalance and sparse graph glue:\\n- wiki page counts are highly skewed (engineering 83, naval-architecture 45, maritime-law 22 vs marine-engineering 19,184)\\n- `knowledge/wikis/cross-links.md` currently contains only about 25 table rows and there is no `cross-links.jsonl` present\\n- `docs/reports/llm-wiki-external-source-priority-queue.md` already contains a prioritization model, but it is static and not wired into ongoing repo decisions\\n- `scripts/knowledge/wiki_health_cron.py` and the broader lint/health tooling focus on correctness checks, not portfolio-strengthening prioritization\\n\\nA scorecard would let the repo continuously answer: which domain is thinnest, which source family is cheapest to promote next, and where cross-link or provenance improvements will produce the biggest compounding return.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Define a strengthening scorecard for each wiki domain using signals such as page count, source-family coverage, cross-link density, orphan/empty-page counts, and provenance/doc_key readiness.\\n- Ingest existing artifacts rather than re-scanning everything: priority queue, staged batch packs, health/lint outputs, accessibility registry, and wiki indexes.\\n- Emit a ranked queue of recommended next strengthening actions.\\n- Separate portfolio signals from implementation-specific lint failures.\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n- Scorecard schema and report format.\\n- Initial ranking across engineering, marine-engineering, maritime-law, naval-architecture, and personal wikis.\\n- Recommended next-action queue tied to source families or concrete issues.\\n- Guidance for folding the scorecard into weekly/periodic intelligence review without duplicating existing health cron output.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] every wiki domain gets a comparable strengthening score/profile\\n- [ ] scorecard consumes existing repo artifacts rather than bespoke manual counting\\n- [ ] output includes a prioritized next-action queue, not just diagnostics\\n- [ ] cross-link sparsity and source-family readiness are both represented\\n- [ ] scorecard clearly distinguishes structural weakness from simple scale differences\\n\\n## Related\\n- Unified review: `docs/reports/2026-04-16-llm-wiki-resource-intelligence-unified-review.md`\\n- Priority queue: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2242\\n- Staged batch packs: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2243\\n- Cross-link infrastructure: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#36, vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#35, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2068\\n- Wiki health automation precedent: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2002 / `scripts/knowledge/wiki_health_cron.py`\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/37\", \"display_title\": \"feat(knowledge): add llm-wiki strengthening scorecard and prioritized action queue\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/22\", \"title\": \"feat(knowledge): generate faceted portal pages for large LLM-wiki domains\", \"issue_number\": 22, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nGenerate faceted portal pages for large LLM-wiki domains so humans and agents can navigate them through curated entry points instead of only giant raw `index.md` tables.\\n\\n## Why\\nParallel repo review found that large wiki domains are technically linked but still weakly usable:\\n- `docs/document-intelligence/intelligence-accessibility-map.md` explicitly says the marine-engineering wiki has \\\"19K pages with no curated entry beyond the index\\\".\\n- Live repo state shows `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/index.md` is 21,605 lines.\\n- `docs/document-intelligence/README.md` currently points humans at each domain's `wiki/index.md`, but there is no faceted or task-oriented portal for large domains.\\n\\nThis means the wiki exists, but the highest-value domain is still hard to enter efficiently.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Start with the largest domain: `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/`.\\n- Define generated portal pages that facet content by page type, standards, tools/solvers, subdomain, and top-entry pages.\\n- Link the portal from both the domain `wiki/index.md` and `docs/document-intelligence/README.md`.\\n- Keep portal generation reproducible/scripted so it does not become a hand-maintained drift surface.\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n- Faceted portal design for large LLM-wikis.\\n- First generated portal for marine-engineering.\\n- Extend-vs-create guidance for future large domains.\\n- Navigation links from the current docs entry points into the portal.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] marine-engineering gets a generated portal page with curated facets and top-entry links\\n- [ ] portal is linked from the current wiki/domain navigation surfaces\\n- [ ] portal generation is reproducible rather than hand-edited\\n- [ ] portal improves human navigation without replacing canonical index pages\\n\\n## Related\\n- Discoverability/accessibility map: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2096\\n- Canonical intelligence entry points: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2137\\n- Cross-link infrastructure: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#36, vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#35, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2068\\n- Strengthening scorecard: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#37\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/22\", \"display_title\": \"feat(knowledge): generate faceted portal pages for large LLM-wiki domains\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/273\", \"title\": \"WRK-666: feat(workspace-hub): WhatsApp \\u2192 WRK capture pipeline \\u2014 monitor number/channel and auto-create work items\", \"issue_number\": 273, \"body\": \"## WRK-666: feat(workspace-hub): WhatsApp \\u2192 WRK capture pipeline \\u2014 monitor number/channel and auto-create work items\\n\\n\\n\\n**Status:** Stage 17: Reclaim (n) | **Priority:** high | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** pipeline\\n\\n**Repo:** ['workspace-hub'] | **Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** dev-primary\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n### What\\n\\nBuild a lightweight listener that monitors a designated WhatsApp number or group\\nchannel and converts incoming messages into WRK items in the repo ecosystem \\u2014\\nthe same way `/work add <desc>` does from the CLI.\\n\\n### Architecture\\n\\n```\\nWhatsApp (phone/group) \\u2192 OpenClaw Narada agent \\u2192 capture script \\u2192 WRK-NNN.md\\n                                                       \\u2193\\n                                               git commit + push\\n                                                       \\u2193\\n                                           INDEX.md regenerated\\n```\\n\\nThe Narada agent (WRK-665) acts as the listener. When a message arrives on the\\nconfigured number/channel it is passed to a capture handler that:\\n1. Classifies the message as a work item (vs. general chat)\\n2. Extracts title, repo target, and priority hints from the text\\n3. Calls `scripts/work-queue/next-id.sh` to get the next WRK-NNN\\n4. Writes the WRK file, commits, and pushes\\n\\n### Monitored sources\\n\\n- **Self number**: messages sent to own WhatsApp number (voice-note transcription supported)\\n- **Dedicated group**: a \\\"Work Capture\\\" WhatsApp group where any member can drop tasks\\n\\n### Message format (loose \\u2014 Narada normalises)\\n\\n```\\nPlain text:   \\\"Add auth module to aceengineer-website\\\"\\nStructured:   \\\"WRK: Fix login redirect | repo: aceengineer-website | priority: high\\\"\\nVoice note:   transcribed via Whisper or OpenAI audio before routing\\n```\\n\\n### Components\\n\\n**1. OpenClaw skill file** (inside the Narada workspace):\\n```\\n~/.openclaw/workspace-whatsapp-ai-group/skills/wrk-capture.js\\n```\\n- Triggered on every message from the configured source number/group\\n- Calls `classify_message()` \\u2192 returns `{is_wrk: bool, title, repo, priority}`\\n- If `is_wrk`, calls the capture script via `child_process.exec`\\n\\n**2. Capture script** (in workspace-hub):\\n```\\nscripts/work-queue/whatsapp-capture.sh <title> <repo> <priority>\\n```\\n- Wraps `next-id.sh` + WRK file creation + `git add/commit/push`\\n- Writes minimal WRK frontmatter + `## What` from the title\\n- Logs to `.claude/work-queue/logs/whatsapp-capture.log`\\n\\n**3. Acknowledgement** (Narada replies to WhatsApp):\\n```\\n\\\"\\u2713 Captured as WRK-NNN: <title> (repo: <repo>, priority: <priority>)\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**4. Configuration** (`.env` or `openclaw.json` extension):\\n```\\nWRK_CAPTURE_SOURCE_NUMBER=+1XXXXXXXXXX   # self or group JID\\nWRK_CAPTURE_REPO_DEFAULT=workspace-hub\\nWRK_CAPTURE_PRIORITY_DEFAULT=medium\\nWRK_REPO_PATH=/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub\\n```\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\\n\\n- [ ] `scripts/work-queue/whatsapp-capture.sh` creates a valid WRK file and commits\\n- [ ] OpenClaw skill classifies messages and routes to capture script\\n- [ ] Source filter prevents processing messages from unrecognised numbers\\n- [ ] End-to-end: WhatsApp message \\u2192 WRK-NNN.md in repo within 10 seconds\\n- [ ] Narada sends acknowledgement reply with WRK ID\\n- [ ] Rate limit (10/hr) and dedup (24h) guards in place\\n- [ ] Capture log at `.claude/work-queue/logs/whatsapp-capture.log`\\n- [ ] Config documented in `docs/agents/narada-openclaw-setup-guide.md` (WRK-665)\\n- [ ] Legal scan passes (no secrets in committed files)\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\\n\\n- [x] **1. Capture** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **3. Triage** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **13. Verify Gate Evidence** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **14. Future Work Synthesis** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **15. Resource Intelligence Update** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **16. User Review** \\u2014 done\\n- [ ] **17. Reclaim** \\u2014 n\\n- [ ] **18. Close** \\u2014 pending\\n- [ ] **19. Archive** \\u2014 pending\\n\\n</details>\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/273\", \"display_title\": \"WRK-666: feat(workspace-hub): WhatsApp \\u2192 WRK capture pipeline \\u2014 monitor number/channel and auto-create work items\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/285\", \"title\": \"WRK-020: \\\"GIS skill for cross-application geospatial tools \\\\u2014 Blender, QGIS, well\\\\\", \"issue_number\": 285, \"body\": \"## WRK-020: GIS skill for cross-application geospatial tools \\u2014 Blender, QGIS, well plotting\\n\\n\\n\\n**Status:** Stage 17: Reclaim (n) | **Priority:** medium | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** gis\\n\\n**Repo:** ['digitalmodel'] | **Complexity:** complex | **Machine:** dev-secondary\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n### What\\n\\nBuild a GIS skill/module within digitalmodel that provides geospatial information, coordinate handling, and visualization tools for use across multiple applications and use cases:\\n- **Applications**: Blender (3D visualization), QGIS (spatial analysis), Google Earth/Maps, and other GIS-capable tools\\n- **Use cases**: Identifying and plotting well locations, bathymetry/terrain rendering, field boundary mapping, pipeline routes, platform locations\\n- **Core capabilities**: Coordinate transformation (geographic \\u2194 projected \\u2194 local), GIS data import/export, spatial queries, and integration APIs for each target application\\n\\nExample workflow: given well coordinates from BSEE or other regulatory data, plot them interactively in Blender with proper geospatial positioning, or export to QGIS for spatial analysis overlaid on bathymetry.\\n\\n### Why\\n\\nGIS operations are needed across multiple workflows (metocean analysis, field data visualization, digital twin positioning, client presentations). A centralized GIS skill avoids duplicating coordinate handling and spatial logic across separate tools, and provides consistent geospatial capabilities to all downstream applications.\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\\n\\n- [x] Coordinate reference system (CRS) handling: WGS84, UTM zones, local project coordinates\\n- [x] Coordinate transformation utilities between CRS types\\n- [x] Well location plotting capability:\\n- [x] QGIS: layer generation with well markers, attribute tables (`qgis_export.py`)\\n- [x] Blender: 3D positioned well objects with metadata (`blender_export.py`)\\n- [x] GIS data format support: GeoJSON, Shapefile, KML/KMZ, GeoTIFF\\n- [x] Spatial query support: find wells within radius, bounding box, polygon\\n- [ ] Bathymetry/terrain data integration for 3D rendering \\u2014 Blender terrain mesh generation deferred (GeoTIFFHandler + QGIS export done)\\n- [x] Blender GIS integration: Python scripts for importing geospatial data (`blender_export.py`)\\n- [x] QGIS integration: processing scripts for well data workflows (`qgis_export.py`, `.qml` style)\\n- [x] API/interface for other applications to consume GIS data (`FeatureLayer`, `WellLayer`, GeoJSON export)\\n- [x] Document supported CRS, data formats, and application integration steps (.claude/skills/gis/SKILL.md)\\n- [x] `coordinates.py`:\\n- [x] `CoordinatePoint` dataclass: lat, lon, elevation, crs\\n- [x] CRS definitions: WGS84 (EPSG:4326), UTM zones, NAD83, local project coords\\n- [x] Transform between CRS using pyproj or manual formulas\\n- [x] Batch transform for coordinate lists\\n- [x] `io/geojson_handler.py`: Read/write GeoJSON, DataFrame conversion\\n- [x] `io/kml_handler.py`: Read/write KML/KMZ (lxml.etree)\\n- [x] `io/shapefile_handler.py`: Shapefile support via optional geopandas/fiona\\n- [x] `io/geotiff_handler.py`: GeoTIFF reader for bathymetry (optional rasterio)\\n- [x] `core/spatial_query.py`:\\n- [x] Find points within radius (Haversine via `GeoPoint.distance_to`)\\n- [x] Bounding box query (`GeoBoundingBox.contains`)\\n- [x] Polygon containment (ray casting)\\n- [x] Nearest-N search (linear scan, ascending sorted)\\n- [x] `integrations/blender_export.py`:\\n- [x] `BlenderExporter.generate_well_script()` \\u2014 self-contained Blender Python script\\n- [x] Coordinate mapping: WGS84 \\u2192 UTM \\u2192 Blender scene units via configurable scale\\n- [x] Well metadata as Blender custom properties; scene origin anchor at centroid\\n- [x] `BlenderExporter.write_well_script()` \\u2014 write to .py file\\n- [x] `BlenderExporter.geo_to_blender()` \\u2014 static coordinate helper\\n- [x] `integrations/qgis_export.py`:\\n- [x] `QGISExporter.generate_project()` \\u2014 QGIS project XML (.qgs) with embedded memory layer\\n- [x] Field definitions and WKT feature geometries in project file\\n- [x] `QGISExporter.generate_well_qml()` \\u2014 QML style definition for well markers\\n- [x] `QGISExporter.to_geojson_layer()` \\u2014 GeoJSON export via FeatureLayer\\n- [x] `integrations/folium_maps.py` \\u2014 Folium/Leaflet HTML maps (pre-existing)\\n- [x] `integrations/google_earth_export.py` \\u2014 styled KML for Google Earth (pre-existing)\\n- [x] `integrations/plotly_maps.py` \\u2014 Plotly mapbox scatter/dashboard (pre-existing)\\n- [x] `layers/feature_layer.py`:\\n- [x] `FeatureLayer`: pandas-backed tabular GIS collection\\n- [x] `centroid`, `bounding_box`, `filter()`, `from_geojson()`, `to_geojson()`\\n- [x] `layers/well_layer.py`:\\n- [x] `WellLayer(FeatureLayer)`: well-specific subclass\\n- [x] `filter_by_status()`, `filter_by_depth_range()`, `to_well_list()`\\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_crs_and_spatial.py`: CRS, UTM detection, GeoPoint distance, GeoBoundingBox (27 tests)\\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_geojson_roundtrip.py`: GeoJSON write\\u2192read\\u2192compare (8 tests)\\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_spatial_queries.py`: radius, bbox, polygon, nearest-N (14 tests)\\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_feature_layer.py`: FeatureLayer + WellLayer construction, filters (19 tests)\\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_blender_export.py`: ast.parse script validation, coord mapping (13 tests)\\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_qgis_export.py`: XML schema validation for .qgs and .qml (18 tests)\\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_coordinates.py`: CoordinatePoint, transform_points, get_utm_crs (5 tests)\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\\n\\n- [x] **1. Capture** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **3. Triage** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **13. Verify Gate Evidence** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **14. Future Work Synthesis** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **15. Resource Intelligence Update** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **16. User Review** \\u2014 done\\n- [ ] **17. Reclaim** \\u2014 n\\n- [ ] **18. Close** \\u2014 pending\\n- [ ] **19. Archive** \\u2014 pending\\n\\n</details>\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/285\", \"display_title\": \"WRK-020: \\\"GIS skill for cross-application geospatial tools \\\\u2014 Blender, QGIS, well\\\\\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/278\", \"title\": \"WRK-1280: Add 4-quadrant propeller data to propeller-rudder literature\", \"issue_number\": 278, \"body\": \"## WRK-1280: Add 4-quadrant propeller data to propeller-rudder literature\\n\\n\\n\\n**Status:** done | **Priority:** medium | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** hydrodynamics\\n\\n**Repo:** ['digitalmodel'] | **Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** dev-primary\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n### Mission\\n\\nExtend the propeller-rudder literature survey (WRK-1148) with 4-quadrant propeller performance data, enabling crash-stop, crash-astern, and full maneuvering envelope modelling.\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\\n\\n- [x] \\u22654 references covering 4-quadrant propeller data gathered and summarised (11 refs)\\n- [x] Fourier coefficient representation of K_T(\\u03b2), K_Q(\\u03b2) documented (van Lammeren format)\\n- [x] Wageningen B-series 4-quadrant coefficients for \\u22651 representative propeller (e.g. B4-70) tabulated\\n- [x] Applicability ranges and limitations documented\\n- [x] New file `propeller-4-quadrant-data.md` + cross-ref in `propeller-rudder-literature.md`\\n- [x] Future work item FW-1 in WRK-1148 evidence marked as `wrk_ref: WRK-1280`\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/278\", \"display_title\": \"WRK-1280: Add 4-quadrant propeller data to propeller-rudder literature\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/276\", \"title\": \"WRK-1149: Method assessment and selection \\u2014 propeller-rudder interaction models\", \"issue_number\": 276, \"body\": \"## WRK-1149: Method assessment and selection \\u2014 propeller-rudder interaction models\\n\\n**Status:** done | **Priority:** medium | **Category:** engineering/hydrodynamics\\n**Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** ace-linux-1\\n\\n---\\n\\n<details><summary>\\ud83d\\udd28 Implementation Summary</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>\\ud83d\\udccb Plan</summary>\\n\\n\\n## Mission\\n\\nAssess and compare candidate methods for propeller-rudder interaction calculation, select the best fit for a fast-running Python model, and document the decision rationale.\\n\\n## What\\n\\nUsing the literature from WRK-1148, evaluate each method family against:\\n\\n| Criterion | Description |\\n|-----------|-------------|\\n| **Accuracy** | Agreement with experimental / RANS data across J range |\\n| **Input requirements** | What ship/propeller/rudder parameters are needed |\\n| **RPM / J range** | Valid from n=0 (engine-off) through full RPM drive-off |\\n| **Computational cost** | Suitable for fast-running (seconds, not hours) Python module |\\n| **Implementation complexity** | Can be coded without proprietary tools |\\n\\nCandidate methods to compare:\\n- Actuator-disk + Rankine-Froude (simplest, least data)\\n- Holtrop-Mennen semi-empirical corrections\\n- S\\u00f6ding/Brix slipstream-in-rudder approach\\n- Molland & Turnock empirical lift/drag corrections\\n- Hybrid: momentum-based slipstream + empirical rudder coefficients\\n\\n## Why\\n\\nMultiple valid methods exist with different trade-offs. Choosing without explicit comparison risks implementing a method that fails outside its validity range (e.g. at low J during drive-off startup).\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] Comparison table: each method scored/ranked on the five criteria above\\n- [ ] Primary method selected with written rationale\\n- [ ] Fallback method identified for edge cases (engine-off free-wheeling, very low J)\\n- [ ] Known limitations and validation gaps documented\\n- [ ] Output: `docs/hydrodynamics/propeller-rudder-method-selection.md`\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>\\u2705 Acceptance Criteria</summary>\\n\\n- [ ] Comparison table: each method scored/ranked on the five criteria above\\n- [ ] Primary method selected with written rationale\\n- [ ] Fallback method identified for edge cases (engine-off free-wheeling, very low J)\\n- [ ] Known limitations and validation gaps documented\\n- [ ] Output: `docs/hydrodynamics/propeller-rudder-method-selection.md`\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>\\ud83e\\uddea TDD Results</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>\\ud83d\\udcca Evidence and Cost</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/276\", \"display_title\": \"WRK-1149: Method assessment and selection \\u2014 propeller-rudder interaction models\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2398\", \"title\": \"feat(knowledge): assess llm-wiki spinout vs embedded workspace-hub architecture\", \"issue_number\": 2398, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nEvaluate whether the llm-wiki system should remain inside `workspace-hub` or be spun out into a standalone repository (or tightly scoped repo family) that serves the wider repo ecosystem.\\n\\n## Why\\nThe llm-wiki program has grown from a local knowledge experiment into a cross-cutting system with its own ingestion pipeline, provenance contracts, promotion waves, governance rules, index/navigation surfaces, and reuse requirements across multiple repos.\\n\\nAt the current scale, repo-bound coupling may become a bottleneck for:\\n- ownership clarity\\n- CI/runtime isolation\\n- clone size and repo churn\\n- versioning of wiki schemas and promotion contracts\\n- reuse across tier-1 repos and future repos\\n- provider/tool portability for search, retrieval, and promotion workflows\\n\\nThis should be decided explicitly rather than by drift.\\n\\n## Decision to make\\nShould llm-wikis:\\n1. remain inside `workspace-hub`\\n2. move to one standalone `llm-wiki` repo\\n3. split into a core platform repo plus separate domain-content repos\\n\\n## Evaluation Criteria\\n- coupling to workspace-hub scripts and skills\\n- cross-repo consumption patterns and backlink needs\\n- size/churn/performance impact on the main repo\\n- governance of durable vs transient knowledge\\n- promotion-pipeline ownership and release cadence\\n- CI/testing boundaries\\n- portability to other machines and agents\\n- search/index build performance\\n- contribution ergonomics for domain-specific wiki work\\n- migration complexity and link-rot risk\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n1. Current-state architecture summary of llm-wiki dependencies on workspace-hub.\\n2. Option comparison matrix: stay-in-place vs single spinout vs split architecture.\\n3. Recommendation with explicit decision criteria and non-goals.\\n4. Migration plan outline if spinout is recommended, including:\\n   - repo boundaries\\n   - sync/backlink strategy\\n   - release/versioning model\\n   - search/index ownership\\n   - path/link migration plan\\n5. Follow-up implementation issues only if the recommendation is to spin out or partially split.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] current llm-wiki coupling to workspace-hub is mapped concretely\\n- [ ] at least three architecture options are compared with trade-offs\\n- [ ] recommendation is justified with ecosystem-level reasoning, not just local convenience\\n- [ ] migration risks and rollback path are described if spinout is recommended\\n- [ ] result clearly states whether llm-wiki should stay embedded, be split out, or be hybridized\\n\\n## Related\\n- vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13 llm-wiki strengthening roadmap and execution waves\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2034 engineering LLM wiki seed + incremental ingest pipeline\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2206 validate single-source-of-truth pyramid conformance\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2207 standards/codes provenance + reuse contract for llm-wiki promotion\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2209 durable-vs-transient knowledge boundary across wikis, issues, registries, and session artifacts\\n- vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#37 llm-wiki strengthening scorecard and prioritized action queue\\n\\n## Notes\\nThis is a strategy/architecture issue. It should not absorb ongoing llm-wiki content promotion work.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2398\", \"display_title\": \"feat(knowledge): assess llm-wiki spinout vs embedded workspace-hub architecture\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/172\", \"title\": \"WRK-009: Reproduce rev30 lower tertiary BSEE field results for repeatability\", \"issue_number\": 172, \"body\": \"## WRK-009: Reproduce rev30 lower tertiary BSEE field results for repeatability\\n\\n**Status:** archived | **Priority:** high | **Category:** data/bsee\\n**Complexity:** medium\\n\\n---\\n\\n<details><summary>\\ud83d\\udccb Plan</summary>\\n\\n# Reproduce rev30 lower tertiary BSEE field results for repeatability\\n\\n## What\\nReproduce the rev30 lower tertiary results previously provided by Roy using BSEE field data for the same time period. Validate that the analysis pipeline produces identical or equivalent results to confirm repeatability of the methodology.\\n\\n## Why\\nEstablishing repeatability is a prerequisite for trusting updated results. Before rerunning with new data or expanding scope, we need confidence the analysis pipeline faithfully reproduces known results.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [x] Identify the rev30 lower tertiary results provided by Roy (reference data)\\n- [x] Identify the exact time period and data inputs used in rev30\\n- [x] Run the BSEE field analysis pipeline with the same inputs\\n- [x] Compare output against Roy's rev30 results\\n- [x] Document any discrepancies with explanations\\n- [x] Confirm repeatability or flag issues\\n\\n## Results\\n\\n**Phase A (Determinism):** 21/21 tests PASS \\u2014 V30 golden baseline confirmed stable\\n**Phase B (Repeatability):** 12/12 tests PASS \\u2014 all 7 producing developments reproduced within \\u00b10.1%\\n\\n| Development | Delta | Status |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Jack St Malo | +0.03% | PASS |\\n| Stones | 0.00% | PASS |\\n| Julia | -0.00% | PASS |\\n| Big Foot | 0.00% | PASS |\\n| Cascade Chinook | 0.00% | PASS |\\n| Anchor | 0.00% | PASS |\\n| Shenandoah | 0.00% | PASS |\\n\\n</details>\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/172\", \"display_title\": \"WRK-009: Reproduce rev30 lower tertiary BSEE field results for repeatability\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/157\", \"title\": \"WRK-1247: POC \\u2014 Deep extraction on 10 XLSX calculation spreadsheets\", \"issue_number\": 157, \"body\": \"## WRK-1247: POC \\u2014 Deep extraction on 10 XLSX calculation spreadsheets\\n\\n\\n\\n**Status:** Stage 20: Archive (pending) | **Priority:** high | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** data-extraction\\n\\n**Repo:** ['workspace-hub'] | **Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** dev-primary\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n### Mission\\n\\nProve XLSX formula extraction yield on 10 calculation spreadsheets before scaling to 23K XLSX files.\\n\\n### What\\n\\nSelect 10 XLSX files from ace_project + dde_project (prioritizing filenames containing\\n\\\"calc\\\", \\\"design\\\", \\\"analysis\\\") and run the dark intelligence workflow on each:\\nExtract \\u2192 Sanitize \\u2192 Archive \\u2192 Generate TDD tests \\u2192 Implement.\\n\\n### XLSX Extractor Enhancements Required\\n\\nThe current `deep-extract.py` XLSX path uses openpyxl for basic table extraction but\\ndoesn't extract formulas \\u2014 only cell values. This POC requires:\\n\\n1. Read cell formulas (not just computed values) via `openpyxl` `data_only=False`\\n2. Map formula dependencies (input \\u2192 calculation \\u2192 output chains)\\n3. Extract named ranges as variable definitions\\n4. Identify calculation blocks vs data blocks (heuristic: cells with formulas vs static data)\\n\\n### Extraction Outputs Per Spreadsheet\\n\\n- YAML calc-report inputs (named ranges \\u2192 variables, formula chains \\u2192 functions)\\n- Python functions implementing extracted calculation logic\\n- pytest test cases using original cell values as assertions\\n- Validation against existing digitalmodel/assetutilities code where overlap exists\\n\\n### Why\\n\\nThe 23,642 XLSX files are the second-largest extraction target after PDFs. Without\\nformula extraction, we only get cell values \\u2014 losing the calculation methodologies\\nthat are the primary engineering value. This POC identifies parser gaps on a controlled\\nset before committing to full-scale extraction.\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\\n\\n\\n**Tests:** Passed: 373\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\\n\\n**Future work:** see evidence file\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\\n\\n- [x] **1. Capture** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **3. Triage** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **13. Agent Cross-Review** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **14. Verify Gate Evidence** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **15. Future Work Synthesis** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **16. Resource Intelligence Update** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **17. User Review - Implementation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **18. Reclaim** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **19. Close** \\u2014 done\\n- [ ] **20. Archive** \\u2014 pending\\n\\n</details>\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/157\", \"display_title\": \"WRK-1247: POC \\u2014 Deep extraction on 10 XLSX calculation spreadsheets\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/135\", \"title\": \"WRK-1269: POC v2 \\u2014 Pattern-aware XLSX-to-Python on 10 workbooks using xlsx-to-python skill\", \"issue_number\": 135, \"body\": \"## WRK-1269: POC v2 \\u2014 Pattern-aware XLSX-to-Python on 10 workbooks using xlsx-to-python skill\\n\\n\\n\\n**Status:** closed | **Priority:** high | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** data-extraction\\n\\n**Repo:** ['workspace-hub'] | **Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** dev-primary\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n### Mission\\n\\nRe-run the 10-workbook POC using the updated xlsx-to-python skill \\u2014 detect row patterns, collapse to Python loops, generate compact runnable code + HTML calc-reports.\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/135\", \"display_title\": \"WRK-1269: POC v2 \\u2014 Pattern-aware XLSX-to-Python on 10 workbooks using xlsx-to-python skill\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2642\", \"title\": \"feat(naval-arch): B1528 SIROCCO moored-current rudder force component report\", \"issue_number\": 2642, \"body\": \"## Purpose\\nPrepare a professional B1528 SIROCCO/Sorrocco moored-current rudder force-component report for engineer review.\\n\\n## Requested scenario\\n- Vessel condition: moored.\\n- Current passing the rudder: 3.5 kn.\\n- Ship speed over ground: 0 kn.\\n- Rudder angles: 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 deg to port and starboard.\\n- Propeller rotation factor: Cr=1.0, neutral non-rotating/no-rotation-correction multiplier.\\n- Required outputs: all force components at the COG with charts, sample working example, assumptions, limitations, schematics, and traceability links.\\n\\n## Scope boundary\\nThis issue covers rudder-induced moored-current loads only. Hull current force, bank effects, tug loads, mooring-line stiffness, current-profile variation, propeller race, and class/IMO compliance conclusions are out of scope unless source coefficients are later supplied.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- [x] Packaged input YAML records B1528 design data, current case, rudder sweep, Cr basis, conventions, and limitations.\\n- [x] Calculation output reports COG components X/Y/Z/K/M/N for port and starboard rudder angles.\\n- [x] Professional Markdown/HTML report includes design data, methodology, assumptions, schematics, charts, sample working example, references, and GitHub traceability links.\\n- [x] PDF export is generated for ACMA B1528 output review.\\n- [x] Focused regression tests validate symmetry, Cr=1.0 logic, sample data point, and report content.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2642\", \"display_title\": \"feat(naval-arch): B1528 SIROCCO moored-current rudder force component report\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/355\", \"title\": \"docs(cli): align public CLI docs/examples with smoke matrix\", \"issue_number\": 355, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nClean up the public CLI/examples documentation surface based on the #352 smoke matrix: ensure registered sub-apps are documented, stale command docs are retired or renamed, unsafe/download-heavy examples carry warnings, and broken examples are fixed or deleted.\\n\\n## Evidence\\nThe #352 CLI/examples smoke matrix found:\\n\\n- `worldenergydata info` omits registered apps: `dashboard`, `eia`, `ndbc`, `forecast`.\\n- `docs/COMMANDS.md` appears unrelated to the worldenergydata CLI; it documents legacy assetutilities slash commands.\\n- `examples/fdas_complete_workflow.py` imports retired `src.worldenergydata.modules.fdas` paths (#278).\\n- `examples/validation_examples.py` imports a missing `validator_template`.\\n- Three LLM examples download ~1.6 GB on first run without a script-level warning.\\n- Some README commands are data-required or unsafe/unbounded without guardrail language.\\n\\nArtifacts:\\n- `docs/reports/2026-04-26-worldenergydata-cli-example-smoke-matrix.md`\\n- `docs/reports/2026-04-26-worldenergydata-cli-example-smoke-matrix.yaml`\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Update docs/README CLI surfaces so all registered sub-apps are documented or explicitly hidden/internal.\\n- Fix `worldenergydata info` output or its backing table to include all intended registered apps.\\n- Rename/remove/rewrite `docs/COMMANDS.md` if it is not a worldenergydata CLI reference.\\n- Fix or delete broken examples rather than leaving stale import paths.\\n- Add explicit warnings to scripts/examples that perform large downloads or model downloads.\\n- Add a reusable smoke matrix script/check if practical.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- README/docs commands are classified as safe, data-required, credential-required, or unbounded where relevant.\\n- Broken examples either run in a bounded fixture mode or are removed with replacement guidance.\\n- `worldenergydata info` no longer omits intended CLI sub-apps.\\n- The changes are tested with bounded no-download smoke checks.\\n\\n## Related\\n- #352 CLI/examples smoke matrix\\n- #278 compatibility shim issue\\n- #313/#327 test infra risks\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/355\", \"display_title\": \"docs(cli): align public CLI docs/examples with smoke matrix\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/354\", \"title\": \"docs(capability): reconcile module manifest, indexes, catalog, CLI info\", \"issue_number\": 354, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nReconcile `worldenergydata` capability indexes so agents and users can trust one canonical module map instead of conflicting README, `MODULE_INDEX.md`, `module-manifest.yaml`, source tree, data catalog, CLI, and scheduler surfaces.\\n\\n## Evidence\\nThe #349 capability audit found major source-of-truth drift:\\n\\n- `README.md` highlights only 3 modules: `bsee`, `marine-safety`, `fdas`.\\n- `MODULE_INDEX.md` / `module-manifest.yaml` advertise 27 modules.\\n- `data/catalog.yaml` reports 12 modules / 44 datasets / ~10.5 MB.\\n- `src/worldenergydata/` contains ~40 top-level package directories.\\n- `cli/main.py` registers 15 sub-apps; several manifest-stable modules have no CLI surface.\\n- Scheduler flags disagree between manifest/config for some modules.\\n\\nArtifacts:\\n- `docs/reports/2026-04-26-worldenergydata-capability-readiness-matrix.md`\\n- `docs/reports/2026-04-26-worldenergydata-capability-readiness-matrix.yaml`\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Define the authoritative capability manifest source and update stale generated/static indexes accordingly.\\n- For every top-level production package, explicitly classify: canonical module, internal support package, legacy shim, deprecated, or excluded.\\n- Align `MODULE_INDEX.md`, `module-manifest.yaml`, README module list, CLI `info`, and data catalog references.\\n- Do not silently mark modules stable unless source, tests, docs, data, and runtime surfaces justify that status.\\n- Produce follow-up issues for modules that require implementation rather than manifest edits.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- There is a single documented rule for module readiness/status classification.\\n- README, `MODULE_INDEX.md`, manifest, CLI `info`, and catalog references no longer materially contradict each other.\\n- Off-manifest packages such as `cost`, `dashboard`, `decommissioning`, `west_africa`, `baker_hughes`, etc. are explicitly classified.\\n- Empty/sample-only data modules are not presented as production-complete.\\n- The update is backed by tests or snapshot checks where practical.\\n\\n## Related\\n- #349 capability audit\\n- #350 data completeness scorecard\\n- #352 CLI smoke matrix\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/354\", \"display_title\": \"docs(capability): reconcile module manifest, indexes, catalog, CLI info\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/384\", \"title\": \"fix(bsee): lazy-load module-level singletons in bsee_data.py \\u2014 unblocks import and GTM demos\", \"issue_number\": 384, \"body\": \"## Problem\\n\\n`src/worldenergydata/bsee/data/bsee_data.py` instantiates 5 module-level singletons at Python import time:\\n\\n```python\\nproduction = ProductionRouter()   # \\u2190 hangs 30+ seconds\\nblock      = BlockRouter()\\nlease      = LeaseRouter()\\nwell       = WellData()\\ndata_refresh = DataRefresh()\\n```\\n\\nOn any machine where the ~300 MB BSEE binary dataset is absent (CI, clean demo environments, client-facing deployments), importing `worldenergydata.bsee` hangs for 30+ seconds or indefinitely. This blocks:\\n- All GTM demos that touch BSEE data\\n- All CI test jobs that import the BSEE module\\n- Any script that imports `worldenergydata.cli.main` (since it imports all subcommands at startup)\\n\\n**RCA**: `ProductionRouter.__init__` (or one of its transitive dependencies) does filesystem or network I/O when the binary data path is absent rather than deferring until first use.\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\nReplace module-level singletons with lazy factory functions or class-level lazy properties:\\n\\n```python\\n# Before (eager \\u2014 hangs at import)\\nproduction = ProductionRouter()\\n\\n# After (lazy \\u2014 defers until first use)\\ndef get_production() -> ProductionRouter:\\n    return ProductionRouter()\\n```\\n\\nAll callers in `BSEEData.router()` already use the singleton through function calls \\u2014 the refactor is a drop-in replacement with no API change at the `BSEEData` level.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] `import worldenergydata.bsee` completes in <1 second on a machine without the BSEE binary dataset\\n- [ ] All existing BSEE tests continue to pass (regression guard)\\n- [ ] `worldenergydata bsee --help` works in <2 seconds without data\\n- [ ] GTM demo scripts that use FDAS/Arps can import `worldenergydata.cli.main` without hanging\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- GTM candidate review: `worldenergydata/docs/reports/gtm/2026-05-04-worldenergydata-gtm-candidate-review.md`\\n- Confirmed hang location: `src/worldenergydata/bsee/data/bsee_data.py:6-10`\\n- Related workspace-hub issue: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#117\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/384\", \"display_title\": \"fix(bsee): lazy-load module-level singletons in bsee_data.py \\u2014 unblocks import and GTM demos\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2544\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): scout Woodfibre LNG corpus for bounded extraction candidates\", \"issue_number\": 2544, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nPlan a metadata-first scout and curated extraction candidate pass for the Woodfibre LNG project corpus under `acma-projects`.\\n\\n## Source of record\\n- Raw corpus: `/mnt/ace/acma-projects/31522-woodfibre-lng`\\n- Wiki domain: `lng-projects`\\n- Existing metadata page from #2535: bucket-level Elements source/catalog page in `knowledge/wikis/lng-projects/`\\n\\n## Why\\nWoodfibre is the largest Elements-ingested corpus: 5,364 files / ~1.879 TB. It must not be processed with broad text extraction or raw copying. The next overnight-safe step is a structure scout: identify high-value, small candidate sets and draft a bounded extraction plan.\\n\\n## Scope\\nIn scope:\\n- Build a top-level and second-level corpus structure map from paths and file metadata.\\n- Identify high-value extraction candidate families without opening or duplicating large binary/source files unnecessarily.\\n- Propose no more than 15 first-tranche artifacts, with explicit size/type limits and extraction methods.\\n- Draft an approval-ready plan for later bounded extraction.\\n\\nOut of scope:\\n- No broad extraction over 1.879 TB.\\n- No copying raw Woodfibre files into git/wiki.\\n- No modifications to `/mnt/ace/acma-projects/31522-woodfibre-lng`.\\n- No source/staging cleanup.\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n- `docs/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-issue-NNN-elements-woodfibre-scout-plan.md`\\n- `.planning/intel/elements-overnight-wave/woodfibre-corpus-scout.md`\\n- Proposed first-tranche candidate table with paths, sizes, type, rationale, and expected wiki target.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- [ ] Scout summarizes corpus structure without broad raw extraction.\\n- [ ] Candidate tranche is bounded to <=15 artifacts and avoids very large binaries unless explicitly justified for metadata-only handling.\\n- [ ] Plan states raw-data policy and #2534 retention boundary.\\n- [ ] Plan identifies whether additional ACMA/client confidentiality review is needed before extraction.\\n- [ ] Issue is left at plan-review, not self-approved.\\n\\n## Related\\n- Umbrella: #2540\\n- Completed metadata indexing: #2535\\n- Completed first-pass extraction: #2536\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2544\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): scout Woodfibre LNG corpus for bounded extraction candidates\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2627\", \"title\": \"wiki(engineering-standards): create DNV-RP-F103 page (unblocks #2609 R3 fix)\", \"issue_number\": 2627, \"body\": \"## Lead\\n\\nUnblocks R3 cluster (16 tests) of [#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609). Per `.claude/rules/calc-citation-contract.md`, the digitalmodel `cathodic_protection.py:67` `DNV_RP_F103_2010` method will need a citation target wiki page that does not currently exist.\\n\\n## Gap (verified at HEAD 2026-05-02)\\n\\n`ls knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/wiki/standards/` shows: `dnv-os-e301.md`, `dnv-rp-b401.md`, `dnv-rp-c203.md`, `dnv-rp-c205.md`, `dnv-rp-f101.md`, `dnv-rp-f105.md`, `dnv-st-f101.md`. **No `dnv-rp-f103.md`.**\\n\\n## Target path\\n\\n`knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/wiki/standards/dnv-rp-f103.md`\\n\\n## Required frontmatter (per [#2471](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2471) schema; L0-prose enforcement per `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/CLAUDE.md`)\\n\\n- `code_id: dnv-rp-f103`\\n- `publisher: DNV`\\n- `revision: \\\"2010\\\"` \\u2014 primary; tests reference both 2010 (canonical method name `DNV_RP_F103_2010`) and 2016 features (per test docstring `Tests implementation of DNV RP-F103:2016 features`). Recommend `revision: \\\"2010\\\"` with `supersedes:` or a sibling note for 2016 features. To be confirmed against test fixtures during page authoring.\\n- `revision_source: \\\"/mnt/ace/O&G-Standards/DNV/DNV_RP_F103_(2010)_Cathodic_Protection_of_Submarine_Pipelines_by_Galvanic_Anodes.pdf\\\"`\\n- `title`, `tags`, `added`, `last_updated`, `domain: engineering-standards` per the wiki schema CLAUDE.md\\n\\n## Reference PDF\\n\\n`/mnt/ace/O&G-Standards/DNV/DNV_RP_F103_(2010)_Cathodic_Protection_of_Submarine_Pipelines_by_Galvanic_Anodes.pdf` (read-only, vendor-derivative; do NOT copy into git per the metadata-only extraction policy used by the b401 sister page).\\n\\n## Source code that will need a citation surface\\n\\n`digitalmodel/src/digitalmodel/infrastructure/base_solvers/hydrodynamics/cathodic_protection.py:67` \\u2014 method `DNV_RP_F103_2010`. From the source docstring, the wiki page will need to capture:\\n\\n- **Annex 1** linear formula (Eq.2 / Eq.4) \\u2014 coating breakdown factors\\n- **Eq.11** \\u2014 referenced in the calc plan for the R3 cluster (pipeline attenuation analysis)\\n- **Table 5-1** \\u2014 current density requirements (burial x internal fluid temperature)\\n- Distributed anode spacing optimization\\n- Anode mass and quantity requirements\\n- Wet-storage period (R3 fix scope)\\n\\n## Sister wiki for structural template\\n\\n`knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/wiki/standards/dnv-rp-b401.md` \\u2014 bounded summary, metadata-only extraction policy, sources list, cross-references to sibling DNV pages.\\n\\n## Why this matters (citation contract clauses)\\n\\nPer `.claude/rules/calc-citation-contract.md`:\\n\\n- Clause 2: \\\\\\\"Citation target: a wiki page with #2471 frontmatter (`code_id`, `publisher`, `revision`).\\\\\\\"\\n- Clause 4: \\\\\\\"Validation is **fail-closed at calc time** per #2481 D2: a missing wiki page or frontmatter mismatch raises `CitationResolutionError` with the `code_id` in the message.\\\\\\\"\\n- Clause 5: \\\\\\\"Resolver is **direct file read** for v1 per #2481 D3.\\\\\\\"\\n\\nWithout `dnv-rp-f103.md`, any R3 calc-citation-emitting fix will fail-closed. The R3 sub-issue cannot be filed until this lands.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/wiki/standards/dnv-rp-f103.md` will exist at the target path\\n- [ ] Frontmatter will comply with [#2471](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2471) schema (`code_id`, `publisher`, `revision`) plus the wiki-schema fields per `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/CLAUDE.md`\\n- [ ] Page will capture key clauses referenced by digitalmodel: Annex 1 (Eq.2/Eq.4), Eq.11, Table 5-1, plus current density tables, attenuation factor, wet-storage period\\n- [ ] Citation resolver will locate the page via direct file read (per `.claude/rules/calc-citation-contract.md` clause 5, v1)\\n- [ ] R3 sub-issue against digitalmodel will become fileable after this lands\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n\\n- Actually creating the R3 fix sub-issue (separate after this lands)\\n- Modifying `digitalmodel` source\\n- Other [#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) clusters (R1, R2, R4, \\u2026)\\n- Copying clause text from the vendor PDF (metadata-only extraction policy)\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Parent umbrella: [#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609)\\n- Calc citation contract rule: `.claude/rules/calc-citation-contract.md`\\n- Wiki frontmatter schema issue: [#2471](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2471)\\n- Wiki schema CLAUDE.md: `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/CLAUDE.md`\\n- Triage plan: `docs/plans/2026-05-03-2609-marine-ops-triage.md`\\n- Sister page: `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/wiki/standards/dnv-rp-b401.md`\\n- Source code: `digitalmodel/src/digitalmodel/infrastructure/base_solvers/hydrodynamics/cathodic_protection.py:67`\\n- Test file: `digitalmodel/tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/test_cathodic_protection_dnv.py`\\n\\n## Authorization\\n\\nUser explicitly authorized this filing as part of \\\\\\\"approve all 4 proceed on all\\\\\\\" on 2026-05-02. `status:plan-approved` applied at creation per that authorization.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2627\", \"display_title\": \"wiki(engineering-standards): create DNV-RP-F103 page (unblocks #2609 R3 fix)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/570\", \"title\": \"fix(tests/orcaflex): replace bare CLI invocations with sys.executable -m \\u2014 clears ~22 of 42 solvers/orcaflex failures\", \"issue_number\": 570, \"body\": \"Sub-issue of [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) cluster S1, sister to marine_ops sub-issues [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#554](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/554) (R2 catenary), [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#555](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/555) (R4 chain DB), [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#556](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/556)\\u2013[vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#565](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/565) (R6 individuals), [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#566](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/566) (R1+R5+R7+R8 batched). Triage plan: `docs/plans/2026-05-03-2609-solvers-orcaflex-triage.md` (workspace-hub).\\n\\n## Symptom\\n\\nTop error (inferred from bare-command pattern; implementing agent will capture verbatim traceback under `--tb=short` before opening PR):\\n\\n```\\nFileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'orcaflex-universal'\\n```\\n\\n(also `'run-to-sim'` for the run-to-sim subset).\\n\\n## Root cause (live-verified)\\n\\nBare-command `subprocess.run(['orcaflex-universal', ...])` invocations don't propagate the venv's `bin/` onto the subprocess `PATH`. The CLI registrations at `pyproject.toml:193-198` are correct and the binaries execute fine standalone:\\n\\n```toml\\n[project.scripts]\\nrun-to-sim = \\\"digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.run_to_sim_cli:main\\\"\\norcaflex-universal = \\\"digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.universal_cli:main\\\"\\norcaflex-sim = \\\"digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.universal_cli:main\\\"\\norcaflex-convert = \\\"digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.format_converter.cli:main\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe failing tests assume implicit `PATH` inheritance that pytest's subprocess invocation pattern doesn't provide. Setting `PATH=<venv>/bin:$PATH` in the subprocess `env=` argument makes the failing tests pass at the same SHA \\u2014 proven live.\\n\\n## Fix template\\n\\nThe lone passing test in the same file (`TestCLIModuleIntegration::test_python_can_import_after_cli_install`, `tests/solvers/orcaflex/test_orcaflex_cli.py:203-212`) uses:\\n\\n```python\\nresult = subprocess.run(\\n    [sys.executable, '-c', 'import digitalmodel.orcaflex; ...'],\\n    capture_output=True, text=True\\n)\\n```\\n\\nApply the same shape to all bare-command tests, swapping the bare CLI name for `[sys.executable, '-m', '<module>']`:\\n\\n- `'orcaflex-universal'` \\u2192 `[sys.executable, '-m', 'digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.universal_cli']`\\n- `'run-to-sim'` \\u2192 `[sys.executable, '-m', 'digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.run_to_sim_cli']`\\n\\nThe `[project.scripts]` entries map `<name>` \\u2192 `<module>:main`, so `python -m <module>` reaches the same `:main` callable with no CLI-API change. Venv-agnostic; matches the lone passing test's pattern.\\n\\n### Why not just fix `PATH`?\\n\\nSetting `env={'PATH': f'{venv_bin}:{os.environ[\\\\\\\"PATH\\\\\\\"]}'}` on every `subprocess.run` would also work but couples tests to venv layout discovery and breaks under `uv run`. The `sys.executable -m` form is cleaner.\\n\\n## Affected tests\\n\\n22 tests across 9 classes, all in `tests/solvers/orcaflex/test_orcaflex_cli.py`:\\n\\n<details>\\n<summary>Full list (click to expand)</summary>\\n\\n- `TestCLIAvailability::test_orcaflex_universal_command_exists` (L16)\\n- `TestCLIAvailability::test_run_to_sim_command_exists` (L26)\\n- `TestUniversalCLIHelp::test_universal_help` (L39)\\n- `TestUniversalCLIHelp::test_universal_version` (L50)\\n- `TestRunToSimCLIHelp::test_run_to_sim_help` (L65)\\n- `TestRunToSimCLIHelp::test_run_to_sim_version` (L76)\\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_universal_pattern_option` (L90)\\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_universal_output_option` (L99)\\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_universal_parallel_option` (L108)\\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_universal_mock_option` (L117)\\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_run_to_sim_all_option` (L126)\\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_run_to_sim_models_option` (L135)\\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_run_to_sim_threads_option` (L144)\\n- `TestCLIErrorHandling::test_universal_invalid_option` (L157)\\n- `TestCLIErrorHandling::test_run_to_sim_invalid_option` (L167)\\n- `TestCLIExamples::test_universal_help_shows_examples` (L180)\\n- `TestCLIExamples::test_run_to_sim_help_shows_examples` (L190)\\n- `TestCLIOutputFormats::test_universal_verbose_output` (L232)\\n- `TestCLIOutputFormats::test_run_to_sim_verbose_output` (L241)\\n- `TestCLICompatibility::test_cli_runs_on_current_python` (L254) \\u2014 invokes both binaries; rewrite both `subprocess.run` calls in this method\\n- `TestCLIDefaults::test_universal_defaults_shown_in_help` (L275)\\n- `TestCLIDefaults::test_run_to_sim_defaults_shown_in_help` (L285)\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n\\n- `TestCLIModuleIntegration::test_cli_commands_from_module` (L214) \\u2014 already uses `sys.executable` but fails on assertion `'orcaflex-universal' in result.stdout`. Likely a real source bug (`list_cli_commands()` namespace mismatch); a separate issue will be spawned per Decision 3 of the triage plan.\\n- Other [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) clusters: S2/S3/S4/S5, E1/E2.\\n- All marine_ops clusters (R1\\u2013R8) \\u2014 covered by sister issues listed above.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] All 22 named tests pass under `pytest -n auto` (xdist) and serial.\\n- [ ] `TestCLIModuleIntegration::test_python_can_import_after_cli_install` continues to pass (no regression in lone-passing test).\\n- [ ] No regression in non-S1 solvers tests (run full `tests/solvers/orcaflex/` suite, compare pre/post FAILED list).\\n- [ ] One PR.\\n\\n## Estimated diff\\n\\nTest-only changes. ~22 occurrences of `['orcaflex-universal', ...]` / `['run-to-sim', ...]` rewritten to `[sys.executable, '-m', '<module>', ...]` in `tests/solvers/orcaflex/test_orcaflex_cli.py`. ~30\\u201340 LoC delta. No source changes.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Triage plan: `docs/plans/2026-05-03-2609-solvers-orcaflex-triage.md` (workspace-hub, committed at `714640353`)\\n- Parent: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609)\\n- Closed precursor: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580)\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/570\", \"display_title\": \"fix(tests/orcaflex): replace bare CLI invocations with sys.executable -m \\u2014 clears ~22 of 42 solvers/orcaflex failures\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/571\", \"title\": \"fix(tests/orcaflex): test_cli_commands_from_module assertion drift \\u2014 separate from S1 PATH cluster\", \"issue_number\": 571, \"body\": \"Sub-issue of [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) \\u2014 separated from S1 PATH cluster after live verification proved the test already uses \\\\`sys.executable\\\\` and still fails.\\n\\n## Test nodeid\\n\\n\\\\`tests/solvers/orcaflex/test_orcaflex_cli.py::TestCLIModuleIntegration::test_cli_commands_from_module\\\\` (line 214, class \\\\`TestCLIModuleIntegration\\\\` at line 200)\\n\\n## Why this is NOT part of S1 ([vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#570](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/570))\\n\\nThe S1 cluster covers ~22 tests that fail with \\\\`FileNotFoundError\\\\` because their bare-command \\\\`subprocess.run(['orcaflex-universal'])\\\\` invocations can't find the binary on PATH. The fix template is to switch to \\\\`sys.executable -m\\\\`.\\n\\nThis test ALREADY uses \\\\`sys.executable\\\\` (with \\\\`-c\\\\` flag, not \\\\`-m\\\\` \\u2014 but \\\\`sys.executable\\\\` either way) to invoke an in-process Python that imports \\\\`list_cli_commands\\\\` from \\\\`digitalmodel.orcaflex\\\\` and prints the result. So PATH-propagation is NOT the failure mode. The test fails on the assertions \\\\`'orcaflex-universal' in result.stdout\\\\` and \\\\`'run-to-sim' in result.stdout\\\\` \\u2014 meaning either \\\\`list_cli_commands()\\\\` no longer returns those commands, returns them in a different format, or the function was renamed/removed.\\n\\nSource verbatim (lines 214\\u2013227):\\n\\n\\\\`\\\\`\\\\`python\\ndef test_cli_commands_from_module(self):\\n    \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"Test CLI commands list from module\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\n    result = subprocess.run(\\n        [sys.executable, '-c',\\n         'from digitalmodel.orcaflex import list_cli_commands; '\\n         'cmds = list_cli_commands(); '\\n         'print(cmds)'],\\n        capture_output=True,\\n        text=True\\n    )\\n    assert result.returncode == 0\\n    assert 'orcaflex-universal' in result.stdout\\n    assert 'run-to-sim' in result.stdout\\n\\\\`\\\\`\\\\`\\n\\n## Hypothesis\\n\\nReal assertion drift in the CLI module-integration test. Likely root causes:\\n- \\\\`list_cli_commands()\\\\` renamed or removed\\n- \\\\`list_cli_commands()\\\\` returns command objects (not strings) and \\\\`print(cmds)\\\\` no longer surfaces the names\\n- Console-script names changed (but \\\\`pyproject.toml:193-198\\\\` still has \\\\`run-to-sim\\\\` and \\\\`orcaflex-universal\\\\` registered, so this is unlikely the root)\\n\\nInvestigation will start by running the inner Python invocation manually to capture what \\\\`list_cli_commands()\\\\` actually returns at HEAD.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] \\\\`test_cli_commands_from_module\\\\` passes\\n- [ ] No regression in other \\\\`TestCLIModuleIntegration\\\\` tests\\n- [ ] Root cause documented in PR (renamed function vs. format change vs. real registry drift)\\n\\n## Estimated diff\\n\\n\\u226410 LoC test or source change once root cause is confirmed.\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n\\n- The 22 S1 PATH-cluster tests \\u2014 sister sub-issue [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#570](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/570)\\n- Other [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) clusters\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Triage plan: \\\\`docs/plans/2026-05-03-2609-solvers-orcaflex-triage.md\\\\` (workspace-hub)\\n- Parent umbrella: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609)\\n- Sister S1 cluster: [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#570](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/570)\\n- Closed parent: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580)\\n\\nUser authorized this filing in session 2026-05-03 (\\\"approve all 4 proceed on all\\\").\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/571\", \"display_title\": \"fix(tests/orcaflex): test_cli_commands_from_module assertion drift \\u2014 separate from S1 PATH cluster\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2479\", \"title\": \"fix(review): Codex stdin-hang regression post-#2406 closure (size-dependent)\", \"issue_number\": 2479, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nThe 2026-04-23 cross-review batch saw **8/8 Codex results UNAVAILABLE** on T2+ plans. Two defects, one wrapper, one upstream:\\n\\n1. **Wrapper bug (fixed in [`fix/codex-stdin-hang`](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/tree/fix/codex-stdin-hang)):** `scripts/review/plan-review-fanout.sh:82` passed `--no-interactive`, removed from codex-cli 0.124.0 (released 2026-04-23). The flag now returns `error: unexpected argument '--no-interactive' found` / rc=2 on every invocation through that wrapper path.\\n\\n2. **Upstream regression (NOT patchable here):** `codex exec` in codex-cli 0.124.0 prints `Reading additional input from stdin...` and blocks indefinitely **even when stdin is redirected to `/dev/null` or the FD is closed**. Every invocation through `scripts/review/submit-to-codex.sh` now trips timeout=300s and exits 124.\\n\\nThe `#2406` fix (`</dev/null` redirect) worked on codex-cli 0.121.0 but is **demonstrably reverted / regressed by the 2026-04-23 upgrade to 0.124.0**. That closure was premature against the broader \\\"substantial plan\\\" failure class.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n### Installed version vs. last-known-working\\n```\\n$ codex --version\\ncodex-cli 0.124.0\\n\\n$ stat -c '%y' /home/vamsee/.npm-global/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex/\\n2026-04-23 15:06:20  # upgrade day == first-failure day\\n```\\n\\n### Live repro of the upstream stdin-hang\\n```\\n$ time timeout 90 codex exec \\\"just say hello\\\" --skip-git-repo-check </dev/null\\nReading additional input from stdin...\\nreal    1m30.033s\\nexit=124\\n```\\nClosing FD 0 (`exec 0<&-`), wrapping in `setsid -w`, and short/trivial prompts all reproduce the same 90-second hang \\u2192 exit 124. Not size-dependent \\u2014 codex 0.124.0 hangs on ANY `exec` invocation where stdin is a non-TTY file/pipe.\\n\\n### Plan-review-fanout flag rejection\\n```\\n$ codex exec --no-interactive \\\"test\\\" --skip-git-repo-check </dev/null\\nerror: unexpected argument '--no-interactive' found\\n  tip: to pass '--no-interactive' as a value, use '-- --no-interactive'\\nUsage: codex exec [OPTIONS] [PROMPT]\\n```\\n\\n### 2026-04-23 batch failure manifest\\n- `scripts/review/results/2026-04-23-plan-2363-codex.md` \\u2192 `# Codex review failed (exit 124)`\\n- `scripts/review/results/2026-04-23-plan-2368-codex.md` \\u2192 same\\n- Retry logs: `/tmp/codex-{2476,2227,2216,2105,2380,2373,2364,2369,2368,2392,2363}-*.out` \\u2014 every one ends with `STDERR: Reading additional input from stdin...`\\n- `/tmp/review-2380.log` \\u2192 `WARNING: Codex review FAILED (exit 124) \\u2014 this is a HARD GATE`\\n\\n### T1 vs T2+ distinction (from the original brief)\\nNot actually size-dependent in the wrapper \\u2014 the hang reproduces on a 90-byte test plan through `submit-to-codex.sh`. The T1 successes in the 2026-04-22 set predate the 0.124.0 upgrade (`scripts/review/results/2026-04-22-plan-2443-codex-r5.md` is a valid MAJOR review from before 2026-04-23 15:06).\\n\\n## Root cause\\n\\n- **Wrapper half:** stale `--no-interactive` flag in `plan-review-fanout.sh:82`. codex-cli dropped the flag (no deprecation window); `codex exec` is non-interactive by default.\\n- **Upstream half:** codex-cli 0.124.0 `exec` subcommand stdin detection regressed \\u2014 it prints `Reading additional input from stdin...` and blocks on FD 0 reads even when stdin is `/dev/null`. No `--stdin=never` / `--no-stdin` flag exists in `codex exec --help` as of 0.124.0. Downgrade to 0.123.0 (or pinning to 0.121.0, the #2406 baseline) is the only known-good workaround until upstream ships a fix.\\n\\n## Fix status\\n\\n- Branch: **[`fix/codex-stdin-hang`](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/tree/fix/codex-stdin-hang)** (commit `257b47dd9`)\\n  - Removes `--no-interactive` from `plan-review-fanout.sh:82`\\n  - Adds `</dev/null` stdin redirect (consistency with `submit-to-codex.sh`)\\n  - Adds regression guard in `test_plan_review_fanout.sh` \\u2014 test 5 fails if the removed flag resurfaces\\n  - All 12 fanout tests pass\\n\\n- **NOT fixed in this branch (upstream):** the `Reading additional input from stdin...` hang in `codex exec` itself. No wrapper-level workaround exists. Requires either:\\n  - Pin/downgrade `@openai/codex` to 0.123.0 or earlier (user decision \\u2014 not executed unilaterally)\\n  - Upstream patch to `codex exec` stdin-detection logic\\n\\n## Note on #2406\\n\\n#2406 was closed 2026-04-20 as fixed against codex-cli 0.121.0; the `</dev/null` fix it landed is still correct for that version but was **silently defeated** by the 0.124.0 upgrade on 2026-04-23. Per `feedback_gh_issue_close_silent_comment_drop`, I am not commenting on #2406 \\u2014 this fresh issue supersedes it. Leave #2406 closed-as-superseded unless maintainers prefer to reopen.\\n\\n## Related\\n- `.claude/memory/topics/feedback_codex_needs_pushed_artifact.md`\\n- `.claude/memory/topics/feedback_codex_sandbox_write_blocked.md`\\n- `.claude/memory/topics/feedback_codex_sandbox_no_execution.md`\\n- `.claude/memory/topics/feedback_codex_sandbox_fallback_paths.md`\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2479\", \"display_title\": \"fix(review): Codex stdin-hang regression post-#2406 closure (size-dependent)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2272\", \"title\": \"test(portability): add repeatable OpenFOAM and Blender smoke verification\", \"issue_number\": 2272, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nAdd repeatable portability verification for core engineering workflows so the ecosystem can continuously check whether the OpenFOAM and Blender baselines are still reproducible.\\n\\nPart of #1782.\\nDepends on the baseline workflow issues for OpenFOAM and Blender.\\n\\n## Motivation\\nPortability should be measurable. Once baseline workflows exist, the ecosystem needs a repeatable verification layer that surfaces drift early.\\n\\n## Scope\\nAdd smoke verification commands and lightweight health checks for the canonical OpenFOAM and Blender baseline workflows.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] one OpenFOAM smoke verification path exists and is documented\\n- [ ] one Blender smoke verification path exists and is documented\\n- [ ] verification output is machine-readable and human-readable\\n- [ ] docs explain expected outputs, pass/fail semantics, and common failure categories\\n- [ ] verification can be re-run after environment/tooling changes to detect drift\\n\\n## Notes\\nPrefer layered validation:\\n- structure first\\n- semantic sanity second\\n- deeper engineering quality checks later\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2272\", \"display_title\": \"test(portability): add repeatable OpenFOAM and Blender smoke verification\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2270\", \"title\": \"feat(blender): standardize headless baseline workflow and smoke render validation\", \"issue_number\": 2270, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nStandardize the canonical Blender baseline for portable engineering work, using headless bpy scripts, example scenes, output conventions, and validation.\\n\\nPart of #1782.\\nPrimary reference: #26.\\nTooling history/reference: #1475.\\n\\n## Motivation\\nBlender work is useful for engineering visualization and animation, but it becomes non-portable when knowledge lives only in manually edited `.blend` files or machine-specific GUI habits. We need a script-first headless baseline.\\n\\n## Scope\\nCreate the baseline Blender portability package in workspace-hub.\\n\\nExpected deliverables:\\n- baseline docs\\n- headless render/script entrypoints\\n- minimal reproducible scene/render example\\n- troubleshooting guidance\\n- shared skill update if reusable guidance is improved\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] baseline doc declares headless `blender -b --python ...` as the canonical path\\n- [ ] minimal reproducible scene/render workflow exists at a repo-tracked path\\n- [ ] output naming and artifact conventions are documented\\n- [ ] validator checks execution success and expected outputs\\n- [ ] common failure modes are documented\\n- [ ] optional CLI-Anything tooling is documented as convenience-only, not required baseline\\n\\n## Notes\\nImplementation should prefer:\\n- procedural scene generation where practical\\n- validation of output structure/metadata first, not pixel-perfect diffs\\n- explicit output directories and deterministic command paths\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2270\", \"display_title\": \"feat(blender): standardize headless baseline workflow and smoke render validation\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2532\", \"title\": \"fix(ci): repair PR review/stage-prompt guard environment failures\", \"issue_number\": 2532, \"body\": \"## Problem\\nTwo independent workspace-hub PRs created during post-reboot salvage (#2530 for vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2408 and vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2531 for vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2424) both hit the same CI infrastructure failures unrelated to their branch diffs:\\n\\n1. `Stage Prompt Drift Guard` fails with:\\n   - `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'workspace_hub'`\\n   - path: `scripts/analysis/stage_prompt_drift_check.py` imports `claude_session_ecosystem_audit`, which imports `workspace_hub.workstations.resolver`\\n2. `Review Evidence Check` fails before checking review evidence with:\\n   - `scripts/enforcement/require-review-on-push.sh: line 19: uv: command not found`\\n\\n## Evidence\\n- PR vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2530: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/pull/2530\\n  - Stage Prompt Drift Guard job: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/actions/runs/25024304678/job/73291841751\\n  - Review Evidence Check job: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/actions/runs/25024304678/job/73291841710\\n- PR vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2531: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/pull/2531\\n  - Stage Prompt Drift Guard job: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/actions/runs/25024305694/job/73291844714\\n  - Review Evidence Check job: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/actions/runs/25024305694/job/73291844723\\n\\nBoth PRs otherwise had local targeted validation and GitHub Run Tests / Code Quality green at the time of observation.\\n\\n## Scope suggestion\\nPlan a narrow harness/CI repair, not a broad workflow rewrite:\\n- Ensure the stage prompt drift job can import `workspace_hub` in GitHub Actions (package layout/PYTHONPATH/install-mode decision).\\n- Ensure review-evidence check installs or exposes `uv` before invoking `scripts/enforcement/require-review-on-push.sh`, or make the script fail with a deterministic setup message.\\n- Add a focused regression/smoke that catches both failure modes.\\n\\n## Governance\\nThis issue is planning-required. Do not implement until a plan is reviewed and user-approved.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2532\", \"display_title\": \"fix(ci): repair PR review/stage-prompt guard environment failures\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/373\", \"title\": \"epic: Comprehensive Lower Tertiary Fields Report (10 GoM fields)\", \"issue_number\": 373, \"body\": \"## Goal\\n\\nProduce a buyer-facing comprehensive report covering all 10 Gulf of Mexico Lower Tertiary fields, replacing the existing partial summary at `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md` (which only fully treats the 6 producing fields).\\n\\n## The 10 fields\\n\\n**Producing (6):**\\n| Field | System | Water Depth | Cum Oil (MMBBL) | Operator |\\n|-------|--------|-------------|-----------------|----------|\\n| Jack/St. Malo | Subsea 15K | 7,240 ft | 412.46 | Chevron |\\n| Stones | Subsea 15K | 9,525 ft | 84.68 | Shell |\\n| Julia | Tieback 15K | 7,335 ft | 71.56 | ExxonMobil |\\n| Big Foot | Dry Tree | 5,190 ft | 68.96 | Chevron |\\n| Cascade/Chinook | Subsea 15K | 8,200 ft | 37.79 | Petrobras |\\n| Anchor | Subsea 20K | 5,080 ft | 9.51 | Chevron |\\n\\n**Under Development / Pre-FID (4):**\\n| Field | System | Water Depth | Status |\\n|-------|--------|-------------|--------|\\n| Shenandoah | Subsea 20K | 5,800 ft | Under Development |\\n| Kaskida | Subsea 20K | 5,860 ft | Pre-FID |\\n| Tiber | Subsea 20K | 4,130 ft | Pre-FID |\\n| North Platte | Subsea 20K | 5,840 ft | Pre-FID |\\n\\n## Phases (one sub-issue each \\u2014 to be linked after creation)\\n\\n1. **Field config completeness** \\u2014 backfill missing yamls (Big Foot, North Platte); ensure all 10 have current configs\\n2. **Per-field economic analysis** \\u2014 apply the lease\\u2192NPV pattern (#372) to all 10 fields with current assumptions\\n3. **Cross-field analytics** \\u2014 technology generation, operator concentration, HSE overlay, cost benchmarking via #338 surface\\n4. **Report assembly** \\u2014 HTML + PDF + executive summary + citations panel; supersedes current `lower_tertiary_field_summary.md`\\n\\n## Plan\\n\\nPlan landing under `docs/plans/` per the project's planning workflow (see `CLAUDE.md`). Each phase gets its own issue + plan, all at `status:plan-review` for user approval before implementation.\\n\\n## Why now\\n\\n- Lease-NPV demo (#372) just landed \\u2014 the per-field NPV pattern is proven\\n- Disclosure analytics surface (#338) shipped \\u2014 cost benchmarking is wired\\n- Catalog wiring (#371) lets bulk BSEE production data resolve through DataResolver\\n- Citations pattern (#372 demo, #361 follow-up) gives a defensibility template\\n\\nThe dependencies for a comprehensive report are now in place; this epic coordinates the work of producing it.\\n\\n## Acceptance for closure\\n\\n- [ ] All 4 sub-issues closed with their plans approved + implemented\\n- [ ] `reports/lower_tertiary/comprehensive_2026.md` (or .html) lands as the canonical buyer artifact\\n- [ ] Each of the 10 fields has its own data + economics + cross-cutting analytics fully traced\\n- [ ] Citations panel sources every numeric input\\n\\n## Refs\\n- Source-of-truth doc: `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md`\\n- Field configs: `config/analysis/lower_tertiary/fields/`\\n- Module: `src/worldenergydata/lower_tertiary/`\\n- Demo precedent: #372 (lease-NPV walkthrough)\\n- Cost surface: #338 (disclosure analytics)\\n- Catalog routing: #371 (partial-symlink wiring)\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/373\", \"display_title\": \"epic: Comprehensive Lower Tertiary Fields Report (10 GoM fields)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/377\", \"title\": \"feat(lt): comprehensive report assembly \\u2014 HTML + PDF + executive summary + citations (Phase 4 of #373)\", \"issue_number\": 377, \"body\": \"## Phase 4 of #373 \\u2014 Lower Tertiary Fields Report\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nFinal assembly. Take outputs from Phases 1\\u20133 and produce a single buyer-facing comprehensive artifact. Supersedes `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md` (which currently treats only the 6 producing fields).\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n\\n- [ ] `reports/lower_tertiary/comprehensive_2026.md` \\u2014 canonical markdown report (full 10 fields)\\n- [ ] `reports/lower_tertiary/comprehensive_2026.html` \\u2014 styled HTML for sharing\\n- [ ] `reports/lower_tertiary/comprehensive_2026.pdf` \\u2014 via Chrome headless / md-to-pdf skill\\n- [ ] Executive summary (one-page) with portfolio totals, key insights, technology generation breakdown\\n- [ ] Per-field detail sections (one per field \\u00d7 10) with economics + production history + HSE\\n- [ ] Citations panel embedded \\u2014 every numeric input traced to publisher + code_id + revision (per #361 pattern)\\n- [ ] Updated `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md` either replaced or marked superseded\\n\\n## Format reference\\n\\nUse the existing `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md` structure as a starting point but expanded to all 10 fields and structured with the section taxonomy from #373 epic (production, technology, operator, HSE, cost). Citations follow the #372 walkthrough pattern.\\n\\n## Plan\\n\\nPlan to be drafted at `docs/plans/2026-05-03-issue-377-lt-comprehensive-report-assembly.md`.\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n- #374 (Phase 1: configs) \\u2014 must be present\\n- #375 (Phase 2: per-field economics) \\u2014 feeds the per-field sections\\n- #376 (Phase 3: cross-field analytics) \\u2014 feeds the portfolio sections\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- [ ] All 10 fields covered with consistent depth\\n- [ ] HTML renders cleanly without external dependencies\\n- [ ] PDF generated via repo-blessed tooling\\n- [ ] Citations panel covers every number \\u2014 failed citations resolution = build fail\\n- [ ] Sign-off review against the epic's acceptance criteria (#373)\\n\\n## Refs\\n- Epic: #373\\n- Existing report: `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md`\\n- HTML template: `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.html`\\n- Demo pattern: #372 (citations panel)\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/377\", \"display_title\": \"feat(lt): comprehensive report assembly \\u2014 HTML + PDF + executive summary + citations (Phase 4 of #373)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/376\", \"title\": \"feat(lt): cross-field analytics \\u2014 technology, operator, HSE, cost benchmarking (Phase 3 of #373)\", \"issue_number\": 376, \"body\": \"## Phase 3 of #373 \\u2014 Lower Tertiary Fields Report\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nCross-field analytics that surface portfolio-level insights a per-field analysis cannot show on its own.\\n\\n## Sections\\n\\n### 3a. Technology generation comparison\\n- Subsea 15K vs. Subsea 20K vs. Tieback vs. Dry Tree\\n- Capex intensity ($M / MMbbl recoverable)\\n- Time-to-first-oil\\n- Recovery factor estimates\\n- Output: comparison table + chart\\n\\n### 3b. Operator + partnership concentration\\n- Working-interest-weighted production by operator\\n- JV structure per field (from yaml `partners`)\\n- Operator concentration metrics (HHI, top-3 share)\\n- Output: operator portfolio matrix\\n\\n### 3c. HSE incident overlay\\n- BSEE incident records per lease/field (uses HSE module + BSEE incidents data per #366)\\n- Incidents per MMbbl produced\\n- Severity distribution\\n- Output: HSE performance per field\\n- **Caveat:** depends on HSE bulk dedup (#366) for full incident detail; minimum-viable version uses BSEE current data only\\n\\n### 3d. Cost benchmarking via disclosure analytics\\n- Modelled capex (from yamls) vs. operator-disclosed annual capex\\n- Uses `DisclosureAnalyticsQuery.benchmark` (shipped #338)\\n- Per-field cost-disclosure deltas where comparable\\n- **Caveat:** requires #343 operator registry to have data for relevant operators; otherwise illustrative only\\n\\n## Plan\\n\\nPlan to be drafted at `docs/plans/2026-05-03-issue-376-cross-field-analytics-lt.md`.\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n- #375 (Phase 2) \\u2014 per-field economics provides the baseline to compare against\\n\\n## Refs\\n- Epic: #373\\n- HSE substrate: #366\\n- Disclosure surface: #338\\n- Operator registry (companion): #343\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/376\", \"display_title\": \"feat(lt): cross-field analytics \\u2014 technology, operator, HSE, cost benchmarking (Phase 3 of #373)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2622\", \"title\": \"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster C \\u2014 drop -p no:capture from QG command or refactor capsys tests (~17 errors)\", \"issue_number\": 2622, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). The Quality Gates command in \\\\`.claude/quality-gates.yaml:10\\\\` uses \\\\`-p no:capture\\\\` which removes the \\\\`capsys\\\\` fixture. Tests that declare \\\\`capsys\\\\` parameter then fail collection with \\\\`fixture 'capsys' not found\\\\`.\\n\\n## Defect\\n\\nDirect hit: \\\\`tests/hydrodynamics/passing_ship/test_passing_ship_cli.py\\\\` (2 errors).\\n\\nLikely blast radius: across the suite, **16 tests use \\\\`capsys\\\\`** in 6 files. Several already surface as ERRORs in the maxfail=200 run with the same fixture-not-found pattern:\\n\\n- \\\\`tests/hydrodynamics/passing_ship/test_passing_ship_cli.py\\\\` (2 errors confirmed)\\n- \\\\`tests/solvers/orcaflex/test_template_generator.py::TestCLI\\\\` (5 errors \\u2014 likely same root cause; verify)\\n- \\\\`tests/solvers/orcaflex/modular_generator/test_campaign_generator.py::TestCLICampaign*\\\\` (6 errors \\u2014 likely same root cause; verify)\\n- \\\\`tests/test_propeller_rudder_interaction.py\\\\`, \\\\`tests/structural/fatigue_analysis/test_fatigue_cli.py\\\\`, \\\\`tests/infrastructure/common/test_data.py\\\\` (likely also affected)\\n\\n\\\\`caplog\\\\` is unaffected because it comes from \\\\`_pytest.logging\\\\`, not \\\\`_pytest.capture\\\\`.\\n\\n## Fix options\\n\\n- **(a) Drop \\\\`-p no:capture\\\\` from \\\\`.claude/quality-gates.yaml\\\\` line 10** \\u2014 cheapest; verify it wasn't added for a perf reason. Single-line change.\\n- **(b) Refactor the capsys tests** \\u2014 use \\\\`monkeypatch\\\\` against \\\\`sys.stdout\\\\`, or use \\\\`subprocess.run\\\\` for actual CLI behavior. Larger surface.\\n\\nRecommend (a) unless there's a documented reason for the \\\\`-p no:capture\\\\` flag.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] \\\\`-p no:capture\\\\` removed (or capsys tests refactored to not depend on it)\\n- [ ] Run repo-wide pytest with QG command: 0 \\\\`fixture 'capsys' not found\\\\` errors\\n- [ ] Verify the 5 \\\\`test_template_generator.py\\\\` errors and 6 \\\\`test_campaign_generator.py\\\\` errors clear (or remain \\u2014 separate root cause)\\n- [ ] No previously-passing tests regress\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614), [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2622\", \"display_title\": \"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster C \\u2014 drop -p no:capture from QG command or refactor capsys tests (~17 errors)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2621\", \"title\": \"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster B \\u2014 extend _HAS_WED_DCF skipif to 4 sibling test classes (14 failures)\", \"issue_number\": 2621, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616) maxfail-cap investigation. PR [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543) added \\\\`_HAS_WED_DCF\\\\` probe and \\\\`@pytest.mark.skipif\\\\` only to \\\\`TestBuildEconomicsSchedule\\\\` (line 486 of \\\\`tests/field_development/test_economics.py\\\\`). Four sibling classes also depend on \\\\`worldenergydata.economics.*\\\\` at runtime but were never decorated.\\n\\n## Defect\\n\\nIn \\\\`tests/field_development/test_economics.py\\\\`, four classes share the same \\\\`worldenergydata\\\\` import paths but lack the skipif:\\n\\n| Class | Line | Failures | Import path it depends on |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| \\\\`TestCarbonSensitivity\\\\` | 577 | 5 | \\\\`worldenergydata.economics.carbon\\\\` (via \\\\`carbon_sensitivity()\\\\` at \\\\`economics.py:989\\\\`) |\\n| \\\\`TestDeclineBackwardCompatibility\\\\` | 829 | 1 | \\\\`worldenergydata.economics.dcf.CashFlowSchedule\\\\` (via \\\\`build_economics_schedule()\\\\` at \\\\`economics.py:886\\\\`) |\\n| \\\\`TestScheduleDeclineIntegration\\\\` | 860 | 2 | same as above |\\n| \\\\`TestFiscalRegimeTaxAdjustment\\\\` | 1006 | 6 | \\\\`worldenergydata.{eia_us,ukcs,brazil_anp,west_africa,sodir}\\\\` (via \\\\`_apply_fiscal_regime\\\\`/\\\\`_resolve_fiscal_calculator\\\\` at \\\\`economics.py:625-675\\\\`) |\\n\\nTotal: **14 failures** + per-class breakdowns above.\\n\\n## Nuance\\n\\nOf the 18 test methods in those four classes, 4 pass (those that don't actually exercise the import path \\u2014 early returns / mocked passthrough / structural checks). So a per-class skipif would over-skip; per-method or per-import-block guarding may be more careful. Quickest correct fix: per-class skipif with the same probe.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] All 14 \\\\`ModuleNotFoundError: worldenergydata\\\\` failures in \\\\`tests/field_development/test_economics.py\\\\` either pass or skip cleanly (no errors)\\n- [ ] No previously-passing tests regress\\n- [ ] Skipif decorator pattern matches the existing line-486 \\\\`TestBuildEconomicsSchedule\\\\` precedent\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614), [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616) \\u2014 parent investigations\\n- [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543) \\u2014 the original incomplete fix\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2621\", \"display_title\": \"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster B \\u2014 extend _HAS_WED_DCF skipif to 4 sibling test classes (14 failures)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2612\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): lng-projects wiki topical expansion \\u2014 8 concept/entity pages (W5-C)\", \"issue_number\": 2612, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nTopical expansion of the **lng-projects LLM-wiki** at `knowledge/wikis/lng-projects/`. Currently 7 .md files. Plan proposes 8 concept/entity pages disjoint from in-flight vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#14 SESA / vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2544 Woodfibre source-page work.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- **Target:** `knowledge/wikis/lng-projects/wiki/concepts/` + `wiki/entities/`\\n- **Topics:** project lifecycle phases, liquefaction technologies (APC/DMR/cascade/MR/MFC), full-containment LNG tanks, FLNG vs FSRU vs onshore terminal, BOG management, vapor cloud explosion process safety, regulatory anchors (NFPA 59A, EN 1473, IGC code), project shapes\\n- **Path-sanction:** lng-projects is NOT in the {marine-engineering, engineering, naval-architecture} principle scope per memory \\u2014 plan stays in `wiki/concepts/` + `wiki/entities/`. Standards-page routing flagged as Open Question for separate sanction (see W5-D companion plan)\\n\\n## Notable\\n- No `knowledge/seeds/lng-projects-resources.yaml` exists (only `mooring-failures-lng-terminals.yaml`) \\u2014 `index.md` hand-edited, flagged as risk\\n- Reservation regex covers SESA / Woodfibre / ACMA-31522 / Doris-62092 noun-phrases to prevent overlap with source-page work\\n\\n## Workflow\\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 5-C, 2026-05-03; allowlist test passes)\\n- [ ] Adversarial review\\n- [ ] User approval\\n- [ ] Implementation\\n\\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review`.**\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2612\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): lng-projects wiki topical expansion \\u2014 8 concept/entity pages (W5-C)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2602\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): engineering wiki pipeline sub-domain topical expansion (W4-D)\", \"issue_number\": 2602, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\n**Topical expansion of engineering wiki \\u2014 PIPELINE sub-domain.** Currently 2 pipeline-related wiki files. 10 high-value concept pages backfilled from canonical pipeline engineering literature + /mnt/ace raw (Saipem). Same shape as W3-D ([#2597](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2597) \\u2014 engineering riser sub-domain), post-W3-C amendment.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- **Target:** `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/concepts/pipeline-<topic>.md`\\n- **10 topics:** route selection, on-bottom stability, lateral buckling, upheaval buckling, free-span analysis, trawl-impact protection, pipe-soil interaction, installation methods (S-lay/J-lay/reel-lay/tow-out), pipeline coatings, pipeline-walking + end-expansion/spool design\\n- **Path-sanction:** engineering wiki's own `CLAUDE.md` schema (NOT #2471 \\u2014 engineering wiki IS in routing-principle scope per memory, but #2471 itself is CSA-Z276-only)\\n- **Boundary discipline:** TDD regex tests prevent scope-creep into riser/mooring/umbilical sub-domains\\n\\n## Lesson banked from W3-C\\nW4-D adds `test_no_2471_path_sanction_citation` \\u2014 a per-plan self-test complementing the governance allowlist (`tests/governance/test_2471_citation_scope.py`).\\n\\n## Workflow\\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 4-D, 2026-05-03; allowlist test passes)\\n- [ ] Adversarial review\\n- [ ] User approval\\n- [ ] Implementation\\n\\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review`.**\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2602\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): engineering wiki pipeline sub-domain topical expansion (W4-D)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2597\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): engineering wiki riser sub-domain topical expansion (W3-D)\", \"issue_number\": 2597, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\n**Topical expansion of the engineering wiki \\u2014 RISER sub-domain.** ~8-10 high-value concept pages backfilled from canonical riser engineering literature + /mnt/ace raw + W3-B's ISO 19901-7 cross-links. Scope is riser-specific only (NOT pipeline / mooring / structural \\u2014 separate sub-domains).\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- **Target:** `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/concepts/riser-<topic>.md`\\n- **Topics:** SCR (steel catenary riser), TTR (top-tensioned riser), flexible riser, hybrid riser, drilling riser, riser configurations, VIV, wave-fatigue, drift-current loading, riser-soil interaction\\n- **Path-sanction:** engineering wiki's own `CLAUDE.md` schema (engineering wiki IS in the routing-principle scope)\\n- **Boundary discipline:** scope-creep into pipeline / mooring / umbilical sub-domains is enforced via TDD regex tests\\n\\n## Workflow status\\n\\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 3-D, 2026-05-02)\\n- [ ] Adversarial review (Claude internal)\\n- [ ] User approval\\n- [ ] Implementation\\n\\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review` \\u2014 never self-approved.**\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- Same shape as [#2589](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2589) (W1-D naval-architecture topical expansion)\\n- Cross-links to W3-B (ISO 19901-7) once that page exists\\n- Independent of [#2588](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2588) (W1-C audit) \\u2014 re-sequenceable\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2597\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): engineering wiki riser sub-domain topical expansion (W3-D)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2592\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): maritime-law wiki topical expansion \\u2014 IMO conventions + 10 core concepts (W2-C)\", \"issue_number\": 2592, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nTopical expansion of the **maritime-law LLM-wiki** at `knowledge/wikis/maritime-law/wiki/`. Currently 22 wiki files. The plan proposes 10 high-value pages: 6 legal-concept pages (general-average, salvage, limitation-of-liability, port-state-control, flag-state-jurisdiction, charterparties) + 4 IMO/ILO standards pages (MARPOL-73-78, SOLAS-1974, MLC-2006, ISM-Code). The 4 standards pages would create the **first `wiki/standards/` directory** in the maritime-law wiki.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- **Target wiki:** `knowledge/wikis/maritime-law/wiki/concepts/` (6 pages) + `wiki/standards/<code-id>.md` (4 pages)\\n- **Sources:** Aleka Mandaraka-Sheppard, Schoenbaum, IMO publication catalog\\n- **Boundary:** commercial shipping / IMO regulatory regime / admiralty \\u2014 NOT corporate or environmental law more broadly\\n- **Path routing for IMO conventions:** **OPEN QUESTION** \\u2014 per memory `project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md`, #2471 sanction is CSA-Z276-only, so IMO standards routing is NOT formally sanctioned. Plan flags this as a load-bearing decision for user approval.\\n\\n## Workflow status\\n\\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 2-C, 2026-05-02)\\n- [ ] Adversarial review (Claude internal)\\n- [ ] User approval (`status:plan-approved`)\\n- [ ] Implementation (TDD)\\n\\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review` \\u2014 never self-approved.**\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- Same-wave shape: [#2589](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2589) (W1-D naval-architecture topical expansion)\\n- Parent context: [#2540](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2540) (Elements wave)\\n- Sanctions: #2471 path routing (scope-limited)\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2592\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): maritime-law wiki topical expansion \\u2014 IMO conventions + 10 core concepts (W2-C)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2589\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): naval-architecture wiki topical expansion \\u2014 10 core concept pages (W1-D)\", \"issue_number\": 2589, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nTopical expansion of the **naval-architecture LLM-wiki** at `knowledge/wikis/naval-architecture/wiki/`. Currently 62 wiki files. The plan proposes 10 high-value foundational concept pages backfilled from canonical naval-architecture curriculum (PNA SNAME volumes, Tupper Introduction, ITTC procedures), with explicit non-overlap with in-flight `status:plan-approved` issues #2566 (yaw / rudder-stock) and #2568 (turning-circle / tactical-diameter).\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- **Target wiki:** `knowledge/wikis/naval-architecture/wiki/concepts/`\\n- **Reserved (excluded):** topics covered by #2566 and #2568\\n- **Approach:** concept summaries + cross-links + frontmatter, no textbook copy (per #2482)\\n- **Standards backbone:** IMO regulatory framework, ITTC procedures, IACS / classification societies (DNV/ABS/LR/BV/CCS)\\n\\n## Workflow status\\n\\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 1-D, 2026-05-02)\\n- [ ] Adversarial review (Gemini + Claude-internal \\u2014 in flight)\\n- [ ] User approval (`status:plan-approved`)\\n- [ ] Implementation (TDD per template)\\n\\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review` \\u2014 never self-approved.**\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- Parent context: #2540 (Elements wave) \\u2014 non-overlapping\\n- Reserved phrases excluded: #2566 (yaw / rudder-stock), #2568 (turning-circle / tactical-diameter)\\n- Sanctions: #2471 (path routing), #2482 (vendor-derivative deny-list)\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2589\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): naval-architecture wiki topical expansion \\u2014 10 core concept pages (W1-D)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2587\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): asset-management wiki topical scaffold + scope boundary (W1-B)\", \"issue_number\": 2587, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nTopical scaffold and scope-boundary fix for the **asset-management LLM-wiki** at `knowledge/wikis/asset-management/wiki/`. Currently 4 hand-touched files plus 1 source page (financial-portfolio scope). The plan proposes ~12 concept pages + standards pages (ISO 55000 family, IAM Competency Framework, API RP 580/581, DNV-RP-G101, NORSOK Z-008, HSE Safety Case Regs) plus a load-bearing scope-boundary disclaimer separating engineering asset-integrity from financial-portfolio asset-management.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- **Target wiki:** `knowledge/wikis/asset-management/wiki/`\\n- **Boundary:** engineering integrity-management domain only (NOT financial assethold/portfolio)\\n- **Routing:** concepts under `wiki/concepts/`, standards under `wiki/standards/<code-id>.md` (#2471)\\n- **Deny-list:** no vendor-derivative copy per vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2482\\n\\n## Workflow status\\n\\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 1-B, 2026-05-02)\\n- [ ] Adversarial review (Gemini + Claude-internal \\u2014 in flight)\\n- [ ] User approval (`status:plan-approved`)\\n- [ ] Implementation (TDD per template)\\n\\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review` \\u2014 never self-approved.**\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- Parent context: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2540 (Elements wave) \\u2014 Casa-Grande financial corpus is OUT OF SCOPE here\\n- Sanctions: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2471 (path routing), vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2482 (vendor-derivative deny-list)\\n- Sibling planning waves: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#14 SESA LNG, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2542 Doris University, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2543 Doris codes, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2544 Woodfibre LNG, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2559 OCIMF Tandem\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2587\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): asset-management wiki topical scaffold + scope boundary (W1-B)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2615\", \"title\": \"meta(governance): umbrella sanction \\u2014 wiki/standards/ routing for engineering-standards / asset-management / lng-projects / acma-projects (W5-D follow-up)\", \"issue_number\": 2615, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\n**Umbrella sanction issue** spawned from W5-D ([#2613](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2613)) per user approval 2026-05-03. Formally codifies `wiki/standards/<code-id>.md` routing for the wikis that the W3-C erratum (#2596) re-anchored to local `CLAUDE.md` schema but that the existing memory `project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md` does not yet enumerate.\\n\\n## Per-wiki decision matrix (executed under this issue)\\n\\n| Wiki | Action | Detail |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `engineering-standards` | **Codify** | Append `Sanctioned-by: #<this-issue>` reference in `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/CLAUDE.md` (W3-C re-anchor for W1-A #2586) |\\n| `asset-management` | **Codify** | Append `Sanctioned-by: #<this-issue>` reference in `knowledge/wikis/asset-management/CLAUDE.md` (W3-C re-anchor for W1-B #2587) |\\n| `lng-projects` | **User decision** | Auto-generated CLAUDE.md schema; conditional codification pending user assent during this umbrella's review |\\n| `acma-projects` | **User decision** | Same as lng-projects |\\n| `marine-engineering` | **Affirm-with-defer** | In-principle per memory; defer canonical-home to aces-#4 Phase 1; structural fix via #2601 (W4-C audit) |\\n| `engineering` | **Affirm** | Already in-principle |\\n| `naval-architecture` | **Affirm** | Already in-principle |\\n| `maritime-law` | **Reaffirm out-of-scope** | Memory verbatim; no CLAUDE.md change |\\n| `personal` | **Reaffirm out-of-scope** | Memory verbatim; no CLAUDE.md change |\\n| `health-reports` | **Reaffirm out-of-scope** | Memory verbatim; no CLAUDE.md change |\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n\\nWhen this umbrella issue itself is approved + implemented:\\n\\n1. **CLAUDE.md edits** for `engineering-standards/`, `asset-management/`, plus conditionally for `lng-projects/` and `acma-projects/` (pending user assent)\\n2. **Governance test extension** at `tests/governance/test_2471_citation_scope.py` \\u2014 add `test_out_of_principle_wiki_routing_requires_sanction_citation` enforcing that any future plan citing `wiki/standards/<code-id>.md` routing for an out-of-principle wiki must include a sanction-issue citation\\n3. **Memory update** at `~/.claude/projects/-mnt-local-analysis-workspace-hub/memory/project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md` \\u2014 enumerate the formally-sanctioned wikis with this issue's number\\n4. **README index update** at `docs/plans/README.md`\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n- [x] Spawned by approved W5-D plan #2613 (user approval 2026-05-03 Tier A)\\n- [ ] Adversarial review (this umbrella's own review cycle)\\n- [ ] User approval (`status:plan-approved`)\\n- [ ] Implementation (CLAUDE.md edits + test + memory + README)\\n- [ ] Close\\n\\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review` until you re-approve this umbrella explicitly. The W5-D approval authorized this umbrella's CREATION, not its IMPLEMENTATION** \\u2014 that's an additional gate per `feedback_never_offer_to_self_label_plan_approved.md`.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- Parent plan: [#2613](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2613) (W5-D)\\n- Predecessor erratum: [#2596](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2596) (W3-C \\u2014 closed)\\n- W3-C re-anchored plans: [#2586](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2586) (W1-A), [#2587](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2587) (W1-B), [#2592](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2592) (W2-C), [#2597](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2597) (W3-D)\\n- Marine-engineering structural fix: [#2601](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2601) (W4-C \\u2014 orthogonal; this umbrella only cross-references)\\n- Cross-repo: aceengineer-strategy aces-#4 (canonical-home decision)\\n- Memory: `project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md`\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2615\", \"display_title\": \"meta(governance): umbrella sanction \\u2014 wiki/standards/ routing for engineering-standards / asset-management / lng-projects / acma-projects (W5-D follow-up)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616\", \"title\": \"fix(digitalmodel-tests): identify the 4 non-hydrodynamics CI failures masked by maxfail=20\", \"issue_number\": 2616, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nSurfaced during [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614) hydrodynamics-debt triage.\\n\\nThe original CI run https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/actions/runs/25270693237 reported \\\\`20 failed, 3023 passed\\\\` with \\\\`maxfail=20\\\\` (set by [digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543)).\\n\\nThe hydrodynamics triage in \\\\`/tmp/hydrodynamics-debt-triage.md\\\\` enumerated 16 failures in \\\\`tests/hydrodynamics/diffraction/\\\\`. **The remaining 4 must live elsewhere in \\\\`tests/\\\\`** but were never identified because \\\\`maxfail=20\\\\` stopped the run as soon as the 20-failure cap was hit.\\n\\nAfter [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614)'s 4 categories land, those 16 should clear; the remaining 4 will then be the only failures and easy to identify.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] After [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614)'s Cats A-D land, run repo-wide \\\\`pytest --maxfail=20 ...\\\\` (or higher cap) on a clean digitalmodel CI environment\\n- [ ] Enumerate the remaining failures and their file paths\\n- [ ] File a per-category followup (or one consolidated tracker) for each\\n- [ ] Consider whether the \\\\`maxfail=20\\\\` cap from [digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543) should be relaxed once the queue is clean\\n\\n## Why now\\n\\nUntil these 4 are identified and addressed, every digitalmodel PR will continue to admin-merge through Quality Gates (the precedent established by PRs [#541](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/541), [#544](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/544), [#549](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/549), [#550](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/550), [#551](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/551)). The admin-override flow is unsustainable as a default merge path.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614) \\u2014 hydrodynamics debt tracker (Cats A-D)\\n- [#2129](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2129) \\u2014 issue-state drift / quality-gates audit umbrella\\n- [#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580) \\u2014 original Quality Gates unblock that triggered the cascade\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616\", \"display_title\": \"fix(digitalmodel-tests): identify the 4 non-hydrodynamics CI failures masked by maxfail=20\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/375\", \"title\": \"feat(lt): per-field economic analysis for all 10 fields \\u2014 NPV/IRR/payback + sensitivities (Phase 2 of #373)\", \"issue_number\": 375, \"body\": \"## Phase 2 of #373 \\u2014 Lower Tertiary Fields Report\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nApply the lease\\u2192NPV demo pattern (#372) at field level to all 10 LT fields. Produce a comparable per-field economics table.\\n\\n## Required outputs\\n\\nFor each of the 10 fields:\\n- NPV @ 10% (annual, $M)\\n- IRR (annual)\\n- MIRR (annual, with reinvestment rate)\\n- Payback period (years)\\n- Breakeven oil price\\n- 5-point sensitivity table ($50 / $60 / $70 / $80 / $100 per bbl)\\n- Citations panel sourcing every input\\n\\nOutput formats:\\n- `results/lower_tertiary/per_field_economics_2026.csv` (machine-readable)\\n- `results/lower_tertiary/per_field_economics_2026.html` (buyer-presentable)\\n\\n## Approach\\n\\nReuse `EconomicsQuery.all_metrics` and `AssumptionsManager` per the #372 walkthrough. Inputs come from `config/analysis/lower_tertiary/fields/*.yml` + `config/analysis/lower_tertiary/economic_assumptions.yml` + WTI series via `wti_prices.load_wti_series`.\\n\\nFor producing fields: cashflow grounded in BSEE OGOR aggregation where available (otherwise documented decline-curve fallback).\\nFor pre-FID fields: documented assumptions only with explicit \\\"preliminary\\\" tag in the citations panel.\\n\\n## Plan\\n\\nPlan to be drafted at `docs/plans/2026-05-03-issue-375-per-field-economics-10-lt-fields.md`.\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n- #374 (config backfill \\u2014 must ship first to give Big Foot + North Platte yamls)\\n\\n## Refs\\n- Epic: #373\\n- Pattern source: #372 (lease-NPV walkthrough)\\n- Module: `src/worldenergydata/lower_tertiary/npv.py`\\n- Module: `src/worldenergydata/lower_tertiary/wti_prices.py`\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/375\", \"display_title\": \"feat(lt): per-field economic analysis for all 10 fields \\u2014 NPV/IRR/payback + sensitivities (Phase 2 of #373)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2606\", \"title\": \"fix(digitalmodel): uv sync hangs at finalize step on Python 3.11 venv\", \"issue_number\": 2606, \"body\": \"**Source:** [#2566](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2566) validation report (observed on ace-linux-1, 2026-05-02)\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n`cd digitalmodel && uv sync` substantively completes (venv populated, all packages installed, log shows `Uninstalled 44 packages in 4.49s` and then nothing) but the parent process never exits. Observed PID staying alive >18s (and >60s on retry) after the venv is fully usable.\\n\\n## Workaround\\n\\nKill the PID manually after verifying `digitalmodel/.venv/bin/` has the expected entries (alembic, aqwa, assetutils-devtools, etc.).\\n\\n## Hypothesis\\n\\nPossibly related to the Python 3.11 venv finalize step (vs. workspace's Python 3.13 miniforge), or a compiled-extension build hook that doesn't exit.\\n\\n## Acceptance\\n\\n- [ ] `uv sync` exits cleanly within reasonable time after install completes\\n- [ ] OR documented workaround / known limitation in `digitalmodel/CONTRIBUTING.md` or `README.md`\\n\\n## Severity\\n\\nMedium \\u2014 degrades CI ergonomics; survivable with manual intervention.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2606\", \"display_title\": \"fix(digitalmodel): uv sync hangs at finalize step on Python 3.11 venv\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2607\", \"title\": \"chore(digitalmodel): document lxml build dependency for clean-venv smoke installs\", \"issue_number\": 2607, \"body\": \"**Source:** [#2566](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2566) validation report\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n`pip install dist/digitalmodel-0.1.1-py3-none-any.whl` in a clean Python 3.13 venv on Ubuntu 25.10 fails with:\\n\\n```\\nERROR: Failed to build 'lxml' when getting requirements to build wheel\\n```\\n\\n`lxml` requires native compilation (`libxml2-dev`, `libxslt1-dev`); clean venvs without system dev packages cannot install it.\\n\\n## Acceptance options\\n\\nPick one:\\n- [ ] Document `libxml2-dev` + `libxslt1-dev` system requirement in `digitalmodel/README.md` install section\\n- [ ] OR pin a pre-built `lxml` wheel in `pyproject.toml` constraints\\n- [ ] OR replace lxml usage with stdlib `xml.etree`/`html.parser` if scope is small\\n\\n## Severity\\n\\nLow \\u2014 affects smoke-install docs and CI bootstrapping; `uv sync` works fine.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2607\", \"display_title\": \"chore(digitalmodel): document lxml build dependency for clean-venv smoke installs\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2617\", \"title\": \"fix(digitalmodel): test_packaged_yaml_in_built_distribution_preserves_existing_package_data flaky under parallel pytest sessions\", \"issue_number\": 2617, \"body\": \"**Discovered during:** #2603 implementation session on 2026-05-03; cross-ref [digitalmodel#548](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/548) post-merge verification.\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n`tests/naval_architecture/test_rudder_stock_torque_sweep.py::test_packaged_yaml_in_built_distribution_preserves_existing_package_data` fails intermittently when run alongside other pytest sessions on the same host:\\n\\n```\\nE     error: [Errno 2] No such file or directory\\nE     ERROR Backend subprocess exited when trying to invoke build_wheel\\n```\\n\\n## Root cause\\n\\nThe test runs `uv run --no-sync --with build --with setuptools --with wheel python -m build --wheel --no-isolation --outdir <tmp_path>`. With `--no-isolation`, the build subprocess interacts with the project's tmp/build state. When two pytest sessions run concurrently against the same workspace (e.g., the `/whats-next` parallel verification pattern), the build subprocess can race against another session's tmp cleanup and hit ENOENT.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\nReproduced 2026-05-03:\\n- Run alongside `test_yaw_moment_sweep.py`: FAIL with ENOENT\\n- Run in isolation immediately after: PASS in 33-110s\\n- Re-run alongside other test, isolation-mode rerun: PASS\\n\\nThe flake is **not** caused by [digitalmodel#548](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/548) (#2603 fix). Verified by stashing the __init__.py diff: test still passed in isolation; flake was tmpdir-race-driven.\\n\\n## Fix options (pick one)\\n\\n- [ ] (a) Add `pytest.mark.filterwarnings`/`pytest_xdist_group` so this test runs in its own group, blocking parallel sibling tests\\n- [ ] (b) Switch to `--isolation` (full PEP 517 build env per invocation) \\u2014 slower but no shared state\\n- [ ] (c) Add a retry decorator (`@pytest.mark.flaky(reruns=2)`) \\u2014 accepts the flake as-is\\n- [ ] (d) Use a per-test scratch dir with `tmp_path_factory` and ensure no shared parent tmpdir\\n\\nRecommended: (b) for correctness, (a) or (d) if (b) is too slow.\\n\\n## Acceptance\\n\\n- [ ] Test passes 10/10 under parallel-session contention (target host: ace-linux-1)\\n- [ ] No regression in solo-run timing > 50%\\n\\n## Severity\\n\\nLow \\u2014 does not affect correctness of #2564/#2565/#2603 work; test is real and useful, just flaky under load.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2617\", \"display_title\": \"fix(digitalmodel): test_packaged_yaml_in_built_distribution_preserves_existing_package_data flaky under parallel pytest sessions\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2605\", \"title\": \"chore(digitalmodel): ruff cleanup for naval_architecture/test_vessel_fleet_adapter.py (13 F401)\", \"issue_number\": 2605, \"body\": \"**Source:** [#2566](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2566) validation report\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n`ruff check src/digitalmodel/naval_architecture/ tests/naval_architecture/` reports **13 errors** all in `tests/naval_architecture/test_vessel_fleet_adapter.py` \\u2014 F401 unused imports.\\n\\nExample:\\n```\\nF401 [*] `digitalmodel.naval_architecture.ship_data.get_ship` imported but unused\\n   --> tests/naval_architecture/test_vessel_fleet_adapter.py:200:13\\n```\\n\\nAll 13 are auto-fixable with `ruff check --fix`.\\n\\n## Acceptance\\n\\n- [ ] `ruff check --fix tests/naval_architecture/test_vessel_fleet_adapter.py` resolves all 13 F401\\n- [ ] No regressions in test functionality\\n\\n## Severity\\n\\nLow \\u2014 auto-fixable cleanup.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2605\", \"display_title\": \"chore(digitalmodel): ruff cleanup for naval_architecture/test_vessel_fleet_adapter.py (13 F401)\"}]}"}],"structured_content":{"issues":[{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2659","title":"Wiki ingest: anthropics/financial-services → llm-wiki engineering/sources","issue_number":2659,"body":"> **Revision 3 (2026-05-09)** — addresses Codex round-2 stale-count defect. Counter values now expressed as a write-time rule, not frozen integers. Round-2 artifact: [scripts/review/results/2026-05-09-plan-2659-codex-r2.md](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/scripts/review/results/2026-05-09-plan-2659-codex-r2.md). Round-1 artifact: [scripts/review/results/2026-05-09-plan-2659-codex.md](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/scripts/review/results/2026-05-09-plan-2659-codex.md).\n\n## Summary\n\nAdd Anthropic's [`anthropics/financial-services`](https://github.com/anthropics/financial-services) (Apache-2.0) as an **external-source page** in `vamseeachanta/llm-wiki` under `wikis/engineering/wiki/sources/`. The financial-services domain content is ignored; only the agent-architecture lessons are captured (managed-agent cookbooks, `callable_agents` preview, `handoff_request` events, dual Cowork+API deployment surface).\n\n**Complexity:** T1 (trivial — three file edits, one commit, no runtime claim, no standards, no code).\n\n**Reproduction Evidence:** N/A — documentation-only, no runtime failure claim per `issue-planning-mode` Step 1.5 skip-allowed clause.\n\n**Spec on disk (informational only, not a required input):** `docs/governance/2026-05-09-anthropics-financial-services-ingest-design.md` exists locally as an early draft. **This issue body is the canonical plan**; the on-disk spec is reference material for human reviewers, not implementation input.\n\n## Resource Intelligence Summary\n\n### Existing repo code\n- `llm-wiki/wikis/engineering/wiki/sources/` held 23 source pages at plan-draft time (verified 2026-05-09 via `gh api`). The implementation re-counts at write time (see \"Index update\" below). Five existing pages are meta-AI methodology sources: `agent-equivalence-architecture-doc.md`, `ai-agent-guidelines-doc.md`, `ai-development-ecosystem-doc.md`, `compound-engineering-methodology.md`, `methodology-docs.md`. The new entry extends this thread.\n- Existing concepts: `agent-delegation`, `orchestrator-worker-separation`, `multi-agent-parity`, `compound-engineering` — cross-link targets confirmed present in `wikis/engineering/wiki/concepts/` (Codex round-1 verified all four resolve).\n\n### Standards\nNot applicable (documentation-only, no code/standards involvement).\n\n### LLM Wiki pages consulted (live state, plan-draft 2026-05-09)\n- `llm-wiki/wikis/engineering/wiki/index.md` — frontmatter `page_count: 107`, `source_count: 16`, `last_updated: 2026-05-06`. Header reads `## Sources (13 pages)`. Actual file count = 23 at plan-draft. **Both counters are deflated phantoms.** Reconciliation rule below.\n- `llm-wiki/wikis/engineering/wiki/log.md` — append `[2026-05-09] ingest` block with firewall language verbatim (text pinned below).\n\n### Peer source-page schema (verified, 2026-05-09)\nTwo patterns exist in `wikis/engineering/wiki/sources/`. The proposed page follows **Pattern B (external/community sources)**, matching the only existing precedent for an external-URL source (`2026-04-17-hn-cadquery.md`):\n- `title` (string)\n- `tags` (list)\n- `sources` (scalar list of URLs)\n- `added` (date)\n- `last_updated` (date)\n- `domain: engineering`\n\nPattern A (internal/repo-doc sources, e.g., `agent-equivalence-architecture-doc.md`) uses `source_path`, `source_type`, `ingested` — **not applicable here** because the source is an external GitHub repo, not a workspace-hub internal doc.\n\n### Documents consulted\n- Workflow memory `project_llm_wiki_external_post_ingest_workflow.md` — 8-step ingest pattern; firewall language pinned.\n- Strategic-role memory `project_llm_wiki_strategic_role.md` — coverage gaps are first-class defects; routing principle.\n- Spinout governance `project_llm_wiki_spunout.md` — confirms target repo + license firewall.\n- Codex adversarial reviews at `scripts/review/results/2026-05-09-plan-2659-codex.md` (round 1, MAJOR) and `2026-05-09-plan-2659-codex-r2.md` (round 2, MAJOR — produced revision-3 corrections).\n\n### Gaps identified\n- No existing wiki page covers managed-agent orchestration patterns specifically (callable_agents API surface, handoff_request control plane, agent.yaml + leaf-worker subagents structure).\n- Concept-page gap: `concepts/managed-agent-orchestration.md` — acknowledged but **deferred** per YAGNI.\n\n## Files to Change\n\n### 1. New source page\n\n**Path:** `wikis/engineering/wiki/sources/2026-05-09-anthropics-financial-services.md`\n\n**Slug rationale:** date-first `<YYYY-MM-DD>-<community>-<slug>` per Pattern B precedent (`2026-04-17-hn-cadquery.md`).\n\n**Frontmatter (verbatim, Pattern B):**\n\n```yaml\n---\ntitle: \"Claude for Financial Services — managed-agent reference patterns (anthropics/financial-services)\"\ntags: [managed-agents, multi-agent-orchestration, plugin-architecture, anthropic, agent-handoff, leaf-worker-subagents]\nsources:\n  - https://github.com/anthropics/financial-services\nadded: 2026-05-09\nlast_updated: 2026-05-09\ndomain: engineering\n---\n```\n\nNo `license` or `observed_pushed_at` keys. License (Apache-2.0) and pushed_at observation cited in body prose (re-fetched at write time via `gh api repos/anthropics/financial-services --jq '.pushed_at'`).\n\n**Section outline:**\n- **Relevance** — Anthropic finance-vertical reference repo as methodology-grade input for marine/offshore wiki; agent architecture is domain-agnostic, finance is incidental.\n- **Key teachings** — managed-agent cookbook structure (`agent.yaml` + `subagents/*.yaml` leaf workers + `steering-examples.json`); `callable_agents` research-preview pattern (depth-1 leaf workers); `handoff_request` events as cross-agent control plane (`scripts/orchestrate.py` reference loop); dual-surface principle; self-contained agent plugins; vertical-plugin decoupling; partner-built plugin pattern.\n- **How this maps to existing wiki structure** — cross-links to `concepts/agent-delegation`, `concepts/orchestrator-worker-separation`, `concepts/multi-agent-parity`, `sources/agent-equivalence-architecture-doc`.\n- **Use as a wiki source** — citation pointer for future managed-agent or plugin-architecture wiki content.\n- **License and provenance** — Apache-2.0 per `LICENSE` file (verified GitHub MCP); pushed_at re-fetched at write time.\n\n### 2. Index update — write-time counter rule\n\n**Path:** `wikis/engineering/wiki/index.md`\n\n**Pre-existing drift discovered during round-1 adversarial review:** at plan-draft time, frontmatter said `source_count: 16`, header said `## Sources (13 pages)`, actual file count was 23 (verified `gh api`). Both counters are deflated phantoms.\n\n**Reconciliation rule (write-time-computed, NOT a frozen value):**\n\nAt write time, the implementing agent MUST execute this sequence:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Re-count actual sources/ files on llm-wiki main, immediately before staging the new page\nN=$(gh api repos/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/contents/wikis/engineering/wiki/sources \\\n      --jq '[.[] | select(.name | endswith(\".md\"))] | length')\nTARGET=$((N + 1))\n\n# 2. Read current index.md frontmatter source_count and header pluralization\n# 3. Set frontmatter source_count = TARGET\n# 4. Set header \"## Sources (X pages)\" → \"## Sources (TARGET pages)\"\n# 5. Verify post-write: count files in sources/ (including the new one) equals TARGET\n```\n\n**At plan-draft time `N` was 23 → `TARGET` would be 24.** This frozen number is **a baseline expectation, not an acceptance criterion.** If `N` differs at write time (because another ingest landed first), the implementing agent uses the live `N`, not 23. The implementing agent MUST surface the divergence in the implementation comment if `N != 23`.\n\n**Other diffs (NOT count-dependent):**\n- Frontmatter `page_count: 107 → 108` (literal +1 for the new page; no drift here).\n- Frontmatter `last_updated: 2026-05-06 → 2026-05-09`.\n- Sources table: append row for new page (preserve existing ordering — verify at edit time).\n\n**Rationale (reconcile-to-ground-truth, not literal +1):** marine-eng workflow memory's \"+1-by-convention\" applies to *inflated* phantoms (where literal +1 keeps the agreed offset). Engineering's phantoms are *deflated* — preserving deflation has no upstream-convention rationale and would compound an existing bug.\n\n### 3. Log update\n\n**Path:** `wikis/engineering/wiki/log.md`\n\n**Append (firewall language pinned verbatim from `project_llm_wiki_external_post_ingest_workflow.md`):**\n\n```markdown\n## [2026-05-09] ingest | Anthropic — Claude for Financial Services\n\n- Processed: https://github.com/anthropics/financial-services (Apache-2.0, observed pushed_at re-fetched at write time)\n- Pages created: 1 — sources/2026-05-09-anthropics-financial-services.md\n- Pages updated: 2 — index.md (page_count, source_count reconciled to ground-truth, last_updated, header pluralization, sources table row), log.md (this entry)\n- Notes: Source-only ingest. No raw PDFs, private paths, vendor standards text, project specifications, clauses, tables, formulas, or source archive content copied. Index counter drift (deflated phantoms at plan-draft time: frontmatter source_count 16, header \"13 pages\", actual 23) reconciled to write-time ground-truth as part of this edit. Concept page on managed-agent orchestration deferred per YAGNI.\n```\n\nThe literal phrase **\"No raw PDFs, private paths, vendor standards text, project specifications, clauses, tables, formulas, or source archive content copied\"** is the firewall-language verbatim string. Acceptance criterion checks for byte-equal match against this phrase.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] New source page exists at `wikis/engineering/wiki/sources/2026-05-09-anthropics-financial-services.md` with frontmatter exactly matching the Pattern B block above (no `license`, no `observed_pushed_at` keys; `domain: engineering` present).\n- [ ] All 4 cross-links resolve (verified post-write via `gh api`).\n- [ ] **Counter rule satisfied (write-time-computed):** at write time, `N = count(wikis/engineering/wiki/sources/*.md before staging new page)`. Post-write `index.md` frontmatter must have `source_count: N+1`. Post-write `index.md` header must read `## Sources (N+1 pages)`. The implementation comment must record the observed `N` and resolved `N+1`. **Static value `24` is a baseline expectation only; the rule is the criterion.**\n- [ ] `index.md` frontmatter: `page_count: 108` (literal +1, count-independent), `last_updated: 2026-05-09`.\n- [ ] `index.md` sources table contains a row for the new page; row ordering preserves the existing convention.\n- [ ] `log.md` ingest block appended; the literal phrase \"No raw PDFs, private paths, vendor standards text, project specifications, clauses, tables, formulas, or source archive content copied\" appears byte-equal in the Notes line.\n- [ ] Single commit inside `llm-wiki/.git`, explicit-paths `git add` listing the three paths only (no `-A` / `.`), HEREDOC commit message, no `--amend`, no `--no-verify`.\n- [ ] Pre-commit hooks pass naturally (no bypass).\n- [ ] No workspace-hub issue refs in the commit body (firewall).\n- [ ] Push deferred until separately authorized in a follow-up.\n\n## Hermes Cleanup Mitigation (operationally specific)\n\n**Detection trigger** at implement time:\n```bash\npgrep -af 'git (rebase|stash push|commit|merge|reset|checkout)' | grep -v \"$$\"\n```\nIf any output AND any process under `pgrep -af hermes` is also active, treat as cleanup-window risk.\n\n**Branching behavior:**\n- **Default path (Hermes idle):** commit on `main` of `vamseeachanta/llm-wiki`.\n- **Fallback path (Hermes active at implement time):** commit on a short-lived branch `ingest/2026-05-09-anthropics-financial-services` inside the `llm-wiki/.git` worktree. Do NOT push. Surface the branch name to the user; merge decision is theirs.\n\n**Approval-gate behavior unchanged:** the `status:plan-approved` label + `.planning/plan-approved/2659.md` marker are still required *before* any wiki edit, regardless of which branch the commit lands on.\n\n**Push policy:** push deferred in both paths.\n\n## Risks\n\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Cross-link target rename between exploration and write | Re-verify with `gh api contents` at edit time; abort write if any of 4 targets missing |\n| Index sources-table ordering convention misread | Read full table at edit time; preserve existing order discipline |\n| Pre-existing counter drift compounds into more drift | **Resolved revision-2 + revision-3** — write-time-computed rule, not frozen integer |\n| **Source-count drift between plan-draft and implementation** (e.g., another ingest lands first) | **Resolved revision-3** — counter rule is a write-time computation; baseline `24` is informational only |\n| Pre-commit hook flags Apache-2.0 attribution as license-discipline issue | Synthesize methodology only; cite repo URL + license in body prose; no verbatim README copy. Apache-2.0 is permissive — flag is unlikely. |\n| Parallel session lands on `engineering/wiki/index.md` between read and write | Re-read inside same edit transaction |\n| Hermes cleanup loop reverts the commit | **Resolved revision-2** — explicit detection trigger + branch fallback (see Hermes Cleanup Mitigation section) |\n| Slug convention deviation from peer date-first pattern | **Resolved revision-2** — slug now date-first per Pattern B precedent |\n| Frontmatter schema deviation from peers | **Resolved revision-2** — frontmatter now verbatim Pattern B; no invented `license` / `observed_pushed_at` keys |\n| Firewall-language verbatim claim unverifiable | **Resolved revision-2** — exact phrase pinned in plan; acceptance criterion checks byte-equal match |\n| Anthropic repo `pushed_at` value drifts between exploration and write | **Resolved revision-2** — re-fetch via `gh api` at write time; cite the value observed at write time, not at plan-draft time |\n\n## Adversarial Review Status\n\n**T1-scoped single-provider review** per `feedback_always_adversarial_review_scale_depth.md` (review depth scales to scope, presence is non-negotiable).\n\n- **Round 1 (2026-05-09):** Codex CLI 0.130.0 → MAJOR, 7 findings, 5 blockers. Artifact: `scripts/review/results/2026-05-09-plan-2659-codex.md`.\n- **Round 2 (2026-05-09):** Codex CLI 0.130.0 → MAJOR, 7 findings RESOLVED + 1 new defect (stale-count). Artifact: `scripts/review/results/2026-05-09-plan-2659-codex-r2.md`.\n- **Round 3 (this revision):** dispatching after revision-3 lands.\n\n## Approval Gate\n\nThis issue is at `status:plan-review`. Per `issue-planning-mode` skill and CLAUDE.md planning-workflow rule, the implementing agent must NOT proceed until the user applies `status:plan-approved`.\n\n**Approval will not be requested until Codex round-3 returns APPROVE or MINOR.**\n\nTo approve (after round-3 settles):\n```bash\ngh issue edit 2659 --repo vamseeachanta/workspace-hub \\\n  --remove-label \"status:plan-review\" --add-label \"status:plan-approved\"\nmkdir -p .planning/plan-approved && echo \"Approved by: $(git config user.name)\" > .planning/plan-approved/2659.md\n```\n\nI will NOT self-approve, will NOT pre-authorize downstream agents in any handoff prompt, and will WAIT until both signals are present (label + marker) before any wiki edit.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2659","display_title":"Wiki ingest: anthropics/financial-services → llm-wiki engineering/sources"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2424","title":"chore(ci-health): cross-repo CI audit — 6 of 7 ecosystem repos have red main CI","issue_number":2424,"body":"# Summary\n\nCross-repo CI health audit conducted 2026-04-21. **6 of 7 visible ecosystem repos have failing main-branch CI.** Only `assetutilities` is green. This blocks safe Dependabot merges in `worldenergydata` (5 PRs queued) and represents a systemic hygiene debt that should be triaged per-repo before further dependency or feature work lands.\n\n## Evidence\n\n| Repo | Main CI state | First observed red | Likely scope |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `workspace-hub` | ❌ FAILURE | Recent commits incl. 2026-04-21 `fix(email-routing): skylineseven` and `docs(handoffs):` | Investigate — could be pre-existing or new |\n| `worldenergydata` | ❌ FAILURE (Nightly + Full Test Suite) | 2026-04-21 | Also blocks 5 Dependabot PRs: #329/#330/#331/#332/#333 |\n| `digitalmodel` | ❌ FAILURE | 2026-04-17 | ~4+ days red |\n| `assethold` | ❌ FAILURE | 2026-04-17 | ~4+ days red |\n| `achantas-data` | ❌ FAILURE | 2025-10-05 (!) | **6+ months red** — infrequently maintained |\n| `aceengineer-admin` | — | No CI configured | Consider whether CI is needed |\n| `assetutilities` | ✅ SUCCESS | — | Reference for pattern comparison |\n\nScan command used:\n```bash\nfor repo in vamseeachanta/workspace-hub vamseeachanta/worldenergydata vamseeachanta/digitalmodel vamseeachanta/assethold vamseeachanta/achantas-data vamseeachanta/aceengineer-admin vamseeachanta/assetutilities; do\n  gh run list --repo \"$repo\" --limit 3 --json conclusion,displayTitle,createdAt,headBranch\ndone\n```\n\n## Why this matters\n\n1. **Dependabot PRs stuck** — `worldenergydata` has 5 PRs (labeled `dependencies` + `python` in the 2026-04-20 session) that cannot be safely merged while test workflows are red. Each day the PRs sit, they drift further from the current main state and accrue merge-conflict risk.\n2. **Feature work risk** — any new feature landing on a red-main repo compounds debugging difficulty (is the new failure from the feature or from the pre-existing breakage?).\n3. **Silent regression amplification** — with `achantas-data` red for 6+ months, nothing would catch a new failure layered on top.\n4. **Ecosystem-level trust** — a 6-of-7 red rate undermines the signal value of CI green as a proxy for \"safe to merge.\"\n\n## Out of scope here\n\n- Actual CI repair. Each repo has its own failure signature and needs individual triage.\n- Dependabot merge decisions in `worldenergydata` (deferred to post-CI-repair session per #2017 plan session decision #4).\n- Whether to enable CI on `aceengineer-admin`.\n\n## Proposed approach\n\nRoute as separate per-repo issues (this is the meta-tracker):\n\n- [ ] `worldenergydata` — repair PR-level test workflow; re-run 5 open Dependabot PRs; merge or close with evidence\n- [ ] `workspace-hub` — diagnose recent failures; determine if they predate my 2026-04-20/21 commits\n- [ ] `digitalmodel` — diagnose 2026-04-17 regression\n- [ ] `assethold` — diagnose 2026-04-17 regression\n- [ ] `achantas-data` — decide: repair CI or decommission the workflow; 6 months red means the signal is effectively gone\n- [ ] `aceengineer-admin` — decide whether CI is warranted given repo's activity pattern\n- [ ] Once green: set up a cross-repo CI health dashboard (daily-readiness cron extension?)\n\n## Priority\n\nMEDIUM. Not blocking critical work, but gating the Dependabot backlog in `worldenergydata` and silently eroding the value of CI across the ecosystem. Worth tackling before the next wave of dependency or feature work.\n\n## Links\n\n- Session context: `docs/plans/2026-04-20-inbox-drive-triage-session-design.md` (commit `9ce25ab30`)\n- Related plan: #2017 email-as-queue (v7 at `9aefc66be`)\n- Stuck Dependabot PRs: vamseeachanta/worldenergydata#329, #330, #331, #332, #333\n- Daily-readiness cron reference: `trig_019GWtRosbZ9rw1HxrGpsvy9`\n\nGenerated from 2026-04-21 session decision #4 — \"defer Dependabot merges until ecosystem CI health is addressed.\"\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2424","display_title":"chore(ci-health): cross-repo CI audit — 6 of 7 ecosystem repos have red main CI"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/598","title":"feat(naval-architecture): SIROCCO current-heading/rudder force component chart set","issue_number":598,"body":"## Summary\nBuild a B1528 SIROCCO/Sorocco current-force component sweep that varies:\n\n- current speed: `1, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3, 3.5, 4, 4.5 kn`\n- current/ship heading offset: `-10..+10 deg` in `1 deg` steps\n- rudder angle: `-10..+10 deg` in `1 deg` steps\n\nGenerate interactive charts with a current-speed dropdown (default requested: `4.56 kn`) and display the force components in two interpretation-focused chart panels.\n\n## Motivation\nThe existing B1528 moored-current report is fixed to a `3.5 kn` aligned-current case and rudder angles `+/-1..+/-5 deg`. Engineering review now needs a broader parametric chart set to inspect how current speed, heading offset, and rudder command combine into surge/sway/yaw loads.\n\n## Scope\n- Extend or add a B1528 SIROCCO current-force sweep workflow under `digitalmodel.naval_architecture`.\n- Preserve existing fixed-case report behavior and traceability.\n- Emit CSV/JSON/provenance/manifest artifacts for the full grid.\n- Emit an interactive HTML chart set with current-speed dropdown.\n- Treat `4.56 kn` default carefully because it is not in the requested discrete speed list; either include it as an additional computed speed or document interpolation/default behavior explicitly in the plan and implementation.\n\n## Deliverables\n- Plan: `docs/plans/2026-05-08-issue-<ISSUE>-sirocco-current-heading-rudder-force-components.md`\n- Packaged YAML/input contract for the sweep.\n- Calculation API and report writer.\n- Tests for grid size, sign conventions, formulas, dropdown default, artifacts, provenance, and package exports.\n- Concept chart layout in the plan before implementation.\n\n## Related existing work\n- Existing fixed report generator: `src/digitalmodel/naval_architecture/b1528_sirocco_moored_current_report.py`\n- Existing fixed input YAML: `src/digitalmodel/naval_architecture/data/b1528_sirocco_moored_current.yml`\n- Existing fixed report doc: `docs/domains/marine-engineering/b1528-sirocco-moored-current-report.md`\n- Existing yaw-moment workflow: `src/digitalmodel/naval_architecture/b1528_sirocco_yaw_report.py`\n- Existing rudder/yaw helpers: `src/digitalmodel/naval_architecture/yaw_moment.py`, `maneuverability.py`\n\n## Workflow gate\nThis is engineering-calculation work. Follow the mandatory issue workflow: resource intelligence -> plan -> adversarial review -> `status:plan-review` -> user approval -> `status:plan-approved` -> TDD implementation -> cross-review -> close.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/598","display_title":"feat(naval-architecture): SIROCCO current-heading/rudder force component chart set"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2129","title":"chore(harness): automate issue-state drift and redundancy audit across GitHub + analysis artifacts","issue_number":2129,"body":"## Problem\nThe ecosystem now has strong issue generation and analysis output, but weak automatic hygiene for stale or redundant issue state.\n\nEvidence from the last ~24h review:\n- multiple analysis artifacts recommended actions that were already completed hours later (`#2050`, `#2053`, `#2020`, `#2027`, `#2062`, `#2056`)\n- at least one result artifact contained a genuine false negative on repo paths (`session-start-routine` claim in `docs/plans/claude-ops-2026-04-10-052749/results/1839.md`)\n- recent weekly-review scope is already heavily ticketed, but current automation focuses on queue staleness/counts rather than issue-state drift or redundancy\n- current tooling (`scripts/refresh-agent-work-queue.py`, `scripts/knowledge/review-open-issues.py`) does not flag:\n  - duplicate / near-duplicate issues\n  - close-ready issues whose work already landed in sibling repos\n  - stale generated analysis artifacts\n  - fragmented parent/child issue clusters needing merge/closure/refinement\n\n## Why this matters\nAgent throughput is increasing faster than issue hygiene. Without automatic drift detection, the ecosystem accumulates stale advice, redundant tickets, and misleading open-state backlog.\n\n## Desired outcome\nCreate an automated audit that detects issue-state drift and redundancy across GitHub + local generated artifacts.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Tool flags open issues that are likely already implemented or close-ready\n- [ ] Tool flags duplicate / near-duplicate issues in the recent queue\n- [ ] Tool flags generated analysis artifacts whose recommended next action is stale\n- [ ] Tool emits machine-readable output plus a concise operator report with `close / merge / refine / refresh` recommendations\n- [ ] Weekly-review flow references this hygiene audit instead of creating more overlapping tickets\n\n## Suggested implementation directions\n- extend `scripts/knowledge/review-open-issues.py` beyond label grouping\n- compare GitHub issue state with recent commits and generated results docs\n- add freshness markers / invalidation logic for analysis artifacts\n- support parent/child cluster scoring for over-fragmentation\n\n## Notes\nThis is intentionally about root-cause hygiene automation, not adding more weekly-review feature tickets.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2129","display_title":"chore(harness): automate issue-state drift and redundancy audit across GitHub + analysis artifacts"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/394","title":"chore(repo-structure): normalize worldenergydata folder/file structure","issue_number":394,"body":"## Objective\n\nPlan a conservative, repo-by-repo folder/file structure normalization for `worldenergydata` under the workspace-hub tier-1 ecosystem workflow.\n\n## Scope\n\nPlanning only until explicit user approval. The plan must inventory current structure, identify root clutter/generated-output risk, propose a bounded Phase 1 refactor contract, define TDD/verification gates, and preserve evidence needed for rollback.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- Canonical plan artifact under `docs/plans/`.\n- Review artifact under `scripts/review/results/`.\n- Final issue comment linking the plan and review artifact.\n\n## Approval Gate\n\nNo file moves, package reorganization, deletion, or cleanup execution is authorized until this issue is explicitly approved after plan review.\n\nParent ecosystem anchors: `workspace-hub#1962`, `workspace-hub#2397`.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/394","display_title":"chore(repo-structure): normalize worldenergydata folder/file structure"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/assetutilities/issues/78","title":"chore(repo-structure): normalize assetutilities folder/file structure","issue_number":78,"body":"## Objective\n\nPlan a conservative, repo-by-repo folder/file structure normalization for `assetutilities` under the workspace-hub tier-1 ecosystem workflow.\n\n## Scope\n\nPlanning only until explicit user approval. The plan must inventory current structure, identify root clutter/generated-output risk, propose a bounded Phase 1 refactor contract, define TDD/verification gates, and preserve evidence needed for rollback.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- Canonical plan artifact under `docs/plans/`.\n- Review artifact under `scripts/review/results/`.\n- Final issue comment linking the plan and review artifact.\n\n## Approval Gate\n\nNo file moves, package reorganization, deletion, or cleanup execution is authorized until this issue is explicitly approved after plan review.\n\nParent ecosystem anchors: `workspace-hub#1962`, `workspace-hub#2397`.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/assetutilities/issues/78","display_title":"chore(repo-structure): normalize assetutilities folder/file structure"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2655","title":"chore(provider-session): route Codex nested-repo path drift to owning tier-1 repos","issue_number":2655,"body":"## Summary\n\nPlan a bounded remediation for Codex provider-session `nested_repo_context_drift`: Codex logs are still reading tier-1 sibling/nested repository paths as if they were missing `workspace-hub` files. The next stream should make this routing explicit in provider-session prompts/docs/tests so Codex re-resolves the owning repo root before treating those reads as workspace-hub stale-path debt.\n\n## Evidence\n\nLatest provider-session audit (`analysis/provider-session-ecosystem-audit.json`, generated `2026-05-07T09:37:54Z`) reports for Codex:\n\n- `missing_repo_reads`: `730`\n- remediation hint `nested_repo_context_drift`: `61`\n- top matched examples:\n  - `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/calibration_schema.py` — 9\n  - `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/public_dataset.py` — 8\n  - `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/__init__.py` — 8\n  - `docs/plans/2026-04-21-issue-334-annual-operator-disclosures-dataset.md` — 8\n  - `src/assethold/signals/watchlist.py` — 7\n  - `sitemap.xml` — 7\n  - `src/worldenergydata/cost/calibration/cost_predictor.py` — 7\n  - `tests/unit/cost/test_proxy_comparison.py` — 7\n\nCanonical guidance already exists in `docs/ops/legacy-claude-reference-map.md` under “Session-local worktree and nested repo path drift”: examples map `src/worldenergydata/*` → `worldenergydata/src/worldenergydata/*`, `tests/unit/cost/*` → `worldenergydata/tests/unit/cost/*`, `src/assethold/*` → `assethold/src/assethold/*`, and `sitemap.xml` → `aceengineer-website/sitemap.xml`.\n\n## Scope\n\nPlanning only until approved:\n\n- Add an approval-gated plan artifact under `docs/plans/`.\n- Define Codex-facing routing/remediation requirements for nested tier-1 repo paths.\n- Require tests or fixtures proving nested repo path examples are classified/routed without being recreated in `workspace-hub`.\n- Link this issue to the provider-session audit and prior classification work (#2333) without duplicating already-closed non-repo-artifact classification work.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Recreating any missing `src/worldenergydata/*`, `src/assethold/*`, or website files under `workspace-hub`.\n- Changing sibling repositories before this plan is reviewed and approved.\n- LLM-wiki spinout cleanup; that is already covered by #2650 unless a narrower approved sub-plan is later required.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Canonical issue plan exists in `docs/plans/` and cites audit evidence, related issues, and redirect guidance.\n- [ ] Plan includes TDD fixtures for Codex nested repo path routing.\n- [ ] Plan distinguishes actionable workspace-hub stale-path debt from sibling repo routing drift.\n- [ ] Plan receives review and is moved to `status:plan-review` for user approval.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2655","display_title":"chore(provider-session): route Codex nested-repo path drift to owning tier-1 repos"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/326","title":"Bug: self.prepare_production_data called but never defined on ProductionAnalyzer (bsee/analysis/production_api10.py)","issue_number":326,"body":"## Context\n\nDiscovered during PR #319 (Block B scope-bugs agent) while restoring the missing `df_temp` / `completion_name` loop in `src/worldenergydata/bsee/analysis/production_api10.py`.\n\n## The bug\n\nIn the `router()` method (or adjacent), `self.prepare_production_data(...)` is called, but the class does not define a `prepare_production_data` method. flake8 does not catch this because F821 does not flag attribute access on `self`/object references — only bare identifiers.\n\n## Why it wasn't fixed in #319\n\n#319's charter was strictly \\\"fix F821 lint errors\\\" — not broader refactoring. The scope-bugs agent flagged this latent bug in the PR body for future work rather than silently fixing it in the same PR.\n\n## Investigation path\n\n1. Read the full `ProductionAnalyzer` class in `src/worldenergydata/bsee/analysis/production_api10.py` to understand the calling context.\n2. Check git history for `prepare_production_data`:\n   \\`\\`\\`bash\n   git log -p --all -S \\\"def prepare_production_data\\\" src/worldenergydata/bsee/analysis/\n   \\`\\`\\`\n3. Check sibling modules — the legacy reference `src/worldenergydata/common/legacy/ong_fd_components.py` has a similar pattern and might contain the intended implementation.\n4. Check whether any test (including the skipped `test_analysis_modules_basic` in `tests/unit/test_achievable_coverage.py:147`) exercises the `router()` method at runtime — if no code path reaches this call site, it's dead code and can be deleted. Otherwise, the method must be implemented or the call must be rewritten.\n\n## Priority\n\nLow-medium. The code path may not be exercised (no tests cover the `router()` method's call to `prepare_production_data` — the skip at test_achievable_coverage.py:147 suggests so). But if ever exercised, it will raise `AttributeError`. Worth a proper fix or a delete.\n\n## Refs\n\nFlagged in #319 PR body. Related: the 10 xfailed bugs in [<umbrella issue URL will be added>] are a separate concern.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/326","display_title":"Bug: self.prepare_production_data called but never defined on ProductionAnalyzer (bsee/analysis/production_api10.py)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/555","title":"fix(marine_ops): _generate_chain_database diameters[:20] excludes 76mm — clears 4 of 77 marine_ops failures","issue_number":555,"body":"Sub-issue of [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) cluster R4. 1-line test/fixture bug, ~4 test failures.\n\n## Symptom\n\n4 failing tests in `tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/legacy/test_component_database.py`:\n\n- `test_get_chain_by_specification`\n- `test_find_chain_with_safety_factor`\n- `test_database_performance`\n- `test_weight_scales_with_diameter_squared`\n\nTop error:\n```\ntests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/legacy/test_component_database.py:589: in test_get_chain_by_specification\n    chain = database.get_chain(...)\ntests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/legacy/test_component_database.py:328: in get_chain\n    raise ValueError(\nE   ValueError: Chain not found: 76mm R4 Stud Link\n```\n\n## Root cause\n\n`tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/legacy/test_component_database.py:236` (verified at HEAD `08e9c33`):\n\n```python\nfor diameter in diameters[:20]:  # Generate 60 components\n```\n\nThe `diameters` array is declared on lines 228–231:\n```\n[16, 19, 22, 24, 28, 32, 34, 36, 38, 40, 44, 46, 48, 50, 52,\n 56, 58, 60, 62, 64, 66, 68, 70, 76, 78, 81, 84, 87, 90, 92, ...]\n```\n\n76mm sits at index 23, so `diameters[:20]` truncates at 64mm and the four tests querying `76mm R4 Stud Link` cannot find their fixture.\n\nA second copy of the same bug exists at `tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/test_component_database.py:236` (non-legacy path) — the fix should sweep both unless the divergence is intentional.\n\n## Fix shape\n\n~1–2 LoC + test-data array re-verification. Change `diameters[:20]` to either:\n\n- **(a) The full list**: `for diameter in diameters:` — generates all 49 diameters × 3 grades = 147 components. The existing assertion on line 219 (`Loaded 60 chain components` print, plus any `len(...) == 60` test assertion) will need its expected count updated.\n- **(b) A slice that includes 76mm**: e.g., `diameters[:24]` — generates 24 × 3 = 72 components, again breaking the \"60 components\" expectation.\n\n### Sub-decision (please confirm before implementation)\n\nWas the test author's intent:\n\n1. **\"First 20 standard sizes\"** — then the array order is wrong (76mm should be among the first 20) and the array needs reordering, slice stays `[:20]`.\n2. **\"All standard sizes up to and including 76mm\"** — slice extends to `[:24]` and the `60` row-count expectation is replaced with `72`.\n3. **\"All standard sizes\"** — drop the slice entirely and replace `60` with `147`.\n\nReading the comment `# Generate 60 components` and the `Loaded 60 chain components` print on line 219, intent (1) is the most plausible original design but is incompatible with current test queries for 76mm. Intent (2) or (3) appears to be what the test queries actually require.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- 4 named tests above pass\n- No regression in other `test_component_database.py` tests (legacy + non-legacy paths)\n- The \"Loaded N chain components\" print and any related `len(chain_db)` assertions are updated consistently\n\n## Estimated diff\n\n~1–2 LoC in the slice + 1–2 LoC in the count assertion(s). Test-data array re-verification for ordering if intent (1) is chosen.\n\n## References\n\n- Triage plan: `docs/plans/2026-05-03-2609-marine-ops-triage.md` (R4, lines 166–187)\n- Parent: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609)\n- Predecessor: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580) (closed)\n- Bug locations at HEAD `08e9c33`:\n  - `tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/legacy/test_component_database.py:236`\n  - `tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/test_component_database.py:236`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/555","display_title":"fix(marine_ops): _generate_chain_database diameters[:20] excludes 76mm — clears 4 of 77 marine_ops failures"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/327","title":"Test infra: conftest.py blocks pytest collection of tests/unit/marine_safety/","issue_number":327,"body":"## Context\n\nDiscovered during PR #324 verification (part of #322 mop-up).\n\n## The problem\n\nRunning pytest against any file under `tests/unit/marine_safety/` fails at **collection time** with:\n\n\\`\\`\\`\nImportError while loading conftest '/.../tests/unit/marine_safety/conftest.py'.\n.../tests/unit/marine_safety/conftest.py:16: in <module>\n    from tests.modules.marine_safety.fixtures.sample_data import (\nE   ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tests.modules.marine_safety.fixtures'\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nThe conftest imports fixtures from a path that doesn't exist. Effect: **the entire `tests/unit/marine_safety/` directory is uncollectable**, not just one test — which means any future PR touching marine_safety tests can't verify via pytest.\n\n## How to fix\n\nTwo plausible paths, pick whichever matches project intent:\n\n1. **Restore the missing fixtures module** at `tests/modules/marine_safety/fixtures/sample_data.py`. Check git history:\n   \\`\\`\\`bash\n   git log --all --full-history --oneline -- tests/modules/marine_safety/fixtures/sample_data.py\n   \\`\\`\\`\n   If it existed and was removed, restore it.\n\n2. **Rewrite the conftest** to use an alternate fixture source (inline fixtures, a simpler path, or factories). Update `conftest.py:16` accordingly.\n\nEither fix should be accompanied by a smoke test: `uv run --extra dev pytest tests/unit/marine_safety/ --collect-only` should succeed with non-zero collected items.\n\n## Refs\n\nSurfaced during #324 but pre-existing (not introduced by that PR). Blocks downstream runtime validation of #324's marine_safety work. Low-urgency since #324 verified via flake8 static analysis, but should be fixed before the next marine_safety-adjacent PR.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/327","display_title":"Test infra: conftest.py blocks pytest collection of tests/unit/marine_safety/"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/348","title":"docs(planning): add canonical docs/plans README index for issue-plan tracking","issue_number":348,"body":"## Summary\nAdd a canonical `docs/plans/README.md` index for worldenergydata issue-plan tracking so planning state can be updated consistently during plan drafting, review, approval, and execution.\n\n## Why\nDuring the latest planning wave for issues #342, #343, and #344, the repo had local plan files under `docs/plans/` but no `docs/plans/README.md` index file. That blocks the standard issue-planning workflow from recording plan status in one discoverable place and makes queue audits harder.\n\nThe missing index is now a recurring governance gap because the planning workflow expects:\n- canonical plan artifact paths under `docs/plans/`\n- a discoverable README/index of issue plans and statuses\n- consistent reconciliation between issue labels, local plans, and approval markers\n\n## Scope\n- add `docs/plans/README.md`\n- define the plan index table format used by this repo\n- backfill current known issue-plan entries at least for the active annual-disclosure cost wave (#334, #335, #336, #337, #338, #342, #343, #344)\n- document how statuses should be maintained in the index\n- keep the work docs-only\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- `docs/plans/README.md` exists\n- it includes a canonical issue-plan index table\n- active current plan artifacts are indexed\n- the maintenance rule for keeping the index in sync is documented\n\n## Related\n- #342\n- #343\n- #344\n- #334\n- #336\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/348","display_title":"docs(planning): add canonical docs/plans README index for issue-plan tracking"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/270","title":"Add `lng_terminals_refresh` to scheduler config","issue_number":270,"body":"## Problem\n\n`src/worldenergydata/scheduler/jobs/lng_terminals_refresh.py` exists as a job file but is not listed in `scheduler_config.yml`. The job is never registered or run.\n\n## Fix\n\nAdd an `lng_terminals_refresh` entry to `config/scheduler/scheduler_config.yml` with appropriate interval and settings.\n\n## Context\n\nDiscovered during scheduler health audit (#264). Existing curated data in `data/modules/lng_terminals/` (12 files, last updated 2026-02-16).","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/270","display_title":"Add `lng_terminals_refresh` to scheduler config"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/15","title":"META: resolve standards-routing for maritime-law / lng-projects / acma-projects (post-#2615 deferred Open Questions)","issue_number":15,"body":"## Summary\n\nThree llm-wikis remain OUT OF the `wiki/standards/<code-id>.md` routing principle scope after umbrella sanction vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2615 landed. They need an explicit user decision before further standards-content work proceeds.\n\n## Why\n\nUmbrella sanction vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2615 (Tier A+B+C, closed 2026-05-03 at commit `3467058b3`) formally extended the routing principle from {marine-engineering, engineering, naval-architecture} to also include {engineering-standards, asset-management}. Three wikis were carried as **conditional Open Questions** but did NOT receive separate user assent during W5-D approval:\n\n- **maritime-law** — IMO/ILO conventions are versioned by `consolidated_edition` rather than `revision`; W2-C (#2592, closed) defaulted all 4 IMO/ILO pages to `wiki/concepts/` to avoid presupposing the routing decision\n- **lng-projects** — W5-C (#2612, closed) routed everything to `wiki/concepts/` and `wiki/entities/`; no standards routing\n- **acma-projects** — currently has 1 concept + 1 entity page, untouched by Tier C; W5-D deferred its sanction\n\nWithout a decision, future contributions either (a) invent ad-hoc paths or (b) get blocked at the W3-C governance test (`tests/governance/test_2471_citation_scope.py::test_out_of_principle_wiki_routing_requires_sanction_citation`) which is now the fail-closed enforcement.\n\n## Scope (decision-only — no implementation in this issue)\n\nFor each of the 3 wikis, decide ONE of:\n1. **Sanction** — extend the routing principle to this wiki via a new sanction citation (`Sanctioned-by: #<this-issue>` pattern). Adds it to `IN_PRINCIPLE_WIKIS` in `tests/governance/test_2471_citation_scope.py`.\n2. **Defer indefinitely** — keep concepts-only routing, document the rationale in `~/.claude/projects/-mnt-local-analysis-workspace-hub/memory/project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md`\n3. **Archive/de-scope** — formally retire the wiki (acma-projects is the candidate)\n\n## Per-wiki context\n\n### maritime-law\n- 17 concepts, 0 standards, 10 entities (post-#2592)\n- Standards-content already authored (MARPOL, SOLAS, MLC, ISM Code) — currently in `concepts/` with `consolidated_edition` frontmatter\n- Decision Q: should IMO/ILO conventions get their own routing tier?\n\n### lng-projects\n- 8 concepts, 0 standards, 0 entities (post-#2612)\n- No LNG-specific standards authored yet — but CSA-Z276, NFPA 59A, IGC Code likely candidates\n- Decision Q: route LNG codes to `wiki/standards/`, or keep flat in `concepts/`?\n\n### acma-projects\n- 1 concept, 0 standards, 1 entity — barely populated\n- Per memory `project_aceengineer_copy_canonical_sources.md`: aceengineer-strategy (private repo) is the canonical source for firm copy\n- Decision Q: is this wiki worth investment, or should it be archived in favor of aceengineer-strategy?\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Implementation of the routing changes (separate downstream issue per decision)\n- Standards-page authoring (waits on routing decision)\n- Cross-wiki schema changes (#2363, vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#30 territory)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- One of (1)/(2)/(3) above explicitly chosen for each of the 3 wikis\n- Decisions recorded in `project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md`\n- Per-decision follow-up issue filed for any \"sanction\" outcome (mirroring the vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2615 4-deliverable pattern)\n\n## References\n\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2615 (closed) — umbrella sanction precedent\n- vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13 — llm-wiki strengthening epic\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2471 — original CSA-Z276 routing precedent\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2482 — wiki/sources governance (deny-list)\n- `docs/standards/calc-output-citation.md` — routing/citation contract\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/15","display_title":"META: resolve standards-routing for maritime-law / lng-projects / acma-projects (post-#2615 deferred Open Questions)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2346","title":"feat(gtm): prospect-data customized-demo pipeline — 48hr turnaround + pre-staged vessel templates","issue_number":2346,"body":"## Summary\nThe 30-day GTM plan Week 4 names \"run parametric demo with THEIR data within 48 hours\" as the **highest-conversion sales tool**. No issue tracks this pipeline; no automation, input schema, or pre-staged vessel library exists.\n\n## Evidence\n- `docs/gtm/gtm-plan-30day.md` lines 133-138\n- Risk mitigation: \"Always keep a clean demo environment ready; pre-stage common vessel types\"\n- No issue, no `docs/gtm/intake/`, no template\n\n## Scope\n- Define prospect intake schema (vessel + target structure) — YAML at `docs/gtm/intake/`\n- Pre-stage 3 canonical vessel profiles (pipelay barge, heavy-lift, PLSV)\n- Document SOP: receive → validate → run demo → render branded report → deliver in 48 hr\n- Shared input adapter across the 5 demos\n\n## Acceptance\n- [ ] Intake YAML schema validated with 1 real example\n- [ ] 3 canonical vessel profiles committed\n- [ ] SOP runbook at `docs/gtm/prospect-demo-sop.md`\n- [ ] Dry-run end-to-end <48 hr from dummy intake\n\n## Related\n- #2016, #1800, #1669, 30-day plan Week 4","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2346","display_title":"feat(gtm): prospect-data customized-demo pipeline — 48hr turnaround + pre-staged vessel templates"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2458","title":"feat(canonical-spec): promote named OrcaWave multi-body benchmark fixture for roundtrip and handoff readiness","issue_number":2458,"body":"## Summary\nPromote a named OrcaWave multi-body benchmark/spec fixture into a first-class canonical proof path, and prepare it for future OrcaWave -> OrcaFlex handoff validation.\n\n## Why\nMulti-body cases are strategically important because they exercise body naming, parent/child relationships, fixed DOFs, and connection semantics that are easy to mishandle. The current repo has evidence for multi-body pathways, but not yet a clearly promoted benchmark-grade canonical proof artifact.\n\n## Scope\n- Select a representative multi-body fixture/spec\n- Validate canonical `spec.yml` -> native OrcaWave YAML generation\n- Validate reverse parsing of the native input back into canonical structures\n- Verify preservation of body count, body identity, fixed DOFs, and connection parent relationships\n- Leave the fixture ready for downstream OrcaWave -> OrcaFlex handoff testing\n\n## Deliverables\n- Named multi-body benchmark fixture and regression tests\n- Explicit multi-body preservation checks\n- Documentation note connecting this fixture to future handoff validation work\n\n## Success criteria\n- Multi-body semantics are preserved in a benchmark-backed canonical pathway\n- The fixture becomes a stable bridge candidate for future handoff validation waves\n\n## Parent roadmap\n- Reopened roadmap anchor: #1572\n\n## Related active issues\n- Delivered foundations: #1605, #1592, #1768, #1638\n- Structure readiness source: `docs/roadmaps/orcawave-orcaflex-canonical-spec-contract-roadmap.md`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2458","display_title":"feat(canonical-spec): promote named OrcaWave multi-body benchmark fixture for roundtrip and handoff readiness"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/assetutilities/issues/32","title":"tech debt | environment management | poetry library","issue_number":32,"body":"Package management and Environments can be manged by using pip, conda, poetry, uv etc. The most latest and most complete package, most used package is pointing towards poetry. uv seems be more command based, simple and robust. \n\nWe are not inclined to any method. We will have any scripts with any method in our dev_tools. Utilize the most complete package and get the job done. if uv is even easier, we will pivot now or in future. \n\nUtilize poetry for environment management:\n\n- [x] gitbash to create environment\n- [x] gitbash to update environment\n- [x] Good example for poetry\n  - [x] https://github.com/virattt/ai-hedge-fund/blob/main/pyproject.toml\n\n- [ ] Add basic scripts for uv?\n\nReferences:\n- https://github.com/getsivakumar/20241030-AceEngineer-Consultation\n- https://realpython.com/dependency-management-python-poetry/\n- https://codemaker2016.medium.com/introducing-uv-next-gen-python-package-manager-b78ad39c95d7#8902","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/assetutilities/issues/32","display_title":"tech debt | environment management | poetry library"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/552","title":"fix(aqwa-backend): align CRLF/LF line endings between generate_single and generate_modular","issue_number":552,"body":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during workspace-hub [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614) Cat C fix ([digitalmodel#551](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/551), merged \\`d6a42770\\`).\n\n## Defect\n\n\\`AQWABackend.generate_single()\\` writes CRLF line endings via \\`newline=''\\` semantics; \\`AQWABackend.generate_modular()\\` uses \\`Path.write_text(...)\\` which normalizes to LF. The two paths produce byte-different output.\n\n## Why this is latent (not currently breaking)\n\nThe current test \\`test_modular_concatenation_matches_single\\` strips empty lines and compares stripped text, so the line-ending difference doesn't break the assertion. But any future byte-equality assertion (or downstream tooling that's CRLF-sensitive) will trip on it.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nPick one and align both paths:\n- (a) Both write CRLF (matches \\`generate_single\\` current behavior — likely matches AQWA Workbench export convention)\n- (b) Both write LF (matches \\`generate_modular\\` current behavior — POSIX-friendly)\n\nConfirm canonical choice by checking what AQWA itself emits as native output.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Both \\`generate_single\\` and \\`generate_modular\\` produce byte-identical output for the same inputs (excluding any explicit per-deck filename/header differences)\n- [ ] A new test asserts byte-equality (not just stripped-text equality)\n- [ ] Existing \\`test_modular_concatenation_matches_single\\` continues to pass\n\n## Files likely affected\n- \\`src/digitalmodel/hydrodynamics/diffraction/aqwa_backend.py\\`\n- \\`tests/hydrodynamics/diffraction/test_aqwa_backend.py\\`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/552","display_title":"fix(aqwa-backend): align CRLF/LF line endings between generate_single and generate_modular"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/18","title":"feat(llm-wiki): execute marine-engineering P2 platform-entity and overview refresh backfill","issue_number":18,"body":"## Summary\nCreate a bounded planning issue for the P2 marine-engineering llm-wiki backfill items identified by the W4-C gap audit: stability-in-waves concept coverage, canonical offshore platform/vessel entity pages, and stale overview regeneration.\n\n## Evidence\n- `docs/audits/2026-05-03-marine-engineering-wiki-gap-audit.md` Table G identifies these P2 backfill items:\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/stability-in-waves.md`\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/fpso.md`\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/flng.md`\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/semisubmersible.md`\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/spar.md`\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/jack-up.md`\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/tlp.md`\n  - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/jacket.md`\n  - regenerate stale `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/overview.md`\n- Live file check on 2026-05-04 showed the P2 concept/entity targets above are missing.\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/overview.md` currently reports stale post-seed-migration counts (`Source documents: 5`, `Total wiki pages: 20`) while the audit/live inventory records roughly `wiki=19,199`, `sources=19,166`, `concepts=14`, `entities=15`.\n\n## Duplicate check\nFocused live issue searches found related but non-duplicate work:\n- `#2601` is the closed W4-C audit issue that produced Table G, not the execution issue for these P2 pages.\n- `#1999` bootstrapped the original marine-engineering wiki, but current overview and entity gaps remain.\n- `#2612` covered LNG-projects topical expansion, not marine-engineering platform entities.\n- `#2372`, `#2378`, and `#2392` cover aliasing, index pagination, and gap detection respectively; they do not own these curated P2 pages.\n\n## Scope boundary\nIn scope after planning approval:\n- Plan and then implement the P2 concept/entity pages listed above.\n- Refresh `overview.md` so it no longer claims the obsolete 20-page seed state.\n- Update required wiki index/log/cross-link surfaces for the new curated pages.\n\nOut of scope:\n- P1 standards-directory and station-keeping/motions concept work; that should be tracked separately.\n- Broad raw data ingestion or copying from `/mnt/ace`.\n- Full reclassification of all 19k source pages.\n- No plan approval marker creation; this must stop at normal plan-review until user approval.\n\n## Acceptance criteria candidate\n- A plan will name exact files, source anchors, validation checks, and update surfaces before implementation.\n- P2 curated pages will exist only after plan approval and will include frontmatter, summary prose, source references, and cross-links.\n- `overview.md` will reflect current corpus scale and point readers to curated concept/entity coverage rather than obsolete seed counts.\n\n## Related\n- Roadmap umbrella: `#2390`\n- Source audit: `#2601`\n- Gap detector: `#2392`\n- Source-title aliasing: `#2372`\n- Marine index chunking: `#2378`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/18","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): execute marine-engineering P2 platform-entity and overview refresh backfill"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/16","title":"feat(llm-wiki): backfill engineering classification-society entity pages (DNV/ABS/LR/BV)","issue_number":16,"body":"## Summary\nCreate a bounded planning issue to backfill first-class `engineering` wiki entity pages for major classification societies surfaced by the engineering wiki gap audit: DNV, ABS, Lloyd's Register (LR), and Bureau Veritas (BV).\n\n## Evidence\n- `docs/audits/2026-05-02-engineering-wiki-gap-audit.md` Table C identifies classification-society entity pages (`DNV`, `ABS`, `LR`, `BV`) as the new gap beyond the Tier B engineering standards issues.\n- The same audit states that Table C items 1, 2, 3, 6, and 7 already overlap with Tier B issues `#2590`, `#2586`, `#2591`, and `#2595`; this issue intentionally targets only the remaining entity-page gap.\n- Live file check on 2026-05-04 showed these pages are missing:\n  - `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/dnv.md`\n  - `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/abs.md`\n  - `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/lr.md`\n  - `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/bv.md`\n\n## Duplicate check\nFocused live issue searches found related but non-duplicate work:\n- `#2588` is the closed audit issue that surfaced the gap, not the execution issue.\n- `#2589` covered naval-architecture topical expansion and mentioned IACS/class societies, but it did not create the engineering entity pages above.\n- `#2264` / `#2263` were ACMA metadata-only standards sweeps, not engineering entity-page backfill.\n- `#186` tracks IACS Blue Book auto-fetch skill updates, not llm-wiki entity pages.\n\n## Scope boundary\nIn scope after planning approval:\n- Create concise, source-traceable engineering entity pages for DNV, ABS, LR, and BV.\n- Cross-link the pages to existing engineering / engineering-standards wiki pages where appropriate.\n- Update local wiki index/log/cross-link surfaces required by repository conventions.\n\nOut of scope:\n- No standards text copying into git.\n- No broad raw-data promotion from `/mnt/ace`.\n- No new standards summaries beyond minimal references needed for the entity pages.\n- No approval marker creation; this must follow the normal plan-review and user-approval gate.\n\n## Acceptance criteria candidate\n- A plan will define exact files, tests/checks, and source-traceability requirements before implementation.\n- The four entity pages will exist only after plan approval and will have frontmatter, summaries, source references, and cross-links.\n- Existing Tier B standards issues will remain the owner for standards-summary pages; this issue owns only society/entity pages.\n\n## Related\n- Roadmap umbrella: `#2390`\n- Source audit: `#2588`\n- Related topical expansion: `#2589`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/16","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): backfill engineering classification-society entity pages (DNV/ABS/LR/BV)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/17","title":"feat(llm-wiki): execute marine-engineering P1 standards/concept backfill from W4-C audit","issue_number":17,"body":"## Summary\nCreate a bounded planning issue for the P1 marine-engineering llm-wiki backfill items identified by the W4-C gap audit: standards-directory bootstrap, motions/RAO concept coverage, and station-keeping concept coverage.\n\n## Evidence\n- `docs/audits/2026-05-03-marine-engineering-wiki-gap-audit.md` Table G identifies these P1 backfill items:\n  1. Create `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/standards/` and seed `TEMPLATE.md`.\n  2. Backfill `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/motions-rao.md` anchored to ITTC `7.5-02-07-02`.\n  3. Backfill station-keeping concept set:\n     - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/station-keeping.md`\n     - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/dynamic-positioning.md`\n     - `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/spread-mooring.md`\n- Live corpus inventory showed `marine-engineering` has `wiki=19,199`, `sources=19,166`, and `stubish=19,148`, so raw/source-page volume substantially exceeds curated concept coverage.\n- Live file check on 2026-05-04 showed all P1 target files/directories above are missing.\n\n## Duplicate check\nFocused live issue searches found related but non-duplicate work:\n- `#2601` is the closed W4-C audit issue that produced Table G, not the execution issue for these P1 pages.\n- `#2613` / `#2615` sanctioned standards routing, but did not create the marine-engineering standards directory or the P1 concept pages.\n- `#2372` addresses source-title aliasing; `#2378` addresses index chunking/pagination; `#2392` addresses gap detection. None own the curated P1 backfill pages.\n- Existing standards-summary issues such as `#2586`, `#2590`, `#2595`, `#2599`, and `#2611` target engineering-standards pages, not marine-engineering concept coverage.\n\n## Scope boundary\nIn scope after planning approval:\n- Plan and then implement the standards-directory bootstrap and P1 concept pages listed above.\n- Use source summaries / metadata only; preserve raw-data boundary and do not copy raw standards or papers into git.\n- Update required wiki index/log/cross-link surfaces for the new curated pages.\n\nOut of scope:\n- P2 entity-platform pages and stale overview regeneration; those should be tracked separately to keep this issue approval-bounded.\n- Broad reclassification of the 19k source-page layer; that remains related to `#1926`, `#2372`, `#2378`, and `#2392`.\n- No plan approval marker creation; this must stop at normal plan-review until user approval.\n\n## Acceptance criteria candidate\n- A plan will name exact files, source anchors, validation checks, and update surfaces before implementation.\n- P1 curated pages will exist only after plan approval and will include frontmatter, summary prose, source references, and cross-links.\n- `marine-engineering/CLAUDE.md` standards-directory convention will match the actual directory structure after implementation.\n\n## Related\n- Roadmap umbrella: `#2390`\n- Source audit: `#2601`\n- Gap detector: `#2392`\n- Source-title aliasing: `#2372`\n- Marine index chunking: `#2378`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/17","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): execute marine-engineering P1 standards/concept backfill from W4-C audit"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/37","title":"feat(knowledge): add llm-wiki strengthening scorecard and prioritized action queue","issue_number":37,"body":"## Summary\nAdd an LLM-wiki strengthening scorecard that ranks wiki domains and source families by expected leverage, so future wiki work is driven by measurable gaps rather than ad hoc intuition.\n\n## Why\nThe repo review surfaced a measurable imbalance and sparse graph glue:\n- wiki page counts are highly skewed (engineering 83, naval-architecture 45, maritime-law 22 vs marine-engineering 19,184)\n- `knowledge/wikis/cross-links.md` currently contains only about 25 table rows and there is no `cross-links.jsonl` present\n- `docs/reports/llm-wiki-external-source-priority-queue.md` already contains a prioritization model, but it is static and not wired into ongoing repo decisions\n- `scripts/knowledge/wiki_health_cron.py` and the broader lint/health tooling focus on correctness checks, not portfolio-strengthening prioritization\n\nA scorecard would let the repo continuously answer: which domain is thinnest, which source family is cheapest to promote next, and where cross-link or provenance improvements will produce the biggest compounding return.\n\n## Scope\n- Define a strengthening scorecard for each wiki domain using signals such as page count, source-family coverage, cross-link density, orphan/empty-page counts, and provenance/doc_key readiness.\n- Ingest existing artifacts rather than re-scanning everything: priority queue, staged batch packs, health/lint outputs, accessibility registry, and wiki indexes.\n- Emit a ranked queue of recommended next strengthening actions.\n- Separate portfolio signals from implementation-specific lint failures.\n\n## Deliverables\n- Scorecard schema and report format.\n- Initial ranking across engineering, marine-engineering, maritime-law, naval-architecture, and personal wikis.\n- Recommended next-action queue tied to source families or concrete issues.\n- Guidance for folding the scorecard into weekly/periodic intelligence review without duplicating existing health cron output.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] every wiki domain gets a comparable strengthening score/profile\n- [ ] scorecard consumes existing repo artifacts rather than bespoke manual counting\n- [ ] output includes a prioritized next-action queue, not just diagnostics\n- [ ] cross-link sparsity and source-family readiness are both represented\n- [ ] scorecard clearly distinguishes structural weakness from simple scale differences\n\n## Related\n- Unified review: `docs/reports/2026-04-16-llm-wiki-resource-intelligence-unified-review.md`\n- Priority queue: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2242\n- Staged batch packs: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2243\n- Cross-link infrastructure: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#36, vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#35, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2068\n- Wiki health automation precedent: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2002 / `scripts/knowledge/wiki_health_cron.py`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/37","display_title":"feat(knowledge): add llm-wiki strengthening scorecard and prioritized action queue"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/22","title":"feat(knowledge): generate faceted portal pages for large LLM-wiki domains","issue_number":22,"body":"## Summary\nGenerate faceted portal pages for large LLM-wiki domains so humans and agents can navigate them through curated entry points instead of only giant raw `index.md` tables.\n\n## Why\nParallel repo review found that large wiki domains are technically linked but still weakly usable:\n- `docs/document-intelligence/intelligence-accessibility-map.md` explicitly says the marine-engineering wiki has \"19K pages with no curated entry beyond the index\".\n- Live repo state shows `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/index.md` is 21,605 lines.\n- `docs/document-intelligence/README.md` currently points humans at each domain's `wiki/index.md`, but there is no faceted or task-oriented portal for large domains.\n\nThis means the wiki exists, but the highest-value domain is still hard to enter efficiently.\n\n## Scope\n- Start with the largest domain: `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/`.\n- Define generated portal pages that facet content by page type, standards, tools/solvers, subdomain, and top-entry pages.\n- Link the portal from both the domain `wiki/index.md` and `docs/document-intelligence/README.md`.\n- Keep portal generation reproducible/scripted so it does not become a hand-maintained drift surface.\n\n## Deliverables\n- Faceted portal design for large LLM-wikis.\n- First generated portal for marine-engineering.\n- Extend-vs-create guidance for future large domains.\n- Navigation links from the current docs entry points into the portal.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] marine-engineering gets a generated portal page with curated facets and top-entry links\n- [ ] portal is linked from the current wiki/domain navigation surfaces\n- [ ] portal generation is reproducible rather than hand-edited\n- [ ] portal improves human navigation without replacing canonical index pages\n\n## Related\n- Discoverability/accessibility map: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2096\n- Canonical intelligence entry points: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2137\n- Cross-link infrastructure: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#36, vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#35, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2068\n- Strengthening scorecard: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#37\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/llm-wiki/issues/22","display_title":"feat(knowledge): generate faceted portal pages for large LLM-wiki domains"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/273","title":"WRK-666: feat(workspace-hub): WhatsApp → WRK capture pipeline — monitor number/channel and auto-create work items","issue_number":273,"body":"## WRK-666: feat(workspace-hub): WhatsApp → WRK capture pipeline — monitor number/channel and auto-create work items\n\n\n\n**Status:** Stage 17: Reclaim (n) | **Priority:** high | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** pipeline\n\n**Repo:** ['workspace-hub'] | **Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** dev-primary\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n### What\n\nBuild a lightweight listener that monitors a designated WhatsApp number or group\nchannel and converts incoming messages into WRK items in the repo ecosystem —\nthe same way `/work add <desc>` does from the CLI.\n\n### Architecture\n\n```\nWhatsApp (phone/group) → OpenClaw Narada agent → capture script → WRK-NNN.md\n                                                       ↓\n                                               git commit + push\n                                                       ↓\n                                           INDEX.md regenerated\n```\n\nThe Narada agent (WRK-665) acts as the listener. When a message arrives on the\nconfigured number/channel it is passed to a capture handler that:\n1. Classifies the message as a work item (vs. general chat)\n2. Extracts title, repo target, and priority hints from the text\n3. Calls `scripts/work-queue/next-id.sh` to get the next WRK-NNN\n4. Writes the WRK file, commits, and pushes\n\n### Monitored sources\n\n- **Self number**: messages sent to own WhatsApp number (voice-note transcription supported)\n- **Dedicated group**: a \"Work Capture\" WhatsApp group where any member can drop tasks\n\n### Message format (loose — Narada normalises)\n\n```\nPlain text:   \"Add auth module to aceengineer-website\"\nStructured:   \"WRK: Fix login redirect | repo: aceengineer-website | priority: high\"\nVoice note:   transcribed via Whisper or OpenAI audio before routing\n```\n\n### Components\n\n**1. OpenClaw skill file** (inside the Narada workspace):\n```\n~/.openclaw/workspace-whatsapp-ai-group/skills/wrk-capture.js\n```\n- Triggered on every message from the configured source number/group\n- Calls `classify_message()` → returns `{is_wrk: bool, title, repo, priority}`\n- If `is_wrk`, calls the capture script via `child_process.exec`\n\n**2. Capture script** (in workspace-hub):\n```\nscripts/work-queue/whatsapp-capture.sh <title> <repo> <priority>\n```\n- Wraps `next-id.sh` + WRK file creation + `git add/commit/push`\n- Writes minimal WRK frontmatter + `## What` from the title\n- Logs to `.claude/work-queue/logs/whatsapp-capture.log`\n\n**3. Acknowledgement** (Narada replies to WhatsApp):\n```\n\"✓ Captured as WRK-NNN: <title> (repo: <repo>, priority: <priority>)\"\n```\n\n**4. Configuration** (`.env` or `openclaw.json` extension):\n```\nWRK_CAPTURE_SOURCE_NUMBER=+1XXXXXXXXXX   # self or group JID\nWRK_CAPTURE_REPO_DEFAULT=workspace-hub\nWRK_CAPTURE_PRIORITY_DEFAULT=medium\nWRK_REPO_PATH=/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub\n```\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\n\n- [ ] `scripts/work-queue/whatsapp-capture.sh` creates a valid WRK file and commits\n- [ ] OpenClaw skill classifies messages and routes to capture script\n- [ ] Source filter prevents processing messages from unrecognised numbers\n- [ ] End-to-end: WhatsApp message → WRK-NNN.md in repo within 10 seconds\n- [ ] Narada sends acknowledgement reply with WRK ID\n- [ ] Rate limit (10/hr) and dedup (24h) guards in place\n- [ ] Capture log at `.claude/work-queue/logs/whatsapp-capture.log`\n- [ ] Config documented in `docs/agents/narada-openclaw-setup-guide.md` (WRK-665)\n- [ ] Legal scan passes (no secrets in committed files)\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\n\n- [x] **1. Capture** — done\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** — done\n- [x] **3. Triage** — done\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** — done\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** — done\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** — done\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** — done\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** — done\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** — done\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** — done\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** — done\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** — done\n- [x] **13. Verify Gate Evidence** — done\n- [x] **14. Future Work Synthesis** — done\n- [x] **15. Resource Intelligence Update** — done\n- [x] **16. User Review** — done\n- [ ] **17. Reclaim** — n\n- [ ] **18. Close** — pending\n- [ ] **19. Archive** — pending\n\n</details>","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/273","display_title":"WRK-666: feat(workspace-hub): WhatsApp → WRK capture pipeline — monitor number/channel and auto-create work items"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/285","title":"WRK-020: \"GIS skill for cross-application geospatial tools \\u2014 Blender, QGIS, well\\","issue_number":285,"body":"## WRK-020: GIS skill for cross-application geospatial tools — Blender, QGIS, well plotting\n\n\n\n**Status:** Stage 17: Reclaim (n) | **Priority:** medium | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** gis\n\n**Repo:** ['digitalmodel'] | **Complexity:** complex | **Machine:** dev-secondary\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n### What\n\nBuild a GIS skill/module within digitalmodel that provides geospatial information, coordinate handling, and visualization tools for use across multiple applications and use cases:\n- **Applications**: Blender (3D visualization), QGIS (spatial analysis), Google Earth/Maps, and other GIS-capable tools\n- **Use cases**: Identifying and plotting well locations, bathymetry/terrain rendering, field boundary mapping, pipeline routes, platform locations\n- **Core capabilities**: Coordinate transformation (geographic ↔ projected ↔ local), GIS data import/export, spatial queries, and integration APIs for each target application\n\nExample workflow: given well coordinates from BSEE or other regulatory data, plot them interactively in Blender with proper geospatial positioning, or export to QGIS for spatial analysis overlaid on bathymetry.\n\n### Why\n\nGIS operations are needed across multiple workflows (metocean analysis, field data visualization, digital twin positioning, client presentations). A centralized GIS skill avoids duplicating coordinate handling and spatial logic across separate tools, and provides consistent geospatial capabilities to all downstream applications.\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\n\n- [x] Coordinate reference system (CRS) handling: WGS84, UTM zones, local project coordinates\n- [x] Coordinate transformation utilities between CRS types\n- [x] Well location plotting capability:\n- [x] QGIS: layer generation with well markers, attribute tables (`qgis_export.py`)\n- [x] Blender: 3D positioned well objects with metadata (`blender_export.py`)\n- [x] GIS data format support: GeoJSON, Shapefile, KML/KMZ, GeoTIFF\n- [x] Spatial query support: find wells within radius, bounding box, polygon\n- [ ] Bathymetry/terrain data integration for 3D rendering — Blender terrain mesh generation deferred (GeoTIFFHandler + QGIS export done)\n- [x] Blender GIS integration: Python scripts for importing geospatial data (`blender_export.py`)\n- [x] QGIS integration: processing scripts for well data workflows (`qgis_export.py`, `.qml` style)\n- [x] API/interface for other applications to consume GIS data (`FeatureLayer`, `WellLayer`, GeoJSON export)\n- [x] Document supported CRS, data formats, and application integration steps (.claude/skills/gis/SKILL.md)\n- [x] `coordinates.py`:\n- [x] `CoordinatePoint` dataclass: lat, lon, elevation, crs\n- [x] CRS definitions: WGS84 (EPSG:4326), UTM zones, NAD83, local project coords\n- [x] Transform between CRS using pyproj or manual formulas\n- [x] Batch transform for coordinate lists\n- [x] `io/geojson_handler.py`: Read/write GeoJSON, DataFrame conversion\n- [x] `io/kml_handler.py`: Read/write KML/KMZ (lxml.etree)\n- [x] `io/shapefile_handler.py`: Shapefile support via optional geopandas/fiona\n- [x] `io/geotiff_handler.py`: GeoTIFF reader for bathymetry (optional rasterio)\n- [x] `core/spatial_query.py`:\n- [x] Find points within radius (Haversine via `GeoPoint.distance_to`)\n- [x] Bounding box query (`GeoBoundingBox.contains`)\n- [x] Polygon containment (ray casting)\n- [x] Nearest-N search (linear scan, ascending sorted)\n- [x] `integrations/blender_export.py`:\n- [x] `BlenderExporter.generate_well_script()` — self-contained Blender Python script\n- [x] Coordinate mapping: WGS84 → UTM → Blender scene units via configurable scale\n- [x] Well metadata as Blender custom properties; scene origin anchor at centroid\n- [x] `BlenderExporter.write_well_script()` — write to .py file\n- [x] `BlenderExporter.geo_to_blender()` — static coordinate helper\n- [x] `integrations/qgis_export.py`:\n- [x] `QGISExporter.generate_project()` — QGIS project XML (.qgs) with embedded memory layer\n- [x] Field definitions and WKT feature geometries in project file\n- [x] `QGISExporter.generate_well_qml()` — QML style definition for well markers\n- [x] `QGISExporter.to_geojson_layer()` — GeoJSON export via FeatureLayer\n- [x] `integrations/folium_maps.py` — Folium/Leaflet HTML maps (pre-existing)\n- [x] `integrations/google_earth_export.py` — styled KML for Google Earth (pre-existing)\n- [x] `integrations/plotly_maps.py` — Plotly mapbox scatter/dashboard (pre-existing)\n- [x] `layers/feature_layer.py`:\n- [x] `FeatureLayer`: pandas-backed tabular GIS collection\n- [x] `centroid`, `bounding_box`, `filter()`, `from_geojson()`, `to_geojson()`\n- [x] `layers/well_layer.py`:\n- [x] `WellLayer(FeatureLayer)`: well-specific subclass\n- [x] `filter_by_status()`, `filter_by_depth_range()`, `to_well_list()`\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_crs_and_spatial.py`: CRS, UTM detection, GeoPoint distance, GeoBoundingBox (27 tests)\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_geojson_roundtrip.py`: GeoJSON write→read→compare (8 tests)\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_spatial_queries.py`: radius, bbox, polygon, nearest-N (14 tests)\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_feature_layer.py`: FeatureLayer + WellLayer construction, filters (19 tests)\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_blender_export.py`: ast.parse script validation, coord mapping (13 tests)\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_qgis_export.py`: XML schema validation for .qgs and .qml (18 tests)\n- [x] `tests/gis/test_coordinates.py`: CoordinatePoint, transform_points, get_utm_crs (5 tests)\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\n\n- [x] **1. Capture** — done\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** — done\n- [x] **3. Triage** — done\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** — done\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** — done\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** — done\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** — done\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** — done\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** — done\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** — done\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** — done\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** — done\n- [x] **13. Verify Gate Evidence** — done\n- [x] **14. Future Work Synthesis** — done\n- [x] **15. Resource Intelligence Update** — done\n- [x] **16. User Review** — done\n- [ ] **17. Reclaim** — n\n- [ ] **18. Close** — pending\n- [ ] **19. Archive** — pending\n\n</details>","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/285","display_title":"WRK-020: \"GIS skill for cross-application geospatial tools \\u2014 Blender, QGIS, well\\"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/278","title":"WRK-1280: Add 4-quadrant propeller data to propeller-rudder literature","issue_number":278,"body":"## WRK-1280: Add 4-quadrant propeller data to propeller-rudder literature\n\n\n\n**Status:** done | **Priority:** medium | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** hydrodynamics\n\n**Repo:** ['digitalmodel'] | **Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** dev-primary\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n### Mission\n\nExtend the propeller-rudder literature survey (WRK-1148) with 4-quadrant propeller performance data, enabling crash-stop, crash-astern, and full maneuvering envelope modelling.\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\n\n- [x] ≥4 references covering 4-quadrant propeller data gathered and summarised (11 refs)\n- [x] Fourier coefficient representation of K_T(β), K_Q(β) documented (van Lammeren format)\n- [x] Wageningen B-series 4-quadrant coefficients for ≥1 representative propeller (e.g. B4-70) tabulated\n- [x] Applicability ranges and limitations documented\n- [x] New file `propeller-4-quadrant-data.md` + cross-ref in `propeller-rudder-literature.md`\n- [x] Future work item FW-1 in WRK-1148 evidence marked as `wrk_ref: WRK-1280`\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/278","display_title":"WRK-1280: Add 4-quadrant propeller data to propeller-rudder literature"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/276","title":"WRK-1149: Method assessment and selection — propeller-rudder interaction models","issue_number":276,"body":"## WRK-1149: Method assessment and selection — propeller-rudder interaction models\n\n**Status:** done | **Priority:** medium | **Category:** engineering/hydrodynamics\n**Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** ace-linux-1\n\n---\n\n<details><summary>🔨 Implementation Summary</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>📋 Plan</summary>\n\n\n## Mission\n\nAssess and compare candidate methods for propeller-rudder interaction calculation, select the best fit for a fast-running Python model, and document the decision rationale.\n\n## What\n\nUsing the literature from WRK-1148, evaluate each method family against:\n\n| Criterion | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Accuracy** | Agreement with experimental / RANS data across J range |\n| **Input requirements** | What ship/propeller/rudder parameters are needed |\n| **RPM / J range** | Valid from n=0 (engine-off) through full RPM drive-off |\n| **Computational cost** | Suitable for fast-running (seconds, not hours) Python module |\n| **Implementation complexity** | Can be coded without proprietary tools |\n\nCandidate methods to compare:\n- Actuator-disk + Rankine-Froude (simplest, least data)\n- Holtrop-Mennen semi-empirical corrections\n- Söding/Brix slipstream-in-rudder approach\n- Molland & Turnock empirical lift/drag corrections\n- Hybrid: momentum-based slipstream + empirical rudder coefficients\n\n## Why\n\nMultiple valid methods exist with different trade-offs. Choosing without explicit comparison risks implementing a method that fails outside its validity range (e.g. at low J during drive-off startup).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Comparison table: each method scored/ranked on the five criteria above\n- [ ] Primary method selected with written rationale\n- [ ] Fallback method identified for edge cases (engine-off free-wheeling, very low J)\n- [ ] Known limitations and validation gaps documented\n- [ ] Output: `docs/hydrodynamics/propeller-rudder-method-selection.md`\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>✅ Acceptance Criteria</summary>\n\n- [ ] Comparison table: each method scored/ranked on the five criteria above\n- [ ] Primary method selected with written rationale\n- [ ] Fallback method identified for edge cases (engine-off free-wheeling, very low J)\n- [ ] Known limitations and validation gaps documented\n- [ ] Output: `docs/hydrodynamics/propeller-rudder-method-selection.md`\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>🧪 TDD Results</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>📊 Evidence and Cost</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/276","display_title":"WRK-1149: Method assessment and selection — propeller-rudder interaction models"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2398","title":"feat(knowledge): assess llm-wiki spinout vs embedded workspace-hub architecture","issue_number":2398,"body":"## Summary\nEvaluate whether the llm-wiki system should remain inside `workspace-hub` or be spun out into a standalone repository (or tightly scoped repo family) that serves the wider repo ecosystem.\n\n## Why\nThe llm-wiki program has grown from a local knowledge experiment into a cross-cutting system with its own ingestion pipeline, provenance contracts, promotion waves, governance rules, index/navigation surfaces, and reuse requirements across multiple repos.\n\nAt the current scale, repo-bound coupling may become a bottleneck for:\n- ownership clarity\n- CI/runtime isolation\n- clone size and repo churn\n- versioning of wiki schemas and promotion contracts\n- reuse across tier-1 repos and future repos\n- provider/tool portability for search, retrieval, and promotion workflows\n\nThis should be decided explicitly rather than by drift.\n\n## Decision to make\nShould llm-wikis:\n1. remain inside `workspace-hub`\n2. move to one standalone `llm-wiki` repo\n3. split into a core platform repo plus separate domain-content repos\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n- coupling to workspace-hub scripts and skills\n- cross-repo consumption patterns and backlink needs\n- size/churn/performance impact on the main repo\n- governance of durable vs transient knowledge\n- promotion-pipeline ownership and release cadence\n- CI/testing boundaries\n- portability to other machines and agents\n- search/index build performance\n- contribution ergonomics for domain-specific wiki work\n- migration complexity and link-rot risk\n\n## Deliverables\n1. Current-state architecture summary of llm-wiki dependencies on workspace-hub.\n2. Option comparison matrix: stay-in-place vs single spinout vs split architecture.\n3. Recommendation with explicit decision criteria and non-goals.\n4. Migration plan outline if spinout is recommended, including:\n   - repo boundaries\n   - sync/backlink strategy\n   - release/versioning model\n   - search/index ownership\n   - path/link migration plan\n5. Follow-up implementation issues only if the recommendation is to spin out or partially split.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] current llm-wiki coupling to workspace-hub is mapped concretely\n- [ ] at least three architecture options are compared with trade-offs\n- [ ] recommendation is justified with ecosystem-level reasoning, not just local convenience\n- [ ] migration risks and rollback path are described if spinout is recommended\n- [ ] result clearly states whether llm-wiki should stay embedded, be split out, or be hybridized\n\n## Related\n- vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13 llm-wiki strengthening roadmap and execution waves\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2034 engineering LLM wiki seed + incremental ingest pipeline\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2206 validate single-source-of-truth pyramid conformance\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2207 standards/codes provenance + reuse contract for llm-wiki promotion\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2209 durable-vs-transient knowledge boundary across wikis, issues, registries, and session artifacts\n- vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#37 llm-wiki strengthening scorecard and prioritized action queue\n\n## Notes\nThis is a strategy/architecture issue. It should not absorb ongoing llm-wiki content promotion work.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2398","display_title":"feat(knowledge): assess llm-wiki spinout vs embedded workspace-hub architecture"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/172","title":"WRK-009: Reproduce rev30 lower tertiary BSEE field results for repeatability","issue_number":172,"body":"## WRK-009: Reproduce rev30 lower tertiary BSEE field results for repeatability\n\n**Status:** archived | **Priority:** high | **Category:** data/bsee\n**Complexity:** medium\n\n---\n\n<details><summary>📋 Plan</summary>\n\n# Reproduce rev30 lower tertiary BSEE field results for repeatability\n\n## What\nReproduce the rev30 lower tertiary results previously provided by Roy using BSEE field data for the same time period. Validate that the analysis pipeline produces identical or equivalent results to confirm repeatability of the methodology.\n\n## Why\nEstablishing repeatability is a prerequisite for trusting updated results. Before rerunning with new data or expanding scope, we need confidence the analysis pipeline faithfully reproduces known results.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] Identify the rev30 lower tertiary results provided by Roy (reference data)\n- [x] Identify the exact time period and data inputs used in rev30\n- [x] Run the BSEE field analysis pipeline with the same inputs\n- [x] Compare output against Roy's rev30 results\n- [x] Document any discrepancies with explanations\n- [x] Confirm repeatability or flag issues\n\n## Results\n\n**Phase A (Determinism):** 21/21 tests PASS — V30 golden baseline confirmed stable\n**Phase B (Repeatability):** 12/12 tests PASS — all 7 producing developments reproduced within ±0.1%\n\n| Development | Delta | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Jack St Malo | +0.03% | PASS |\n| Stones | 0.00% | PASS |\n| Julia | -0.00% | PASS |\n| Big Foot | 0.00% | PASS |\n| Cascade Chinook | 0.00% | PASS |\n| Anchor | 0.00% | PASS |\n| Shenandoah | 0.00% | PASS |\n\n</details>","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/172","display_title":"WRK-009: Reproduce rev30 lower tertiary BSEE field results for repeatability"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/157","title":"WRK-1247: POC — Deep extraction on 10 XLSX calculation spreadsheets","issue_number":157,"body":"## WRK-1247: POC — Deep extraction on 10 XLSX calculation spreadsheets\n\n\n\n**Status:** Stage 20: Archive (pending) | **Priority:** high | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** data-extraction\n\n**Repo:** ['workspace-hub'] | **Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** dev-primary\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n### Mission\n\nProve XLSX formula extraction yield on 10 calculation spreadsheets before scaling to 23K XLSX files.\n\n### What\n\nSelect 10 XLSX files from ace_project + dde_project (prioritizing filenames containing\n\"calc\", \"design\", \"analysis\") and run the dark intelligence workflow on each:\nExtract → Sanitize → Archive → Generate TDD tests → Implement.\n\n### XLSX Extractor Enhancements Required\n\nThe current `deep-extract.py` XLSX path uses openpyxl for basic table extraction but\ndoesn't extract formulas — only cell values. This POC requires:\n\n1. Read cell formulas (not just computed values) via `openpyxl` `data_only=False`\n2. Map formula dependencies (input → calculation → output chains)\n3. Extract named ranges as variable definitions\n4. Identify calculation blocks vs data blocks (heuristic: cells with formulas vs static data)\n\n### Extraction Outputs Per Spreadsheet\n\n- YAML calc-report inputs (named ranges → variables, formula chains → functions)\n- Python functions implementing extracted calculation logic\n- pytest test cases using original cell values as assertions\n- Validation against existing digitalmodel/assetutilities code where overlap exists\n\n### Why\n\nThe 23,642 XLSX files are the second-largest extraction target after PDFs. Without\nformula extraction, we only get cell values — losing the calculation methodologies\nthat are the primary engineering value. This POC identifies parser gaps on a controlled\nset before committing to full-scale extraction.\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\n\n\n**Tests:** Passed: 373\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\n\n**Future work:** see evidence file\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\n\n- [x] **1. Capture** — done\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** — done\n- [x] **3. Triage** — done\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** — done\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** — done\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** — done\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** — done\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** — done\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** — done\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** — done\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** — done\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** — done\n- [x] **13. Agent Cross-Review** — done\n- [x] **14. Verify Gate Evidence** — done\n- [x] **15. Future Work Synthesis** — done\n- [x] **16. Resource Intelligence Update** — done\n- [x] **17. User Review - Implementation** — done\n- [x] **18. Reclaim** — done\n- [x] **19. Close** — done\n- [ ] **20. Archive** — pending\n\n</details>","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/157","display_title":"WRK-1247: POC — Deep extraction on 10 XLSX calculation spreadsheets"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/135","title":"WRK-1269: POC v2 — Pattern-aware XLSX-to-Python on 10 workbooks using xlsx-to-python skill","issue_number":135,"body":"## WRK-1269: POC v2 — Pattern-aware XLSX-to-Python on 10 workbooks using xlsx-to-python skill\n\n\n\n**Status:** closed | **Priority:** high | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** data-extraction\n\n**Repo:** ['workspace-hub'] | **Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** dev-primary\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n### Mission\n\nRe-run the 10-workbook POC using the updated xlsx-to-python skill — detect row patterns, collapse to Python loops, generate compact runnable code + HTML calc-reports.\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/135","display_title":"WRK-1269: POC v2 — Pattern-aware XLSX-to-Python on 10 workbooks using xlsx-to-python skill"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2642","title":"feat(naval-arch): B1528 SIROCCO moored-current rudder force component report","issue_number":2642,"body":"## Purpose\nPrepare a professional B1528 SIROCCO/Sorrocco moored-current rudder force-component report for engineer review.\n\n## Requested scenario\n- Vessel condition: moored.\n- Current passing the rudder: 3.5 kn.\n- Ship speed over ground: 0 kn.\n- Rudder angles: 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 deg to port and starboard.\n- Propeller rotation factor: Cr=1.0, neutral non-rotating/no-rotation-correction multiplier.\n- Required outputs: all force components at the COG with charts, sample working example, assumptions, limitations, schematics, and traceability links.\n\n## Scope boundary\nThis issue covers rudder-induced moored-current loads only. Hull current force, bank effects, tug loads, mooring-line stiffness, current-profile variation, propeller race, and class/IMO compliance conclusions are out of scope unless source coefficients are later supplied.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [x] Packaged input YAML records B1528 design data, current case, rudder sweep, Cr basis, conventions, and limitations.\n- [x] Calculation output reports COG components X/Y/Z/K/M/N for port and starboard rudder angles.\n- [x] Professional Markdown/HTML report includes design data, methodology, assumptions, schematics, charts, sample working example, references, and GitHub traceability links.\n- [x] PDF export is generated for ACMA B1528 output review.\n- [x] Focused regression tests validate symmetry, Cr=1.0 logic, sample data point, and report content.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2642","display_title":"feat(naval-arch): B1528 SIROCCO moored-current rudder force component report"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/355","title":"docs(cli): align public CLI docs/examples with smoke matrix","issue_number":355,"body":"## Summary\nClean up the public CLI/examples documentation surface based on the #352 smoke matrix: ensure registered sub-apps are documented, stale command docs are retired or renamed, unsafe/download-heavy examples carry warnings, and broken examples are fixed or deleted.\n\n## Evidence\nThe #352 CLI/examples smoke matrix found:\n\n- `worldenergydata info` omits registered apps: `dashboard`, `eia`, `ndbc`, `forecast`.\n- `docs/COMMANDS.md` appears unrelated to the worldenergydata CLI; it documents legacy assetutilities slash commands.\n- `examples/fdas_complete_workflow.py` imports retired `src.worldenergydata.modules.fdas` paths (#278).\n- `examples/validation_examples.py` imports a missing `validator_template`.\n- Three LLM examples download ~1.6 GB on first run without a script-level warning.\n- Some README commands are data-required or unsafe/unbounded without guardrail language.\n\nArtifacts:\n- `docs/reports/2026-04-26-worldenergydata-cli-example-smoke-matrix.md`\n- `docs/reports/2026-04-26-worldenergydata-cli-example-smoke-matrix.yaml`\n\n## Scope\n- Update docs/README CLI surfaces so all registered sub-apps are documented or explicitly hidden/internal.\n- Fix `worldenergydata info` output or its backing table to include all intended registered apps.\n- Rename/remove/rewrite `docs/COMMANDS.md` if it is not a worldenergydata CLI reference.\n- Fix or delete broken examples rather than leaving stale import paths.\n- Add explicit warnings to scripts/examples that perform large downloads or model downloads.\n- Add a reusable smoke matrix script/check if practical.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- README/docs commands are classified as safe, data-required, credential-required, or unbounded where relevant.\n- Broken examples either run in a bounded fixture mode or are removed with replacement guidance.\n- `worldenergydata info` no longer omits intended CLI sub-apps.\n- The changes are tested with bounded no-download smoke checks.\n\n## Related\n- #352 CLI/examples smoke matrix\n- #278 compatibility shim issue\n- #313/#327 test infra risks\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/355","display_title":"docs(cli): align public CLI docs/examples with smoke matrix"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/354","title":"docs(capability): reconcile module manifest, indexes, catalog, CLI info","issue_number":354,"body":"## Summary\nReconcile `worldenergydata` capability indexes so agents and users can trust one canonical module map instead of conflicting README, `MODULE_INDEX.md`, `module-manifest.yaml`, source tree, data catalog, CLI, and scheduler surfaces.\n\n## Evidence\nThe #349 capability audit found major source-of-truth drift:\n\n- `README.md` highlights only 3 modules: `bsee`, `marine-safety`, `fdas`.\n- `MODULE_INDEX.md` / `module-manifest.yaml` advertise 27 modules.\n- `data/catalog.yaml` reports 12 modules / 44 datasets / ~10.5 MB.\n- `src/worldenergydata/` contains ~40 top-level package directories.\n- `cli/main.py` registers 15 sub-apps; several manifest-stable modules have no CLI surface.\n- Scheduler flags disagree between manifest/config for some modules.\n\nArtifacts:\n- `docs/reports/2026-04-26-worldenergydata-capability-readiness-matrix.md`\n- `docs/reports/2026-04-26-worldenergydata-capability-readiness-matrix.yaml`\n\n## Scope\n- Define the authoritative capability manifest source and update stale generated/static indexes accordingly.\n- For every top-level production package, explicitly classify: canonical module, internal support package, legacy shim, deprecated, or excluded.\n- Align `MODULE_INDEX.md`, `module-manifest.yaml`, README module list, CLI `info`, and data catalog references.\n- Do not silently mark modules stable unless source, tests, docs, data, and runtime surfaces justify that status.\n- Produce follow-up issues for modules that require implementation rather than manifest edits.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- There is a single documented rule for module readiness/status classification.\n- README, `MODULE_INDEX.md`, manifest, CLI `info`, and catalog references no longer materially contradict each other.\n- Off-manifest packages such as `cost`, `dashboard`, `decommissioning`, `west_africa`, `baker_hughes`, etc. are explicitly classified.\n- Empty/sample-only data modules are not presented as production-complete.\n- The update is backed by tests or snapshot checks where practical.\n\n## Related\n- #349 capability audit\n- #350 data completeness scorecard\n- #352 CLI smoke matrix\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/354","display_title":"docs(capability): reconcile module manifest, indexes, catalog, CLI info"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/384","title":"fix(bsee): lazy-load module-level singletons in bsee_data.py — unblocks import and GTM demos","issue_number":384,"body":"## Problem\n\n`src/worldenergydata/bsee/data/bsee_data.py` instantiates 5 module-level singletons at Python import time:\n\n```python\nproduction = ProductionRouter()   # ← hangs 30+ seconds\nblock      = BlockRouter()\nlease      = LeaseRouter()\nwell       = WellData()\ndata_refresh = DataRefresh()\n```\n\nOn any machine where the ~300 MB BSEE binary dataset is absent (CI, clean demo environments, client-facing deployments), importing `worldenergydata.bsee` hangs for 30+ seconds or indefinitely. This blocks:\n- All GTM demos that touch BSEE data\n- All CI test jobs that import the BSEE module\n- Any script that imports `worldenergydata.cli.main` (since it imports all subcommands at startup)\n\n**RCA**: `ProductionRouter.__init__` (or one of its transitive dependencies) does filesystem or network I/O when the binary data path is absent rather than deferring until first use.\n\n## Fix\n\nReplace module-level singletons with lazy factory functions or class-level lazy properties:\n\n```python\n# Before (eager — hangs at import)\nproduction = ProductionRouter()\n\n# After (lazy — defers until first use)\ndef get_production() -> ProductionRouter:\n    return ProductionRouter()\n```\n\nAll callers in `BSEEData.router()` already use the singleton through function calls — the refactor is a drop-in replacement with no API change at the `BSEEData` level.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `import worldenergydata.bsee` completes in <1 second on a machine without the BSEE binary dataset\n- [ ] All existing BSEE tests continue to pass (regression guard)\n- [ ] `worldenergydata bsee --help` works in <2 seconds without data\n- [ ] GTM demo scripts that use FDAS/Arps can import `worldenergydata.cli.main` without hanging\n\n## References\n\n- GTM candidate review: `worldenergydata/docs/reports/gtm/2026-05-04-worldenergydata-gtm-candidate-review.md`\n- Confirmed hang location: `src/worldenergydata/bsee/data/bsee_data.py:6-10`\n- Related workspace-hub issue: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#117","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/384","display_title":"fix(bsee): lazy-load module-level singletons in bsee_data.py — unblocks import and GTM demos"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2544","title":"feat(llm-wiki): scout Woodfibre LNG corpus for bounded extraction candidates","issue_number":2544,"body":"## Summary\nPlan a metadata-first scout and curated extraction candidate pass for the Woodfibre LNG project corpus under `acma-projects`.\n\n## Source of record\n- Raw corpus: `/mnt/ace/acma-projects/31522-woodfibre-lng`\n- Wiki domain: `lng-projects`\n- Existing metadata page from #2535: bucket-level Elements source/catalog page in `knowledge/wikis/lng-projects/`\n\n## Why\nWoodfibre is the largest Elements-ingested corpus: 5,364 files / ~1.879 TB. It must not be processed with broad text extraction or raw copying. The next overnight-safe step is a structure scout: identify high-value, small candidate sets and draft a bounded extraction plan.\n\n## Scope\nIn scope:\n- Build a top-level and second-level corpus structure map from paths and file metadata.\n- Identify high-value extraction candidate families without opening or duplicating large binary/source files unnecessarily.\n- Propose no more than 15 first-tranche artifacts, with explicit size/type limits and extraction methods.\n- Draft an approval-ready plan for later bounded extraction.\n\nOut of scope:\n- No broad extraction over 1.879 TB.\n- No copying raw Woodfibre files into git/wiki.\n- No modifications to `/mnt/ace/acma-projects/31522-woodfibre-lng`.\n- No source/staging cleanup.\n\n## Deliverables\n- `docs/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-issue-NNN-elements-woodfibre-scout-plan.md`\n- `.planning/intel/elements-overnight-wave/woodfibre-corpus-scout.md`\n- Proposed first-tranche candidate table with paths, sizes, type, rationale, and expected wiki target.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Scout summarizes corpus structure without broad raw extraction.\n- [ ] Candidate tranche is bounded to <=15 artifacts and avoids very large binaries unless explicitly justified for metadata-only handling.\n- [ ] Plan states raw-data policy and #2534 retention boundary.\n- [ ] Plan identifies whether additional ACMA/client confidentiality review is needed before extraction.\n- [ ] Issue is left at plan-review, not self-approved.\n\n## Related\n- Umbrella: #2540\n- Completed metadata indexing: #2535\n- Completed first-pass extraction: #2536\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2544","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): scout Woodfibre LNG corpus for bounded extraction candidates"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2627","title":"wiki(engineering-standards): create DNV-RP-F103 page (unblocks #2609 R3 fix)","issue_number":2627,"body":"## Lead\n\nUnblocks R3 cluster (16 tests) of [#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609). Per `.claude/rules/calc-citation-contract.md`, the digitalmodel `cathodic_protection.py:67` `DNV_RP_F103_2010` method will need a citation target wiki page that does not currently exist.\n\n## Gap (verified at HEAD 2026-05-02)\n\n`ls knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/wiki/standards/` shows: `dnv-os-e301.md`, `dnv-rp-b401.md`, `dnv-rp-c203.md`, `dnv-rp-c205.md`, `dnv-rp-f101.md`, `dnv-rp-f105.md`, `dnv-st-f101.md`. **No `dnv-rp-f103.md`.**\n\n## Target path\n\n`knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/wiki/standards/dnv-rp-f103.md`\n\n## Required frontmatter (per [#2471](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2471) schema; L0-prose enforcement per `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/CLAUDE.md`)\n\n- `code_id: dnv-rp-f103`\n- `publisher: DNV`\n- `revision: \"2010\"` — primary; tests reference both 2010 (canonical method name `DNV_RP_F103_2010`) and 2016 features (per test docstring `Tests implementation of DNV RP-F103:2016 features`). Recommend `revision: \"2010\"` with `supersedes:` or a sibling note for 2016 features. To be confirmed against test fixtures during page authoring.\n- `revision_source: \"/mnt/ace/O&G-Standards/DNV/DNV_RP_F103_(2010)_Cathodic_Protection_of_Submarine_Pipelines_by_Galvanic_Anodes.pdf\"`\n- `title`, `tags`, `added`, `last_updated`, `domain: engineering-standards` per the wiki schema CLAUDE.md\n\n## Reference PDF\n\n`/mnt/ace/O&G-Standards/DNV/DNV_RP_F103_(2010)_Cathodic_Protection_of_Submarine_Pipelines_by_Galvanic_Anodes.pdf` (read-only, vendor-derivative; do NOT copy into git per the metadata-only extraction policy used by the b401 sister page).\n\n## Source code that will need a citation surface\n\n`digitalmodel/src/digitalmodel/infrastructure/base_solvers/hydrodynamics/cathodic_protection.py:67` — method `DNV_RP_F103_2010`. From the source docstring, the wiki page will need to capture:\n\n- **Annex 1** linear formula (Eq.2 / Eq.4) — coating breakdown factors\n- **Eq.11** — referenced in the calc plan for the R3 cluster (pipeline attenuation analysis)\n- **Table 5-1** — current density requirements (burial x internal fluid temperature)\n- Distributed anode spacing optimization\n- Anode mass and quantity requirements\n- Wet-storage period (R3 fix scope)\n\n## Sister wiki for structural template\n\n`knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/wiki/standards/dnv-rp-b401.md` — bounded summary, metadata-only extraction policy, sources list, cross-references to sibling DNV pages.\n\n## Why this matters (citation contract clauses)\n\nPer `.claude/rules/calc-citation-contract.md`:\n\n- Clause 2: \\\"Citation target: a wiki page with #2471 frontmatter (`code_id`, `publisher`, `revision`).\\\"\n- Clause 4: \\\"Validation is **fail-closed at calc time** per #2481 D2: a missing wiki page or frontmatter mismatch raises `CitationResolutionError` with the `code_id` in the message.\\\"\n- Clause 5: \\\"Resolver is **direct file read** for v1 per #2481 D3.\\\"\n\nWithout `dnv-rp-f103.md`, any R3 calc-citation-emitting fix will fail-closed. The R3 sub-issue cannot be filed until this lands.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/wiki/standards/dnv-rp-f103.md` will exist at the target path\n- [ ] Frontmatter will comply with [#2471](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2471) schema (`code_id`, `publisher`, `revision`) plus the wiki-schema fields per `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/CLAUDE.md`\n- [ ] Page will capture key clauses referenced by digitalmodel: Annex 1 (Eq.2/Eq.4), Eq.11, Table 5-1, plus current density tables, attenuation factor, wet-storage period\n- [ ] Citation resolver will locate the page via direct file read (per `.claude/rules/calc-citation-contract.md` clause 5, v1)\n- [ ] R3 sub-issue against digitalmodel will become fileable after this lands\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Actually creating the R3 fix sub-issue (separate after this lands)\n- Modifying `digitalmodel` source\n- Other [#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) clusters (R1, R2, R4, …)\n- Copying clause text from the vendor PDF (metadata-only extraction policy)\n\n## References\n\n- Parent umbrella: [#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609)\n- Calc citation contract rule: `.claude/rules/calc-citation-contract.md`\n- Wiki frontmatter schema issue: [#2471](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2471)\n- Wiki schema CLAUDE.md: `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/CLAUDE.md`\n- Triage plan: `docs/plans/2026-05-03-2609-marine-ops-triage.md`\n- Sister page: `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/wiki/standards/dnv-rp-b401.md`\n- Source code: `digitalmodel/src/digitalmodel/infrastructure/base_solvers/hydrodynamics/cathodic_protection.py:67`\n- Test file: `digitalmodel/tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/test_cathodic_protection_dnv.py`\n\n## Authorization\n\nUser explicitly authorized this filing as part of \\\"approve all 4 proceed on all\\\" on 2026-05-02. `status:plan-approved` applied at creation per that authorization.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2627","display_title":"wiki(engineering-standards): create DNV-RP-F103 page (unblocks #2609 R3 fix)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/570","title":"fix(tests/orcaflex): replace bare CLI invocations with sys.executable -m — clears ~22 of 42 solvers/orcaflex failures","issue_number":570,"body":"Sub-issue of [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) cluster S1, sister to marine_ops sub-issues [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#554](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/554) (R2 catenary), [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#555](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/555) (R4 chain DB), [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#556](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/556)–[vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#565](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/565) (R6 individuals), [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#566](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/566) (R1+R5+R7+R8 batched). Triage plan: `docs/plans/2026-05-03-2609-solvers-orcaflex-triage.md` (workspace-hub).\n\n## Symptom\n\nTop error (inferred from bare-command pattern; implementing agent will capture verbatim traceback under `--tb=short` before opening PR):\n\n```\nFileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'orcaflex-universal'\n```\n\n(also `'run-to-sim'` for the run-to-sim subset).\n\n## Root cause (live-verified)\n\nBare-command `subprocess.run(['orcaflex-universal', ...])` invocations don't propagate the venv's `bin/` onto the subprocess `PATH`. The CLI registrations at `pyproject.toml:193-198` are correct and the binaries execute fine standalone:\n\n```toml\n[project.scripts]\nrun-to-sim = \"digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.run_to_sim_cli:main\"\norcaflex-universal = \"digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.universal_cli:main\"\norcaflex-sim = \"digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.universal_cli:main\"\norcaflex-convert = \"digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.format_converter.cli:main\"\n```\n\nThe failing tests assume implicit `PATH` inheritance that pytest's subprocess invocation pattern doesn't provide. Setting `PATH=<venv>/bin:$PATH` in the subprocess `env=` argument makes the failing tests pass at the same SHA — proven live.\n\n## Fix template\n\nThe lone passing test in the same file (`TestCLIModuleIntegration::test_python_can_import_after_cli_install`, `tests/solvers/orcaflex/test_orcaflex_cli.py:203-212`) uses:\n\n```python\nresult = subprocess.run(\n    [sys.executable, '-c', 'import digitalmodel.orcaflex; ...'],\n    capture_output=True, text=True\n)\n```\n\nApply the same shape to all bare-command tests, swapping the bare CLI name for `[sys.executable, '-m', '<module>']`:\n\n- `'orcaflex-universal'` → `[sys.executable, '-m', 'digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.universal_cli']`\n- `'run-to-sim'` → `[sys.executable, '-m', 'digitalmodel.solvers.orcaflex.run_to_sim_cli']`\n\nThe `[project.scripts]` entries map `<name>` → `<module>:main`, so `python -m <module>` reaches the same `:main` callable with no CLI-API change. Venv-agnostic; matches the lone passing test's pattern.\n\n### Why not just fix `PATH`?\n\nSetting `env={'PATH': f'{venv_bin}:{os.environ[\\\"PATH\\\"]}'}` on every `subprocess.run` would also work but couples tests to venv layout discovery and breaks under `uv run`. The `sys.executable -m` form is cleaner.\n\n## Affected tests\n\n22 tests across 9 classes, all in `tests/solvers/orcaflex/test_orcaflex_cli.py`:\n\n<details>\n<summary>Full list (click to expand)</summary>\n\n- `TestCLIAvailability::test_orcaflex_universal_command_exists` (L16)\n- `TestCLIAvailability::test_run_to_sim_command_exists` (L26)\n- `TestUniversalCLIHelp::test_universal_help` (L39)\n- `TestUniversalCLIHelp::test_universal_version` (L50)\n- `TestRunToSimCLIHelp::test_run_to_sim_help` (L65)\n- `TestRunToSimCLIHelp::test_run_to_sim_version` (L76)\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_universal_pattern_option` (L90)\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_universal_output_option` (L99)\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_universal_parallel_option` (L108)\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_universal_mock_option` (L117)\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_run_to_sim_all_option` (L126)\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_run_to_sim_models_option` (L135)\n- `TestCLIOptions::test_run_to_sim_threads_option` (L144)\n- `TestCLIErrorHandling::test_universal_invalid_option` (L157)\n- `TestCLIErrorHandling::test_run_to_sim_invalid_option` (L167)\n- `TestCLIExamples::test_universal_help_shows_examples` (L180)\n- `TestCLIExamples::test_run_to_sim_help_shows_examples` (L190)\n- `TestCLIOutputFormats::test_universal_verbose_output` (L232)\n- `TestCLIOutputFormats::test_run_to_sim_verbose_output` (L241)\n- `TestCLICompatibility::test_cli_runs_on_current_python` (L254) — invokes both binaries; rewrite both `subprocess.run` calls in this method\n- `TestCLIDefaults::test_universal_defaults_shown_in_help` (L275)\n- `TestCLIDefaults::test_run_to_sim_defaults_shown_in_help` (L285)\n\n</details>\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- `TestCLIModuleIntegration::test_cli_commands_from_module` (L214) — already uses `sys.executable` but fails on assertion `'orcaflex-universal' in result.stdout`. Likely a real source bug (`list_cli_commands()` namespace mismatch); a separate issue will be spawned per Decision 3 of the triage plan.\n- Other [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) clusters: S2/S3/S4/S5, E1/E2.\n- All marine_ops clusters (R1–R8) — covered by sister issues listed above.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] All 22 named tests pass under `pytest -n auto` (xdist) and serial.\n- [ ] `TestCLIModuleIntegration::test_python_can_import_after_cli_install` continues to pass (no regression in lone-passing test).\n- [ ] No regression in non-S1 solvers tests (run full `tests/solvers/orcaflex/` suite, compare pre/post FAILED list).\n- [ ] One PR.\n\n## Estimated diff\n\nTest-only changes. ~22 occurrences of `['orcaflex-universal', ...]` / `['run-to-sim', ...]` rewritten to `[sys.executable, '-m', '<module>', ...]` in `tests/solvers/orcaflex/test_orcaflex_cli.py`. ~30–40 LoC delta. No source changes.\n\n## References\n\n- Triage plan: `docs/plans/2026-05-03-2609-solvers-orcaflex-triage.md` (workspace-hub, committed at `714640353`)\n- Parent: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609)\n- Closed precursor: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580)","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/570","display_title":"fix(tests/orcaflex): replace bare CLI invocations with sys.executable -m — clears ~22 of 42 solvers/orcaflex failures"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/571","title":"fix(tests/orcaflex): test_cli_commands_from_module assertion drift — separate from S1 PATH cluster","issue_number":571,"body":"Sub-issue of [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) — separated from S1 PATH cluster after live verification proved the test already uses \\`sys.executable\\` and still fails.\n\n## Test nodeid\n\n\\`tests/solvers/orcaflex/test_orcaflex_cli.py::TestCLIModuleIntegration::test_cli_commands_from_module\\` (line 214, class \\`TestCLIModuleIntegration\\` at line 200)\n\n## Why this is NOT part of S1 ([vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#570](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/570))\n\nThe S1 cluster covers ~22 tests that fail with \\`FileNotFoundError\\` because their bare-command \\`subprocess.run(['orcaflex-universal'])\\` invocations can't find the binary on PATH. The fix template is to switch to \\`sys.executable -m\\`.\n\nThis test ALREADY uses \\`sys.executable\\` (with \\`-c\\` flag, not \\`-m\\` — but \\`sys.executable\\` either way) to invoke an in-process Python that imports \\`list_cli_commands\\` from \\`digitalmodel.orcaflex\\` and prints the result. So PATH-propagation is NOT the failure mode. The test fails on the assertions \\`'orcaflex-universal' in result.stdout\\` and \\`'run-to-sim' in result.stdout\\` — meaning either \\`list_cli_commands()\\` no longer returns those commands, returns them in a different format, or the function was renamed/removed.\n\nSource verbatim (lines 214–227):\n\n\\`\\`\\`python\ndef test_cli_commands_from_module(self):\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Test CLI commands list from module\\\"\\\"\\\"\n    result = subprocess.run(\n        [sys.executable, '-c',\n         'from digitalmodel.orcaflex import list_cli_commands; '\n         'cmds = list_cli_commands(); '\n         'print(cmds)'],\n        capture_output=True,\n        text=True\n    )\n    assert result.returncode == 0\n    assert 'orcaflex-universal' in result.stdout\n    assert 'run-to-sim' in result.stdout\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Hypothesis\n\nReal assertion drift in the CLI module-integration test. Likely root causes:\n- \\`list_cli_commands()\\` renamed or removed\n- \\`list_cli_commands()\\` returns command objects (not strings) and \\`print(cmds)\\` no longer surfaces the names\n- Console-script names changed (but \\`pyproject.toml:193-198\\` still has \\`run-to-sim\\` and \\`orcaflex-universal\\` registered, so this is unlikely the root)\n\nInvestigation will start by running the inner Python invocation manually to capture what \\`list_cli_commands()\\` actually returns at HEAD.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] \\`test_cli_commands_from_module\\` passes\n- [ ] No regression in other \\`TestCLIModuleIntegration\\` tests\n- [ ] Root cause documented in PR (renamed function vs. format change vs. real registry drift)\n\n## Estimated diff\n\n≤10 LoC test or source change once root cause is confirmed.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The 22 S1 PATH-cluster tests — sister sub-issue [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#570](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/570)\n- Other [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609) clusters\n\n## References\n\n- Triage plan: \\`docs/plans/2026-05-03-2609-solvers-orcaflex-triage.md\\` (workspace-hub)\n- Parent umbrella: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2609](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609)\n- Sister S1 cluster: [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#570](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/570)\n- Closed parent: [vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580)\n\nUser authorized this filing in session 2026-05-03 (\"approve all 4 proceed on all\").","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/issues/571","display_title":"fix(tests/orcaflex): test_cli_commands_from_module assertion drift — separate from S1 PATH cluster"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2479","title":"fix(review): Codex stdin-hang regression post-#2406 closure (size-dependent)","issue_number":2479,"body":"## Summary\n\nThe 2026-04-23 cross-review batch saw **8/8 Codex results UNAVAILABLE** on T2+ plans. Two defects, one wrapper, one upstream:\n\n1. **Wrapper bug (fixed in [`fix/codex-stdin-hang`](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/tree/fix/codex-stdin-hang)):** `scripts/review/plan-review-fanout.sh:82` passed `--no-interactive`, removed from codex-cli 0.124.0 (released 2026-04-23). The flag now returns `error: unexpected argument '--no-interactive' found` / rc=2 on every invocation through that wrapper path.\n\n2. **Upstream regression (NOT patchable here):** `codex exec` in codex-cli 0.124.0 prints `Reading additional input from stdin...` and blocks indefinitely **even when stdin is redirected to `/dev/null` or the FD is closed**. Every invocation through `scripts/review/submit-to-codex.sh` now trips timeout=300s and exits 124.\n\nThe `#2406` fix (`</dev/null` redirect) worked on codex-cli 0.121.0 but is **demonstrably reverted / regressed by the 2026-04-23 upgrade to 0.124.0**. That closure was premature against the broader \"substantial plan\" failure class.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Installed version vs. last-known-working\n```\n$ codex --version\ncodex-cli 0.124.0\n\n$ stat -c '%y' /home/vamsee/.npm-global/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex/\n2026-04-23 15:06:20  # upgrade day == first-failure day\n```\n\n### Live repro of the upstream stdin-hang\n```\n$ time timeout 90 codex exec \"just say hello\" --skip-git-repo-check </dev/null\nReading additional input from stdin...\nreal    1m30.033s\nexit=124\n```\nClosing FD 0 (`exec 0<&-`), wrapping in `setsid -w`, and short/trivial prompts all reproduce the same 90-second hang → exit 124. Not size-dependent — codex 0.124.0 hangs on ANY `exec` invocation where stdin is a non-TTY file/pipe.\n\n### Plan-review-fanout flag rejection\n```\n$ codex exec --no-interactive \"test\" --skip-git-repo-check </dev/null\nerror: unexpected argument '--no-interactive' found\n  tip: to pass '--no-interactive' as a value, use '-- --no-interactive'\nUsage: codex exec [OPTIONS] [PROMPT]\n```\n\n### 2026-04-23 batch failure manifest\n- `scripts/review/results/2026-04-23-plan-2363-codex.md` → `# Codex review failed (exit 124)`\n- `scripts/review/results/2026-04-23-plan-2368-codex.md` → same\n- Retry logs: `/tmp/codex-{2476,2227,2216,2105,2380,2373,2364,2369,2368,2392,2363}-*.out` — every one ends with `STDERR: Reading additional input from stdin...`\n- `/tmp/review-2380.log` → `WARNING: Codex review FAILED (exit 124) — this is a HARD GATE`\n\n### T1 vs T2+ distinction (from the original brief)\nNot actually size-dependent in the wrapper — the hang reproduces on a 90-byte test plan through `submit-to-codex.sh`. The T1 successes in the 2026-04-22 set predate the 0.124.0 upgrade (`scripts/review/results/2026-04-22-plan-2443-codex-r5.md` is a valid MAJOR review from before 2026-04-23 15:06).\n\n## Root cause\n\n- **Wrapper half:** stale `--no-interactive` flag in `plan-review-fanout.sh:82`. codex-cli dropped the flag (no deprecation window); `codex exec` is non-interactive by default.\n- **Upstream half:** codex-cli 0.124.0 `exec` subcommand stdin detection regressed — it prints `Reading additional input from stdin...` and blocks on FD 0 reads even when stdin is `/dev/null`. No `--stdin=never` / `--no-stdin` flag exists in `codex exec --help` as of 0.124.0. Downgrade to 0.123.0 (or pinning to 0.121.0, the #2406 baseline) is the only known-good workaround until upstream ships a fix.\n\n## Fix status\n\n- Branch: **[`fix/codex-stdin-hang`](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/tree/fix/codex-stdin-hang)** (commit `257b47dd9`)\n  - Removes `--no-interactive` from `plan-review-fanout.sh:82`\n  - Adds `</dev/null` stdin redirect (consistency with `submit-to-codex.sh`)\n  - Adds regression guard in `test_plan_review_fanout.sh` — test 5 fails if the removed flag resurfaces\n  - All 12 fanout tests pass\n\n- **NOT fixed in this branch (upstream):** the `Reading additional input from stdin...` hang in `codex exec` itself. No wrapper-level workaround exists. Requires either:\n  - Pin/downgrade `@openai/codex` to 0.123.0 or earlier (user decision — not executed unilaterally)\n  - Upstream patch to `codex exec` stdin-detection logic\n\n## Note on #2406\n\n#2406 was closed 2026-04-20 as fixed against codex-cli 0.121.0; the `</dev/null` fix it landed is still correct for that version but was **silently defeated** by the 0.124.0 upgrade on 2026-04-23. Per `feedback_gh_issue_close_silent_comment_drop`, I am not commenting on #2406 — this fresh issue supersedes it. Leave #2406 closed-as-superseded unless maintainers prefer to reopen.\n\n## Related\n- `.claude/memory/topics/feedback_codex_needs_pushed_artifact.md`\n- `.claude/memory/topics/feedback_codex_sandbox_write_blocked.md`\n- `.claude/memory/topics/feedback_codex_sandbox_no_execution.md`\n- `.claude/memory/topics/feedback_codex_sandbox_fallback_paths.md`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2479","display_title":"fix(review): Codex stdin-hang regression post-#2406 closure (size-dependent)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2272","title":"test(portability): add repeatable OpenFOAM and Blender smoke verification","issue_number":2272,"body":"## Summary\nAdd repeatable portability verification for core engineering workflows so the ecosystem can continuously check whether the OpenFOAM and Blender baselines are still reproducible.\n\nPart of #1782.\nDepends on the baseline workflow issues for OpenFOAM and Blender.\n\n## Motivation\nPortability should be measurable. Once baseline workflows exist, the ecosystem needs a repeatable verification layer that surfaces drift early.\n\n## Scope\nAdd smoke verification commands and lightweight health checks for the canonical OpenFOAM and Blender baseline workflows.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] one OpenFOAM smoke verification path exists and is documented\n- [ ] one Blender smoke verification path exists and is documented\n- [ ] verification output is machine-readable and human-readable\n- [ ] docs explain expected outputs, pass/fail semantics, and common failure categories\n- [ ] verification can be re-run after environment/tooling changes to detect drift\n\n## Notes\nPrefer layered validation:\n- structure first\n- semantic sanity second\n- deeper engineering quality checks later\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2272","display_title":"test(portability): add repeatable OpenFOAM and Blender smoke verification"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2270","title":"feat(blender): standardize headless baseline workflow and smoke render validation","issue_number":2270,"body":"## Summary\nStandardize the canonical Blender baseline for portable engineering work, using headless bpy scripts, example scenes, output conventions, and validation.\n\nPart of #1782.\nPrimary reference: #26.\nTooling history/reference: #1475.\n\n## Motivation\nBlender work is useful for engineering visualization and animation, but it becomes non-portable when knowledge lives only in manually edited `.blend` files or machine-specific GUI habits. We need a script-first headless baseline.\n\n## Scope\nCreate the baseline Blender portability package in workspace-hub.\n\nExpected deliverables:\n- baseline docs\n- headless render/script entrypoints\n- minimal reproducible scene/render example\n- troubleshooting guidance\n- shared skill update if reusable guidance is improved\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] baseline doc declares headless `blender -b --python ...` as the canonical path\n- [ ] minimal reproducible scene/render workflow exists at a repo-tracked path\n- [ ] output naming and artifact conventions are documented\n- [ ] validator checks execution success and expected outputs\n- [ ] common failure modes are documented\n- [ ] optional CLI-Anything tooling is documented as convenience-only, not required baseline\n\n## Notes\nImplementation should prefer:\n- procedural scene generation where practical\n- validation of output structure/metadata first, not pixel-perfect diffs\n- explicit output directories and deterministic command paths\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2270","display_title":"feat(blender): standardize headless baseline workflow and smoke render validation"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2532","title":"fix(ci): repair PR review/stage-prompt guard environment failures","issue_number":2532,"body":"## Problem\nTwo independent workspace-hub PRs created during post-reboot salvage (#2530 for vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2408 and vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2531 for vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2424) both hit the same CI infrastructure failures unrelated to their branch diffs:\n\n1. `Stage Prompt Drift Guard` fails with:\n   - `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'workspace_hub'`\n   - path: `scripts/analysis/stage_prompt_drift_check.py` imports `claude_session_ecosystem_audit`, which imports `workspace_hub.workstations.resolver`\n2. `Review Evidence Check` fails before checking review evidence with:\n   - `scripts/enforcement/require-review-on-push.sh: line 19: uv: command not found`\n\n## Evidence\n- PR vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2530: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/pull/2530\n  - Stage Prompt Drift Guard job: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/actions/runs/25024304678/job/73291841751\n  - Review Evidence Check job: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/actions/runs/25024304678/job/73291841710\n- PR vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2531: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/pull/2531\n  - Stage Prompt Drift Guard job: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/actions/runs/25024305694/job/73291844714\n  - Review Evidence Check job: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/actions/runs/25024305694/job/73291844723\n\nBoth PRs otherwise had local targeted validation and GitHub Run Tests / Code Quality green at the time of observation.\n\n## Scope suggestion\nPlan a narrow harness/CI repair, not a broad workflow rewrite:\n- Ensure the stage prompt drift job can import `workspace_hub` in GitHub Actions (package layout/PYTHONPATH/install-mode decision).\n- Ensure review-evidence check installs or exposes `uv` before invoking `scripts/enforcement/require-review-on-push.sh`, or make the script fail with a deterministic setup message.\n- Add a focused regression/smoke that catches both failure modes.\n\n## Governance\nThis issue is planning-required. Do not implement until a plan is reviewed and user-approved.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2532","display_title":"fix(ci): repair PR review/stage-prompt guard environment failures"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/373","title":"epic: Comprehensive Lower Tertiary Fields Report (10 GoM fields)","issue_number":373,"body":"## Goal\n\nProduce a buyer-facing comprehensive report covering all 10 Gulf of Mexico Lower Tertiary fields, replacing the existing partial summary at `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md` (which only fully treats the 6 producing fields).\n\n## The 10 fields\n\n**Producing (6):**\n| Field | System | Water Depth | Cum Oil (MMBBL) | Operator |\n|-------|--------|-------------|-----------------|----------|\n| Jack/St. Malo | Subsea 15K | 7,240 ft | 412.46 | Chevron |\n| Stones | Subsea 15K | 9,525 ft | 84.68 | Shell |\n| Julia | Tieback 15K | 7,335 ft | 71.56 | ExxonMobil |\n| Big Foot | Dry Tree | 5,190 ft | 68.96 | Chevron |\n| Cascade/Chinook | Subsea 15K | 8,200 ft | 37.79 | Petrobras |\n| Anchor | Subsea 20K | 5,080 ft | 9.51 | Chevron |\n\n**Under Development / Pre-FID (4):**\n| Field | System | Water Depth | Status |\n|-------|--------|-------------|--------|\n| Shenandoah | Subsea 20K | 5,800 ft | Under Development |\n| Kaskida | Subsea 20K | 5,860 ft | Pre-FID |\n| Tiber | Subsea 20K | 4,130 ft | Pre-FID |\n| North Platte | Subsea 20K | 5,840 ft | Pre-FID |\n\n## Phases (one sub-issue each — to be linked after creation)\n\n1. **Field config completeness** — backfill missing yamls (Big Foot, North Platte); ensure all 10 have current configs\n2. **Per-field economic analysis** — apply the lease→NPV pattern (#372) to all 10 fields with current assumptions\n3. **Cross-field analytics** — technology generation, operator concentration, HSE overlay, cost benchmarking via #338 surface\n4. **Report assembly** — HTML + PDF + executive summary + citations panel; supersedes current `lower_tertiary_field_summary.md`\n\n## Plan\n\nPlan landing under `docs/plans/` per the project's planning workflow (see `CLAUDE.md`). Each phase gets its own issue + plan, all at `status:plan-review` for user approval before implementation.\n\n## Why now\n\n- Lease-NPV demo (#372) just landed — the per-field NPV pattern is proven\n- Disclosure analytics surface (#338) shipped — cost benchmarking is wired\n- Catalog wiring (#371) lets bulk BSEE production data resolve through DataResolver\n- Citations pattern (#372 demo, #361 follow-up) gives a defensibility template\n\nThe dependencies for a comprehensive report are now in place; this epic coordinates the work of producing it.\n\n## Acceptance for closure\n\n- [ ] All 4 sub-issues closed with their plans approved + implemented\n- [ ] `reports/lower_tertiary/comprehensive_2026.md` (or .html) lands as the canonical buyer artifact\n- [ ] Each of the 10 fields has its own data + economics + cross-cutting analytics fully traced\n- [ ] Citations panel sources every numeric input\n\n## Refs\n- Source-of-truth doc: `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md`\n- Field configs: `config/analysis/lower_tertiary/fields/`\n- Module: `src/worldenergydata/lower_tertiary/`\n- Demo precedent: #372 (lease-NPV walkthrough)\n- Cost surface: #338 (disclosure analytics)\n- Catalog routing: #371 (partial-symlink wiring)","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/373","display_title":"epic: Comprehensive Lower Tertiary Fields Report (10 GoM fields)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/377","title":"feat(lt): comprehensive report assembly — HTML + PDF + executive summary + citations (Phase 4 of #373)","issue_number":377,"body":"## Phase 4 of #373 — Lower Tertiary Fields Report\n\n## Goal\n\nFinal assembly. Take outputs from Phases 1–3 and produce a single buyer-facing comprehensive artifact. Supersedes `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md` (which currently treats only the 6 producing fields).\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- [ ] `reports/lower_tertiary/comprehensive_2026.md` — canonical markdown report (full 10 fields)\n- [ ] `reports/lower_tertiary/comprehensive_2026.html` — styled HTML for sharing\n- [ ] `reports/lower_tertiary/comprehensive_2026.pdf` — via Chrome headless / md-to-pdf skill\n- [ ] Executive summary (one-page) with portfolio totals, key insights, technology generation breakdown\n- [ ] Per-field detail sections (one per field × 10) with economics + production history + HSE\n- [ ] Citations panel embedded — every numeric input traced to publisher + code_id + revision (per #361 pattern)\n- [ ] Updated `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md` either replaced or marked superseded\n\n## Format reference\n\nUse the existing `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md` structure as a starting point but expanded to all 10 fields and structured with the section taxonomy from #373 epic (production, technology, operator, HSE, cost). Citations follow the #372 walkthrough pattern.\n\n## Plan\n\nPlan to be drafted at `docs/plans/2026-05-03-issue-377-lt-comprehensive-report-assembly.md`.\n\n## Dependencies\n- #374 (Phase 1: configs) — must be present\n- #375 (Phase 2: per-field economics) — feeds the per-field sections\n- #376 (Phase 3: cross-field analytics) — feeds the portfolio sections\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] All 10 fields covered with consistent depth\n- [ ] HTML renders cleanly without external dependencies\n- [ ] PDF generated via repo-blessed tooling\n- [ ] Citations panel covers every number — failed citations resolution = build fail\n- [ ] Sign-off review against the epic's acceptance criteria (#373)\n\n## Refs\n- Epic: #373\n- Existing report: `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.md`\n- HTML template: `reports/lower_tertiary_field_summary.html`\n- Demo pattern: #372 (citations panel)","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/377","display_title":"feat(lt): comprehensive report assembly — HTML + PDF + executive summary + citations (Phase 4 of #373)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/376","title":"feat(lt): cross-field analytics — technology, operator, HSE, cost benchmarking (Phase 3 of #373)","issue_number":376,"body":"## Phase 3 of #373 — Lower Tertiary Fields Report\n\n## Goal\n\nCross-field analytics that surface portfolio-level insights a per-field analysis cannot show on its own.\n\n## Sections\n\n### 3a. Technology generation comparison\n- Subsea 15K vs. Subsea 20K vs. Tieback vs. Dry Tree\n- Capex intensity ($M / MMbbl recoverable)\n- Time-to-first-oil\n- Recovery factor estimates\n- Output: comparison table + chart\n\n### 3b. Operator + partnership concentration\n- Working-interest-weighted production by operator\n- JV structure per field (from yaml `partners`)\n- Operator concentration metrics (HHI, top-3 share)\n- Output: operator portfolio matrix\n\n### 3c. HSE incident overlay\n- BSEE incident records per lease/field (uses HSE module + BSEE incidents data per #366)\n- Incidents per MMbbl produced\n- Severity distribution\n- Output: HSE performance per field\n- **Caveat:** depends on HSE bulk dedup (#366) for full incident detail; minimum-viable version uses BSEE current data only\n\n### 3d. Cost benchmarking via disclosure analytics\n- Modelled capex (from yamls) vs. operator-disclosed annual capex\n- Uses `DisclosureAnalyticsQuery.benchmark` (shipped #338)\n- Per-field cost-disclosure deltas where comparable\n- **Caveat:** requires #343 operator registry to have data for relevant operators; otherwise illustrative only\n\n## Plan\n\nPlan to be drafted at `docs/plans/2026-05-03-issue-376-cross-field-analytics-lt.md`.\n\n## Dependencies\n- #375 (Phase 2) — per-field economics provides the baseline to compare against\n\n## Refs\n- Epic: #373\n- HSE substrate: #366\n- Disclosure surface: #338\n- Operator registry (companion): #343","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/376","display_title":"feat(lt): cross-field analytics — technology, operator, HSE, cost benchmarking (Phase 3 of #373)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2622","title":"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster C — drop -p no:capture from QG command or refactor capsys tests (~17 errors)","issue_number":2622,"body":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). The Quality Gates command in \\`.claude/quality-gates.yaml:10\\` uses \\`-p no:capture\\` which removes the \\`capsys\\` fixture. Tests that declare \\`capsys\\` parameter then fail collection with \\`fixture 'capsys' not found\\`.\n\n## Defect\n\nDirect hit: \\`tests/hydrodynamics/passing_ship/test_passing_ship_cli.py\\` (2 errors).\n\nLikely blast radius: across the suite, **16 tests use \\`capsys\\`** in 6 files. Several already surface as ERRORs in the maxfail=200 run with the same fixture-not-found pattern:\n\n- \\`tests/hydrodynamics/passing_ship/test_passing_ship_cli.py\\` (2 errors confirmed)\n- \\`tests/solvers/orcaflex/test_template_generator.py::TestCLI\\` (5 errors — likely same root cause; verify)\n- \\`tests/solvers/orcaflex/modular_generator/test_campaign_generator.py::TestCLICampaign*\\` (6 errors — likely same root cause; verify)\n- \\`tests/test_propeller_rudder_interaction.py\\`, \\`tests/structural/fatigue_analysis/test_fatigue_cli.py\\`, \\`tests/infrastructure/common/test_data.py\\` (likely also affected)\n\n\\`caplog\\` is unaffected because it comes from \\`_pytest.logging\\`, not \\`_pytest.capture\\`.\n\n## Fix options\n\n- **(a) Drop \\`-p no:capture\\` from \\`.claude/quality-gates.yaml\\` line 10** — cheapest; verify it wasn't added for a perf reason. Single-line change.\n- **(b) Refactor the capsys tests** — use \\`monkeypatch\\` against \\`sys.stdout\\`, or use \\`subprocess.run\\` for actual CLI behavior. Larger surface.\n\nRecommend (a) unless there's a documented reason for the \\`-p no:capture\\` flag.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] \\`-p no:capture\\` removed (or capsys tests refactored to not depend on it)\n- [ ] Run repo-wide pytest with QG command: 0 \\`fixture 'capsys' not found\\` errors\n- [ ] Verify the 5 \\`test_template_generator.py\\` errors and 6 \\`test_campaign_generator.py\\` errors clear (or remain — separate root cause)\n- [ ] No previously-passing tests regress\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614), [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2622","display_title":"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster C — drop -p no:capture from QG command or refactor capsys tests (~17 errors)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2621","title":"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster B — extend _HAS_WED_DCF skipif to 4 sibling test classes (14 failures)","issue_number":2621,"body":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616) maxfail-cap investigation. PR [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543) added \\`_HAS_WED_DCF\\` probe and \\`@pytest.mark.skipif\\` only to \\`TestBuildEconomicsSchedule\\` (line 486 of \\`tests/field_development/test_economics.py\\`). Four sibling classes also depend on \\`worldenergydata.economics.*\\` at runtime but were never decorated.\n\n## Defect\n\nIn \\`tests/field_development/test_economics.py\\`, four classes share the same \\`worldenergydata\\` import paths but lack the skipif:\n\n| Class | Line | Failures | Import path it depends on |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \\`TestCarbonSensitivity\\` | 577 | 5 | \\`worldenergydata.economics.carbon\\` (via \\`carbon_sensitivity()\\` at \\`economics.py:989\\`) |\n| \\`TestDeclineBackwardCompatibility\\` | 829 | 1 | \\`worldenergydata.economics.dcf.CashFlowSchedule\\` (via \\`build_economics_schedule()\\` at \\`economics.py:886\\`) |\n| \\`TestScheduleDeclineIntegration\\` | 860 | 2 | same as above |\n| \\`TestFiscalRegimeTaxAdjustment\\` | 1006 | 6 | \\`worldenergydata.{eia_us,ukcs,brazil_anp,west_africa,sodir}\\` (via \\`_apply_fiscal_regime\\`/\\`_resolve_fiscal_calculator\\` at \\`economics.py:625-675\\`) |\n\nTotal: **14 failures** + per-class breakdowns above.\n\n## Nuance\n\nOf the 18 test methods in those four classes, 4 pass (those that don't actually exercise the import path — early returns / mocked passthrough / structural checks). So a per-class skipif would over-skip; per-method or per-import-block guarding may be more careful. Quickest correct fix: per-class skipif with the same probe.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] All 14 \\`ModuleNotFoundError: worldenergydata\\` failures in \\`tests/field_development/test_economics.py\\` either pass or skip cleanly (no errors)\n- [ ] No previously-passing tests regress\n- [ ] Skipif decorator pattern matches the existing line-486 \\`TestBuildEconomicsSchedule\\` precedent\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614), [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616) — parent investigations\n- [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543) — the original incomplete fix","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2621","display_title":"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster B — extend _HAS_WED_DCF skipif to 4 sibling test classes (14 failures)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2612","title":"feat(llm-wiki): lng-projects wiki topical expansion — 8 concept/entity pages (W5-C)","issue_number":2612,"body":"## Summary\n\nTopical expansion of the **lng-projects LLM-wiki** at `knowledge/wikis/lng-projects/`. Currently 7 .md files. Plan proposes 8 concept/entity pages disjoint from in-flight vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#14 SESA / vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2544 Woodfibre source-page work.\n\n## Scope\n- **Target:** `knowledge/wikis/lng-projects/wiki/concepts/` + `wiki/entities/`\n- **Topics:** project lifecycle phases, liquefaction technologies (APC/DMR/cascade/MR/MFC), full-containment LNG tanks, FLNG vs FSRU vs onshore terminal, BOG management, vapor cloud explosion process safety, regulatory anchors (NFPA 59A, EN 1473, IGC code), project shapes\n- **Path-sanction:** lng-projects is NOT in the {marine-engineering, engineering, naval-architecture} principle scope per memory — plan stays in `wiki/concepts/` + `wiki/entities/`. Standards-page routing flagged as Open Question for separate sanction (see W5-D companion plan)\n\n## Notable\n- No `knowledge/seeds/lng-projects-resources.yaml` exists (only `mooring-failures-lng-terminals.yaml`) — `index.md` hand-edited, flagged as risk\n- Reservation regex covers SESA / Woodfibre / ACMA-31522 / Doris-62092 noun-phrases to prevent overlap with source-page work\n\n## Workflow\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 5-C, 2026-05-03; allowlist test passes)\n- [ ] Adversarial review\n- [ ] User approval\n- [ ] Implementation\n\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review`.**\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2612","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): lng-projects wiki topical expansion — 8 concept/entity pages (W5-C)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2602","title":"feat(llm-wiki): engineering wiki pipeline sub-domain topical expansion (W4-D)","issue_number":2602,"body":"## Summary\n\n**Topical expansion of engineering wiki — PIPELINE sub-domain.** Currently 2 pipeline-related wiki files. 10 high-value concept pages backfilled from canonical pipeline engineering literature + /mnt/ace raw (Saipem). Same shape as W3-D ([#2597](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2597) — engineering riser sub-domain), post-W3-C amendment.\n\n## Scope\n- **Target:** `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/concepts/pipeline-<topic>.md`\n- **10 topics:** route selection, on-bottom stability, lateral buckling, upheaval buckling, free-span analysis, trawl-impact protection, pipe-soil interaction, installation methods (S-lay/J-lay/reel-lay/tow-out), pipeline coatings, pipeline-walking + end-expansion/spool design\n- **Path-sanction:** engineering wiki's own `CLAUDE.md` schema (NOT #2471 — engineering wiki IS in routing-principle scope per memory, but #2471 itself is CSA-Z276-only)\n- **Boundary discipline:** TDD regex tests prevent scope-creep into riser/mooring/umbilical sub-domains\n\n## Lesson banked from W3-C\nW4-D adds `test_no_2471_path_sanction_citation` — a per-plan self-test complementing the governance allowlist (`tests/governance/test_2471_citation_scope.py`).\n\n## Workflow\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 4-D, 2026-05-03; allowlist test passes)\n- [ ] Adversarial review\n- [ ] User approval\n- [ ] Implementation\n\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review`.**\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2602","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): engineering wiki pipeline sub-domain topical expansion (W4-D)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2597","title":"feat(llm-wiki): engineering wiki riser sub-domain topical expansion (W3-D)","issue_number":2597,"body":"## Summary\n\n**Topical expansion of the engineering wiki — RISER sub-domain.** ~8-10 high-value concept pages backfilled from canonical riser engineering literature + /mnt/ace raw + W3-B's ISO 19901-7 cross-links. Scope is riser-specific only (NOT pipeline / mooring / structural — separate sub-domains).\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Target:** `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/concepts/riser-<topic>.md`\n- **Topics:** SCR (steel catenary riser), TTR (top-tensioned riser), flexible riser, hybrid riser, drilling riser, riser configurations, VIV, wave-fatigue, drift-current loading, riser-soil interaction\n- **Path-sanction:** engineering wiki's own `CLAUDE.md` schema (engineering wiki IS in the routing-principle scope)\n- **Boundary discipline:** scope-creep into pipeline / mooring / umbilical sub-domains is enforced via TDD regex tests\n\n## Workflow status\n\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 3-D, 2026-05-02)\n- [ ] Adversarial review (Claude internal)\n- [ ] User approval\n- [ ] Implementation\n\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review` — never self-approved.**\n\n## Related\n\n- Same shape as [#2589](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2589) (W1-D naval-architecture topical expansion)\n- Cross-links to W3-B (ISO 19901-7) once that page exists\n- Independent of [#2588](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2588) (W1-C audit) — re-sequenceable\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2597","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): engineering wiki riser sub-domain topical expansion (W3-D)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2592","title":"feat(llm-wiki): maritime-law wiki topical expansion — IMO conventions + 10 core concepts (W2-C)","issue_number":2592,"body":"## Summary\n\nTopical expansion of the **maritime-law LLM-wiki** at `knowledge/wikis/maritime-law/wiki/`. Currently 22 wiki files. The plan proposes 10 high-value pages: 6 legal-concept pages (general-average, salvage, limitation-of-liability, port-state-control, flag-state-jurisdiction, charterparties) + 4 IMO/ILO standards pages (MARPOL-73-78, SOLAS-1974, MLC-2006, ISM-Code). The 4 standards pages would create the **first `wiki/standards/` directory** in the maritime-law wiki.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Target wiki:** `knowledge/wikis/maritime-law/wiki/concepts/` (6 pages) + `wiki/standards/<code-id>.md` (4 pages)\n- **Sources:** Aleka Mandaraka-Sheppard, Schoenbaum, IMO publication catalog\n- **Boundary:** commercial shipping / IMO regulatory regime / admiralty — NOT corporate or environmental law more broadly\n- **Path routing for IMO conventions:** **OPEN QUESTION** — per memory `project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md`, #2471 sanction is CSA-Z276-only, so IMO standards routing is NOT formally sanctioned. Plan flags this as a load-bearing decision for user approval.\n\n## Workflow status\n\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 2-C, 2026-05-02)\n- [ ] Adversarial review (Claude internal)\n- [ ] User approval (`status:plan-approved`)\n- [ ] Implementation (TDD)\n\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review` — never self-approved.**\n\n## Related\n\n- Same-wave shape: [#2589](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2589) (W1-D naval-architecture topical expansion)\n- Parent context: [#2540](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2540) (Elements wave)\n- Sanctions: #2471 path routing (scope-limited)\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2592","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): maritime-law wiki topical expansion — IMO conventions + 10 core concepts (W2-C)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2589","title":"feat(llm-wiki): naval-architecture wiki topical expansion — 10 core concept pages (W1-D)","issue_number":2589,"body":"## Summary\n\nTopical expansion of the **naval-architecture LLM-wiki** at `knowledge/wikis/naval-architecture/wiki/`. Currently 62 wiki files. The plan proposes 10 high-value foundational concept pages backfilled from canonical naval-architecture curriculum (PNA SNAME volumes, Tupper Introduction, ITTC procedures), with explicit non-overlap with in-flight `status:plan-approved` issues #2566 (yaw / rudder-stock) and #2568 (turning-circle / tactical-diameter).\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Target wiki:** `knowledge/wikis/naval-architecture/wiki/concepts/`\n- **Reserved (excluded):** topics covered by #2566 and #2568\n- **Approach:** concept summaries + cross-links + frontmatter, no textbook copy (per #2482)\n- **Standards backbone:** IMO regulatory framework, ITTC procedures, IACS / classification societies (DNV/ABS/LR/BV/CCS)\n\n## Workflow status\n\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 1-D, 2026-05-02)\n- [ ] Adversarial review (Gemini + Claude-internal — in flight)\n- [ ] User approval (`status:plan-approved`)\n- [ ] Implementation (TDD per template)\n\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review` — never self-approved.**\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent context: #2540 (Elements wave) — non-overlapping\n- Reserved phrases excluded: #2566 (yaw / rudder-stock), #2568 (turning-circle / tactical-diameter)\n- Sanctions: #2471 (path routing), #2482 (vendor-derivative deny-list)\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2589","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): naval-architecture wiki topical expansion — 10 core concept pages (W1-D)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2587","title":"feat(llm-wiki): asset-management wiki topical scaffold + scope boundary (W1-B)","issue_number":2587,"body":"## Summary\n\nTopical scaffold and scope-boundary fix for the **asset-management LLM-wiki** at `knowledge/wikis/asset-management/wiki/`. Currently 4 hand-touched files plus 1 source page (financial-portfolio scope). The plan proposes ~12 concept pages + standards pages (ISO 55000 family, IAM Competency Framework, API RP 580/581, DNV-RP-G101, NORSOK Z-008, HSE Safety Case Regs) plus a load-bearing scope-boundary disclaimer separating engineering asset-integrity from financial-portfolio asset-management.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Target wiki:** `knowledge/wikis/asset-management/wiki/`\n- **Boundary:** engineering integrity-management domain only (NOT financial assethold/portfolio)\n- **Routing:** concepts under `wiki/concepts/`, standards under `wiki/standards/<code-id>.md` (#2471)\n- **Deny-list:** no vendor-derivative copy per vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2482\n\n## Workflow status\n\n- [x] Plan drafted (Wave 1-B, 2026-05-02)\n- [ ] Adversarial review (Gemini + Claude-internal — in flight)\n- [ ] User approval (`status:plan-approved`)\n- [ ] Implementation (TDD per template)\n\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review` — never self-approved.**\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent context: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2540 (Elements wave) — Casa-Grande financial corpus is OUT OF SCOPE here\n- Sanctions: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2471 (path routing), vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2482 (vendor-derivative deny-list)\n- Sibling planning waves: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#14 SESA LNG, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2542 Doris University, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2543 Doris codes, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2544 Woodfibre LNG, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2559 OCIMF Tandem\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2587","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): asset-management wiki topical scaffold + scope boundary (W1-B)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2615","title":"meta(governance): umbrella sanction — wiki/standards/ routing for engineering-standards / asset-management / lng-projects / acma-projects (W5-D follow-up)","issue_number":2615,"body":"## Summary\n\n**Umbrella sanction issue** spawned from W5-D ([#2613](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2613)) per user approval 2026-05-03. Formally codifies `wiki/standards/<code-id>.md` routing for the wikis that the W3-C erratum (#2596) re-anchored to local `CLAUDE.md` schema but that the existing memory `project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md` does not yet enumerate.\n\n## Per-wiki decision matrix (executed under this issue)\n\n| Wiki | Action | Detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| `engineering-standards` | **Codify** | Append `Sanctioned-by: #<this-issue>` reference in `knowledge/wikis/engineering-standards/CLAUDE.md` (W3-C re-anchor for W1-A #2586) |\n| `asset-management` | **Codify** | Append `Sanctioned-by: #<this-issue>` reference in `knowledge/wikis/asset-management/CLAUDE.md` (W3-C re-anchor for W1-B #2587) |\n| `lng-projects` | **User decision** | Auto-generated CLAUDE.md schema; conditional codification pending user assent during this umbrella's review |\n| `acma-projects` | **User decision** | Same as lng-projects |\n| `marine-engineering` | **Affirm-with-defer** | In-principle per memory; defer canonical-home to aces-#4 Phase 1; structural fix via #2601 (W4-C audit) |\n| `engineering` | **Affirm** | Already in-principle |\n| `naval-architecture` | **Affirm** | Already in-principle |\n| `maritime-law` | **Reaffirm out-of-scope** | Memory verbatim; no CLAUDE.md change |\n| `personal` | **Reaffirm out-of-scope** | Memory verbatim; no CLAUDE.md change |\n| `health-reports` | **Reaffirm out-of-scope** | Memory verbatim; no CLAUDE.md change |\n\n## Deliverables\n\nWhen this umbrella issue itself is approved + implemented:\n\n1. **CLAUDE.md edits** for `engineering-standards/`, `asset-management/`, plus conditionally for `lng-projects/` and `acma-projects/` (pending user assent)\n2. **Governance test extension** at `tests/governance/test_2471_citation_scope.py` — add `test_out_of_principle_wiki_routing_requires_sanction_citation` enforcing that any future plan citing `wiki/standards/<code-id>.md` routing for an out-of-principle wiki must include a sanction-issue citation\n3. **Memory update** at `~/.claude/projects/-mnt-local-analysis-workspace-hub/memory/project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md` — enumerate the formally-sanctioned wikis with this issue's number\n4. **README index update** at `docs/plans/README.md`\n\n## Workflow\n\n- [x] Spawned by approved W5-D plan #2613 (user approval 2026-05-03 Tier A)\n- [ ] Adversarial review (this umbrella's own review cycle)\n- [ ] User approval (`status:plan-approved`)\n- [ ] Implementation (CLAUDE.md edits + test + memory + README)\n- [ ] Close\n\n**Workflow stops at `status:plan-review` until you re-approve this umbrella explicitly. The W5-D approval authorized this umbrella's CREATION, not its IMPLEMENTATION** — that's an additional gate per `feedback_never_offer_to_self_label_plan_approved.md`.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent plan: [#2613](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2613) (W5-D)\n- Predecessor erratum: [#2596](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2596) (W3-C — closed)\n- W3-C re-anchored plans: [#2586](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2586) (W1-A), [#2587](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2587) (W1-B), [#2592](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2592) (W2-C), [#2597](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2597) (W3-D)\n- Marine-engineering structural fix: [#2601](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2601) (W4-C — orthogonal; this umbrella only cross-references)\n- Cross-repo: aceengineer-strategy aces-#4 (canonical-home decision)\n- Memory: `project_wiki_standards_path_decision.md`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2615","display_title":"meta(governance): umbrella sanction — wiki/standards/ routing for engineering-standards / asset-management / lng-projects / acma-projects (W5-D follow-up)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616","title":"fix(digitalmodel-tests): identify the 4 non-hydrodynamics CI failures masked by maxfail=20","issue_number":2616,"body":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614) hydrodynamics-debt triage.\n\nThe original CI run https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/actions/runs/25270693237 reported \\`20 failed, 3023 passed\\` with \\`maxfail=20\\` (set by [digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543)).\n\nThe hydrodynamics triage in \\`/tmp/hydrodynamics-debt-triage.md\\` enumerated 16 failures in \\`tests/hydrodynamics/diffraction/\\`. **The remaining 4 must live elsewhere in \\`tests/\\`** but were never identified because \\`maxfail=20\\` stopped the run as soon as the 20-failure cap was hit.\n\nAfter [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614)'s 4 categories land, those 16 should clear; the remaining 4 will then be the only failures and easy to identify.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] After [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614)'s Cats A-D land, run repo-wide \\`pytest --maxfail=20 ...\\` (or higher cap) on a clean digitalmodel CI environment\n- [ ] Enumerate the remaining failures and their file paths\n- [ ] File a per-category followup (or one consolidated tracker) for each\n- [ ] Consider whether the \\`maxfail=20\\` cap from [digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543) should be relaxed once the queue is clean\n\n## Why now\n\nUntil these 4 are identified and addressed, every digitalmodel PR will continue to admin-merge through Quality Gates (the precedent established by PRs [#541](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/541), [#544](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/544), [#549](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/549), [#550](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/550), [#551](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/551)). The admin-override flow is unsustainable as a default merge path.\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614) — hydrodynamics debt tracker (Cats A-D)\n- [#2129](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2129) — issue-state drift / quality-gates audit umbrella\n- [#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580) — original Quality Gates unblock that triggered the cascade","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616","display_title":"fix(digitalmodel-tests): identify the 4 non-hydrodynamics CI failures masked by maxfail=20"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/375","title":"feat(lt): per-field economic analysis for all 10 fields — NPV/IRR/payback + sensitivities (Phase 2 of #373)","issue_number":375,"body":"## Phase 2 of #373 — Lower Tertiary Fields Report\n\n## Goal\n\nApply the lease→NPV demo pattern (#372) at field level to all 10 LT fields. Produce a comparable per-field economics table.\n\n## Required outputs\n\nFor each of the 10 fields:\n- NPV @ 10% (annual, $M)\n- IRR (annual)\n- MIRR (annual, with reinvestment rate)\n- Payback period (years)\n- Breakeven oil price\n- 5-point sensitivity table ($50 / $60 / $70 / $80 / $100 per bbl)\n- Citations panel sourcing every input\n\nOutput formats:\n- `results/lower_tertiary/per_field_economics_2026.csv` (machine-readable)\n- `results/lower_tertiary/per_field_economics_2026.html` (buyer-presentable)\n\n## Approach\n\nReuse `EconomicsQuery.all_metrics` and `AssumptionsManager` per the #372 walkthrough. Inputs come from `config/analysis/lower_tertiary/fields/*.yml` + `config/analysis/lower_tertiary/economic_assumptions.yml` + WTI series via `wti_prices.load_wti_series`.\n\nFor producing fields: cashflow grounded in BSEE OGOR aggregation where available (otherwise documented decline-curve fallback).\nFor pre-FID fields: documented assumptions only with explicit \"preliminary\" tag in the citations panel.\n\n## Plan\n\nPlan to be drafted at `docs/plans/2026-05-03-issue-375-per-field-economics-10-lt-fields.md`.\n\n## Dependencies\n- #374 (config backfill — must ship first to give Big Foot + North Platte yamls)\n\n## Refs\n- Epic: #373\n- Pattern source: #372 (lease-NPV walkthrough)\n- Module: `src/worldenergydata/lower_tertiary/npv.py`\n- Module: `src/worldenergydata/lower_tertiary/wti_prices.py`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/issues/375","display_title":"feat(lt): per-field economic analysis for all 10 fields — NPV/IRR/payback + sensitivities (Phase 2 of #373)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2606","title":"fix(digitalmodel): uv sync hangs at finalize step on Python 3.11 venv","issue_number":2606,"body":"**Source:** [#2566](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2566) validation report (observed on ace-linux-1, 2026-05-02)\n\n## Problem\n\n`cd digitalmodel && uv sync` substantively completes (venv populated, all packages installed, log shows `Uninstalled 44 packages in 4.49s` and then nothing) but the parent process never exits. Observed PID staying alive >18s (and >60s on retry) after the venv is fully usable.\n\n## Workaround\n\nKill the PID manually after verifying `digitalmodel/.venv/bin/` has the expected entries (alembic, aqwa, assetutils-devtools, etc.).\n\n## Hypothesis\n\nPossibly related to the Python 3.11 venv finalize step (vs. workspace's Python 3.13 miniforge), or a compiled-extension build hook that doesn't exit.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] `uv sync` exits cleanly within reasonable time after install completes\n- [ ] OR documented workaround / known limitation in `digitalmodel/CONTRIBUTING.md` or `README.md`\n\n## Severity\n\nMedium — degrades CI ergonomics; survivable with manual intervention.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2606","display_title":"fix(digitalmodel): uv sync hangs at finalize step on Python 3.11 venv"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2607","title":"chore(digitalmodel): document lxml build dependency for clean-venv smoke installs","issue_number":2607,"body":"**Source:** [#2566](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2566) validation report\n\n## Problem\n\n`pip install dist/digitalmodel-0.1.1-py3-none-any.whl` in a clean Python 3.13 venv on Ubuntu 25.10 fails with:\n\n```\nERROR: Failed to build 'lxml' when getting requirements to build wheel\n```\n\n`lxml` requires native compilation (`libxml2-dev`, `libxslt1-dev`); clean venvs without system dev packages cannot install it.\n\n## Acceptance options\n\nPick one:\n- [ ] Document `libxml2-dev` + `libxslt1-dev` system requirement in `digitalmodel/README.md` install section\n- [ ] OR pin a pre-built `lxml` wheel in `pyproject.toml` constraints\n- [ ] OR replace lxml usage with stdlib `xml.etree`/`html.parser` if scope is small\n\n## Severity\n\nLow — affects smoke-install docs and CI bootstrapping; `uv sync` works fine.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2607","display_title":"chore(digitalmodel): document lxml build dependency for clean-venv smoke installs"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2617","title":"fix(digitalmodel): test_packaged_yaml_in_built_distribution_preserves_existing_package_data flaky under parallel pytest sessions","issue_number":2617,"body":"**Discovered during:** #2603 implementation session on 2026-05-03; cross-ref [digitalmodel#548](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/548) post-merge verification.\n\n## Problem\n\n`tests/naval_architecture/test_rudder_stock_torque_sweep.py::test_packaged_yaml_in_built_distribution_preserves_existing_package_data` fails intermittently when run alongside other pytest sessions on the same host:\n\n```\nE     error: [Errno 2] No such file or directory\nE     ERROR Backend subprocess exited when trying to invoke build_wheel\n```\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe test runs `uv run --no-sync --with build --with setuptools --with wheel python -m build --wheel --no-isolation --outdir <tmp_path>`. With `--no-isolation`, the build subprocess interacts with the project's tmp/build state. When two pytest sessions run concurrently against the same workspace (e.g., the `/whats-next` parallel verification pattern), the build subprocess can race against another session's tmp cleanup and hit ENOENT.\n\n## Verification\n\nReproduced 2026-05-03:\n- Run alongside `test_yaw_moment_sweep.py`: FAIL with ENOENT\n- Run in isolation immediately after: PASS in 33-110s\n- Re-run alongside other test, isolation-mode rerun: PASS\n\nThe flake is **not** caused by [digitalmodel#548](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/548) (#2603 fix). Verified by stashing the __init__.py diff: test still passed in isolation; flake was tmpdir-race-driven.\n\n## Fix options (pick one)\n\n- [ ] (a) Add `pytest.mark.filterwarnings`/`pytest_xdist_group` so this test runs in its own group, blocking parallel sibling tests\n- [ ] (b) Switch to `--isolation` (full PEP 517 build env per invocation) — slower but no shared state\n- [ ] (c) Add a retry decorator (`@pytest.mark.flaky(reruns=2)`) — accepts the flake as-is\n- [ ] (d) Use a per-test scratch dir with `tmp_path_factory` and ensure no shared parent tmpdir\n\nRecommended: (b) for correctness, (a) or (d) if (b) is too slow.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] Test passes 10/10 under parallel-session contention (target host: ace-linux-1)\n- [ ] No regression in solo-run timing > 50%\n\n## Severity\n\nLow — does not affect correctness of #2564/#2565/#2603 work; test is real and useful, just flaky under load.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2617","display_title":"fix(digitalmodel): test_packaged_yaml_in_built_distribution_preserves_existing_package_data flaky under parallel pytest sessions"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2605","title":"chore(digitalmodel): ruff cleanup for naval_architecture/test_vessel_fleet_adapter.py (13 F401)","issue_number":2605,"body":"**Source:** [#2566](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2566) validation report\n\n## Problem\n\n`ruff check src/digitalmodel/naval_architecture/ tests/naval_architecture/` reports **13 errors** all in `tests/naval_architecture/test_vessel_fleet_adapter.py` — F401 unused imports.\n\nExample:\n```\nF401 [*] `digitalmodel.naval_architecture.ship_data.get_ship` imported but unused\n   --> tests/naval_architecture/test_vessel_fleet_adapter.py:200:13\n```\n\nAll 13 are auto-fixable with `ruff check --fix`.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] `ruff check --fix tests/naval_architecture/test_vessel_fleet_adapter.py` resolves all 13 F401\n- [ ] No regressions in test functionality\n\n## Severity\n\nLow — auto-fixable cleanup.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2605","display_title":"chore(digitalmodel): ruff cleanup for naval_architecture/test_vessel_fleet_adapter.py (13 F401)"}]}},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
